{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 1 Organization and General Authority \u00a7 2-101. Department established. \u00a7 2-102. Commissioner. \u00a7 2-103. Deputies, assistants, etc. \u00a7 2-103.1. Suicide Prevention Coordinator. \u00a7 2-104. Commissioner's Charter powers. \u00a7 2-105. General duties. \u00a7 2-106. Rules and regulations. \u00a7 2-107. Right of entry.", "num": "1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|1", "title": "Subtitle 1 Organization and General Authority", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-101", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-101. Department established. There is a Department of Health, as established in Article VII, \u00a7 54 of the City Charter . (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-101", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|1|2-101", "title": "\u00a7 2-101. Department established.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-101"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-102", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-102. Commissioner. The head of the Department is the Commissioner of Health, who is appointed as provided in Article VII, \u00a7 55 of the City Charter . (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-102", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|1|2-102", "title": "\u00a7 2-102. Commissioner.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-102"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-103", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-103. Deputies, assistants, etc. (a) In general. The Commissioner may appoint deputies, assistants, professional employees, and other officers and employees as provided in the Ordinance of Estimates. (b) Identification. While on duty, all officers and employees of the Department must have and, on request, display official identification denoting their employment. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a73", "num": "2-103", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|1|2-103", "title": "\u00a7 2-103. Deputies, assistants, etc.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-103"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-103.1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-103.1. Suicide Prevention Coordinator. (a) In general. There is a Suicide Prevention Coordinator within the Health Department. (b) Scope. The Suicide Prevention Coordinator shall: (1) collaborate with system partners to support behavioral health system management; and (2) work to prevent suicides and mental health crises. (c) Duties. The Suicide Prevention Coordinator is authorized to: (1) reach out to community organizations, groups and agencies to inform them about available resources regarding suicide prevention; (2) coordinate and provide suicide prevention trainings and trainings regarding the relationship between trauma and suicide as needed; (3) carry out new public awareness prevention strategies; (4) distribute resource materials as appropriate; (5) generally collaborate with: (i) the State Behavioral Health Administration's Office of Suicide Prevention; (ii) the Baltimore Police Department; (iii) the Fire Department; (iv) local emergency departments; (v) Baltimore Crisis Response; (vi) the Baltimore City Public School System; (vii) academic partners; (viii) people with mental health conditions; and (ix) other local agencies and services; (6) collaborate with the Health Department's Child Fatality Review team and the Health Department's Maternal Mortality Review team to develop and implement prevention plans; (7) collaborate with emergency departments, pediatricians, primary care physicians, the Baltimore City Public School System, and colleges and universities to encourage screening for suicidality and to establish continuity of care for patients at risk of suicide; (8) develop and coordinate a data collection and analysis plan to determine trends regarding suicides and suicide attempts; (9) develop a plan of action to reduce suicides in Baltimore City; (10) provide consultations to mental health, school, juvenile, and social services professionals; (11) represent the Health Department in interagency meetings and committees; (12) support system accountability to ensure that services are responsive to the behavioral health needs of Baltimore City residents; (13) work with diverse populations in Baltimore City to actively decrease stigma associated with mental health; and (14) fulfill other related duties as assigned by the Health Commissioner. (d) Potential contractual position. The Health Commissioner may elect to contract with the local behavioral health authority to fill the Suicide Prevention Coordinator position. (Ord. 21-063.) Editor's Notes This section was enacted by Ord. 21-063, effective July 1, 2022.", "num": "2-103.1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|1|2-103.1", "title": "\u00a7 2-103.1. Suicide Prevention Coordinator.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-103.1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-104", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-104. Commissioner's Charter powers. Under Article VII, \u00a7 56 of the City Charter , the Commissioner has the general care of and responsibility for: (1) enforcing all laws for the preservation of the health of the inhabitants of the City; (2) the study and prevention of disease, epidemics, and nuisances affecting public health; and (3) establishing and implementing policies for: (i) treating and preventing physical and mental illnesses; and (ii) educating the public about environmental, physical, and mental health. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-104", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|1|2-104", "title": "\u00a7 2-104. Commissioner's Charter powers.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-104"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-105", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-105. General duties. In addition to any duties set forth elsewhere, the Commissioner has the following duties: (1) to observe and inspect areas in and near the City that might be health hazards; (2) to enforce all laws relating to health and the correction of health hazards; (3) in the absence of a law needed to correct a health hazard, to report and recommend to the Mayor any extraordinary action needed; (4) to investigate and report all cases of communicable diseases and take immediate action to stop their spread; (5) to remove and abate nuisances; and (6) to report to and advise the Mayor and other City authorities on all matters relating to the preservation of the health of the people. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a71(exc. last cl.)", "num": "2-105", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|1|2-105", "title": "\u00a7 2-105. General duties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-105"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-106", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-106. Rules and regulations. Subject to Title 4 {\"Administrative Procedure Act \u2013 Regulations\"} of the City General Provisions Article , the Commissioner may adopt and enforce rules and regulations to carry out this article . (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Editor's Notes By authority of Ordinance 20-431, Section 5, the Director of Legislative Reference, in consultation with the Law Department, has conformed this section to refer expressly to and reflect the requirements of the recently-enacted Administrative Procedure Act that, effective January 15, 2021, governs the proposal, adoption, and publication of administrative rules and regulations.", "num": "2-106", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|1|2-106", "title": "\u00a7 2-106. Rules and regulations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-106"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-107", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-107. Right of entry. (a) In general. The Commissioner may, at all reasonable times, enter any structure or premises within the City: (1) whenever the Commissioner has reason to believe that a health hazard, nuisance, or violation of this article exists in or is emanating from that structure or premises; (2) to inspect any property, facilities, equipment, operations, animals, records, or other things required or authorized by this article to be inspected; or (3) otherwise as necessary or appropriate to enforce this article . (b) Dwellings. (1) \"Dwelling\" defined. In this subsection, \"dwelling\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 5-301(b) of this article . (2) Governing procedures. The entry and inspection of a dwelling must be made in accordance with \u00a7 104.6 {\"Right of entry\"} of the Baltimore City Building Code . (3) Search warrant. A search warrant must be obtained in accordance with \u00a7 104.6 {\"Right of entry\"} of the Baltimore City Building Code , except as otherwise specified in that section. (Ord. 13-093; Ord. 02-475; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a726(e), inter alia, art. 11, \u00a7105, art. 19, \u00a79", "num": "2-107", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|1|2-107", "title": "\u00a7 2-107. Right of entry.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-107"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/2", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 2 Cooperation with Department Part I Physicians; Police \u00a7\u00a7 2-201 \u2013 2-202 Part II Prohibited Conduct \u00a7\u00a7 2-205 \u2013 2-207 Part III Penalties. \u00a7\u00a7 2-211 \u2013 2-213", "num": "2", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2", "title": "Subtitle 2 Cooperation with Department", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/2"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/2/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I Physicians; Police \u00a7 2-201. Physicians to report and assist. \u00a7 2-202. Police to execute orders. 2-203 to 2-204. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|I", "title": "Part I Physicians; Police", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/2/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-201", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-201. Physicians to report and assist. On notice from the Commissioner, any practicing physician in the City must provide information, advice, and assistance to the Commissioner in all matters that relate to the preservation of the health of the people and the prevention of communicable diseases. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a71(last cl.)", "num": "2-201", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|I|2-201", "title": "\u00a7 2-201. Physicians to report and assist.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-201"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-202", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-202. Police to execute orders. Baltimore City Police Officers are directed to execute all orders of the Commissioner that relate to the preservation of the health in the City. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a74", "num": "2-202", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|I|2-202", "title": "\u00a7 2-202. Police to execute orders.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-202"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-203to2-204", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 2-203 to 2-204. {Reserved}", "num": "2-203 to 2-204", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|I|2-203to2-204", "title": "2-203 to 2-204. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-203to2-204"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/2/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II Prohibited Conduct \u00a7 2-205. Obstructing, etc., Departmental personnel. \u00a7 2-206. Impersonating Departmental personnel. \u00a7 2-207. Failure to comply with Commissioner's order or notice. 2-208 to 2-209. {Reserved}", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|II", "title": "Part II Prohibited Conduct", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/2/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-205", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-205. Obstructing, etc., Departmental personnel. No person may knowingly obstruct, resist, or interfere with the Commissioner or any officer or employee of the Department while carrying out their powers and duties. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a75(1st cl.)", "num": "2-205", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|II|2-205", "title": "\u00a7 2-205. Obstructing, etc., Departmental personnel.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-205"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-206", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-206. Impersonating Departmental personnel. No person may, without authority to do so: (1) use any identification issued to employees of the Department; or (2) otherwise represent him- or herself as an employee of the Department. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a73(3rd par., 1st cl.)", "num": "2-206", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|II|2-206", "title": "\u00a7 2-206. Impersonating Departmental personnel.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-206"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-207", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-207. Failure to comply with Commissioner's order or notice. No person may refuse, neglect, or otherwise fail to comply with any order or notice issued under this article by or under the authority of the Commissioner. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a76(1st cl.)", "num": "2-207", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|II|2-207", "title": "\u00a7 2-207. Failure to comply with Commissioner's order or notice.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-207"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-208to2-209", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 2-208 to 2-209. {Reserved}", "num": "2-208 to 2-209", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|II|2-208to2-209", "title": "2-208 to 2-209. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-208to2-209"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/2/III", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part III Penalties. \u00a7 2-211. Violation a misdemeanor. \u00a7 2-212. Basic penalty: $500. \u00a7 2-213. Failure to obey notice or order: $200 plus $50/day.", "num": "III", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|III", "title": "Part III Penalties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/2/III"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-211", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-211. Violation a misdemeanor. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to the penalties provided in this Part III. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-211", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|III|2-211", "title": "\u00a7 2-211. Violation a misdemeanor.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-211"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-212", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-212. Basic penalty: $500. Except as otherwise specified in this Part III, the penalty for a violation of this subtitle is a fine of not more than $500 for each offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a73(3rd par.(last cl.)), \u00a75(last cl.)", "num": "2-212", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|III|2-212", "title": "\u00a7 2-212. Basic penalty: $500.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-212"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-213", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-213. Failure to obey notice or order: $200 plus $50/day. Unless a higher penalty is specified in this article for the same offense, the penalty for a violation of \u00a7 2-207 {\"Failure to comply with order or notice\"} of this subtitle is a fine of not more than $200 for each offense, plus $50 for each day that the offense continues. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a76(last cl.)", "num": "2-213", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|2|III|2-213", "title": "\u00a7 2-213. Failure to obey notice or order: $200 plus $50/day.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-213"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/3", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 3 Administrative Hearings \u00a7 2-301. Scope of subtitle. \u00a7 2-302. Opportunity for hearing. \u00a7 2-303. Delegation of hearing authority. \u00a7 2-304. Conduct of hearing. \u00a7 2-305. Evidence. \u00a7 2-306. Final decisions. \u00a7 2-307. Judicial and appellate review.", "num": "3", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|3", "title": "Subtitle 3 Administrative Hearings", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2/3"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-301", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-301. Scope of subtitle. (a) In general. Except as otherwise specified in this article , this subtitle applies whenever: (1) a person is aggrieved by a notice, order, decision, or other action of the Department; or (2) the Department proposes to deny, revoke, suspend, or refuse to renew a license or permit. (b) Exclusion for animal control and protection. This subtitle does not apply to matters that: (1) relate to the Health Department's Office of Animal Control, or (2) otherwise arise under Title 10 {\"Animal Control and Protection\"} of this article . (Ord. 15-322; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-301", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|3|2-301", "title": "\u00a7 2-301. Scope of subtitle.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-301"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-302", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-302. Opportunity for hearing. (a) Licenses and permits. (1) Before the Commissioner takes any final action to deny, revoke, suspend, or refuse to renew a license or permit, the Commissioner must notify the person against whom the action is contemplated of the proposed action. (2) The notice must: (i) be in writing; and (ii) state that a hearing will be provided if, within 10 days of the notice (or any longer period specified in the notice), the person files with the Commissioner a written request for a hearing. (b) Other situations. (1) In all other situations, any person aggrieved by a notice, order, decision, or other action of the Department may request a hearing on the matter. (2) The request must: (i) be in writing; (ii) state the grounds on which the person is contesting the notice, decision, order, or other action; and (iii) unless a different time is specified in this article , be filed with the Commissioner within 10 days of the notice, decision, order, or other action. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-302", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|3|2-302", "title": "\u00a7 2-302. Opportunity for hearing.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-302"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-303", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-303. Delegation of hearing authority. (a) In general. Hearings may be conducted by: (1) the Commissioner; or (2) a hearing officer designated by the Commissioner. (b) Scope of delegation. The Commissioner may delegate to a hearing officer the authority to issue: (1) proposed or final findings of fact; (2) proposed or final conclusions of law; (3) proposed or final findings of fact and conclusions of law; (4) proposed or final orders; or (5) the final administrative decision of the Department. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-303", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|3|2-303", "title": "\u00a7 2-303. Delegation of hearing authority.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-303"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-304", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-304. Conduct of hearing. (a) Notice. (1) The Commissioner must provide all parties reasonable written notice of the hearing. (2) The notice must state: (i) the date, time, place, and nature of the hearing; (ii) the right of a party to be represented, at the party's own expense, by an attorney or, if permitted by law, other representative; (iii) the right of a party to call witnesses and submit documents or other evidence under \u00a7 2-305 of this subtitle ; and (iv) that failure to appear for the scheduled hearing may result in an adverse action against the party. (b) Hearings to be open and informal. Except as otherwise provided by law or by rule or regulation of the Department, all hearings must be: (1) open to the public; and (2) conducted in an orderly but informal manner. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-304", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|3|2-304", "title": "\u00a7 2-304. Conduct of hearing.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-304"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-305", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-305. Evidence. (a) In general. Except as otherwise provided by this section or by rule or regulation of the Department, formal rules of evidence and trial procedures do not apply. (b) Right to submit. On a genuine issue of fact, a party is entitled to: (1) call witnesses; (2) offer evidence, including rebuttal evidence; (3) cross-examine any witness that another party or the Department calls; and (4) present summation and argument. (c) Scope. The Commissioner or hearing officer: (1) may admit probative evidence that reasonable and prudent individuals commonly accept in the conduct of their affairs and give probative effect to that evidence; (2) may not exclude evidence solely on the basis that it is hearsay; (3) must give effect to a privilege recognized by law; (4) may receive documentary evidence in the form of copies or excerpts or through incorporation by reference; (5) may take official notice of a fact that is judicially noticeable or that is general, technical, or scientific and within the specialized knowledge of the Department; and (6) may exclude evidence that is: (i) incompetent; (ii) irrelevant; (iii) immaterial; or (iv) unduly repetitious. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-305", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|3|2-305", "title": "\u00a7 2-305. Evidence.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-305"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-306", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-306. Final decisions. (a) Form and contents. A final decision must: (1) be in writing; and (2) contain separate statements of: (i) the findings of fact; (ii) the conclusions of law; and (iii) the decision or order. (b) Distribution. A copy of the final decision must be mailed or delivered to each party or that party's attorney of record. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-306", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|3|2-306", "title": "\u00a7 2-306. Final decisions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-306"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-307", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 2-307. Judicial and appellate review. (a) Judicial review. A party aggrieved by a final decision under this subtitle may seek judicial review of that decision by petition to the Circuit Court for Baltimore City in accordance with the Maryland Rules of Procedure. (b) Appellate review. A party to the judicial review may appeal the court's final judgment to the Court of Special Appeals in accordance with the Maryland Rules of Procedure. (Ord. 04-672; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "2-307", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|2|3|2-307", "title": "\u00a7 2-307. Judicial and appellate review.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/2-307"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Title 3 Health Facilities Subtitle 1 [Repealed by Ord. 12-027] Subtitle 2 Nursing Homes \u00a7\u00a7 3-201 \u2013 3-206 Subtitle 3 City Clinics and Centers \u00a7\u00a7 3-301 \u2013 3-303 Subtitle 4 Temporary Hospitals \u00a7\u00a7 3-401 \u2013 3-406 Subtitle 5 Limited-Service Pregnancy Centers \u00a7\u00a7 3-501 \u2013 3-506", "num": "3", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3", "title": "Title 3 Health Facilities", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 1 [Repealed by Ord. 12-027]", "num": "1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|1", "title": "Subtitle 1 [Repealed by Ord. 12-027]", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/2", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 2 Nursing Homes \u00a7 3-201. \"Nursing home\" defined. \u00a7 3-202. Cooling requirements. \u00a7 3-203. Equipment and supply standards. 3-204 to 3-205. {Reserved} \u00a7 3-206. Civil penalties: $1,000.", "num": "2", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|2", "title": "Subtitle 2 Nursing Homes", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/2"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-201", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-201. \"Nursing home\" defined. In this subtitle, \"nursing home\" means: (1) a comprehensive care facility, as defined in COMAR 10.07.02.01B(6); (2) an assisted living program, as defined in COMAR 10.07.14.02B(10); and (3) an extended care facility, as defined in COMAR 10.07.02.01B(12). (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7100A(a)", "num": "3-201", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|2|3-201", "title": "\u00a7 3-201. \"Nursing home\" defined.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-201"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-202", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-202. Cooling requirements. From June 1 through September 30 of each year, every nursing home must maintain and operate, throughout all rooms and areas that are occupied or used by residents, a cooling system that maintains a temperature of less than 82\u00b0 F. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7100A(a)(7), (b)", "num": "3-202", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|2|3-202", "title": "\u00a7 3-202. Cooling requirements.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-202"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-203", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-203. Equipment and supply standards. (a) Commissioner to develop. The Commissioner must develop standards and requirements for the following items used in nursing homes: (1) patient lifting and mobility equipment; (2) bathroom and shower equipment; and (3) linens and toiletries. (b) Nursing homes to comply. Every nursing home must comply with the standards and requirements adopted under this section. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7100A(c)", "num": "3-203", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|2|3-203", "title": "\u00a7 3-203. Equipment and supply standards.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-203"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-204to3-205", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 3-204 to 3-205. {Reserved}", "num": "3-204 to 3-205", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|2|3-204to3-205", "title": "3-204 to 3-205. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-204to3-205"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-206", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-206. Civil penalties: $1,000. (a) In general. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle is subject to a civil fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense. (b) Each day a separate offense. Each day that a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7100A(d)", "num": "3-206", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|2|3-206", "title": "\u00a7 3-206. Civil penalties: $1,000.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-206"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/3", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 3 City Clinics and Centers \u00a7 3-301. Commissioner may establish. \u00a7 3-302. Operational guidelines and procedures. \u00a7 3-303. Fees.", "num": "3", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|3", "title": "Subtitle 3 City Clinics and Centers", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/3"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-301", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-301. Commissioner may establish. The Commissioner of Health may establish health clinics and centers in the City. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a751(1st sen.)", "num": "3-301", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|3|3-301", "title": "\u00a7 3-301. Commissioner may establish.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-301"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-302", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-302. Operational guidelines and procedures. (a) In general. The Commissioner must adopt guidelines and procedures for the administration and operation of these clinics and centers. (b) Scope. Among other matters, the guidelines and procedures must govern: (1) the eligibility of individuals for services; and (2) the fees to be charged for those services. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a751(1st sen.), (2nd sen.(part))", "num": "3-302", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|3|3-302", "title": "\u00a7 3-302. Operational guidelines and procedures.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-302"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-303", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-303. Fees. Fees for services at City clinics and centers: (1) may not exceed the actual cost of the services; and (2) must be approved by the Board of Estimates. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a751(2nd sen.(part))", "num": "3-303", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|3|3-303", "title": "\u00a7 3-303. Fees.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-303"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/4", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 4 Temporary Hospitals \u00a7 3-401. Establishment. \u00a7 3-402. Rules and regulations. \u00a7 3-403. Individuals too sick to move. 3-404 to 3-405. {Reserved} \u00a7 3-406. Penalties: $500.", "num": "4", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|4", "title": "Subtitle 4 Temporary Hospitals", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/4"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-401", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-401. Establishment. To prevent morbidity or mortality constituting a threat to public health, the Commissioner of Health, with the approval of the Mayor may: (1) rent or erect suitable structures for temporary hospitals or places to receive the sick or infected; and (2) require sick or infected individuals to be moved to these temporary hospitals or places. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7196(1st, 2nd cls.)", "num": "3-401", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|4|3-401", "title": "\u00a7 3-401. Establishment.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-401"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-402", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-402. Rules and regulations. In the rules and regulations adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article , the Commissioner may, with the approval of the Mayor, adopt rules and regulations to govern: (1) all temporary hospitals or places established under this section; and (2) all individuals residing in or in any way concerned with them. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7196(1st, 3rd cls.) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section..", "num": "3-402", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|4|3-402", "title": "\u00a7 3-402. Rules and regulations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-402"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-403", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-403. Individuals too sick to move. If an individual cannot be moved without danger to his or her health, the house or place where he or she remains is considered to be a temporary hospital for purposes of this subtitle. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7196(2nd cl.)", "num": "3-403", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|4|3-403", "title": "\u00a7 3-403. Individuals too sick to move.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-403"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-404to3-405", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 3-404 to 3-405. {Reserved}", "num": "3-404 to 3-405", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|4|3-404to3-405", "title": "3-404 to 3-405. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-404to3-405"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-406", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-406. Penalties: $500. (a) In general. Any person who violates a rule or regulation adopted under this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $500 for each offense. (b) Each day a separate offense. Each day that a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7204", "num": "3-406", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|4|3-406", "title": "\u00a7 3-406. Penalties: $500.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-406"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/5", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 5 Limited-Service Pregnancy Centers \u00a7 3-501. \"Limited-service pregnancy center\" defined. \u00a7 3-502. Disclaimer required. \u00a7 3-503. Violation notice. 3-504 to 3-505. {Reserved} \u00a7 3-506. Enforcement by citation.", "num": "5", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|5", "title": "Subtitle 5 Limited-Service Pregnancy Centers", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3/5"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-501", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-501. \"Limited-service pregnancy center\" defined. In this subtitle, \"limited-service pregnancy center\" means any person: (1) whose primary purpose is to provide pregnancy-related services; and (2) who: (i) for a fee or as a free service, provides information about pregnancy-related services; but (ii) does not provide or refer for: (A) abortions; or (B) nondirective and comprehensive birth-control services. (Ord. 09-252.)", "num": "3-501", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|5|3-501", "title": "\u00a7 3-501. \"Limited-service pregnancy center\" defined.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-501"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-502", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-502. Disclaimer required. (a) In general. A limited-service pregnancy center must provide its clients and potential clients with a disclaimer substantially to the effect that the center does not provide or make referral for abortion or birth-control services. (b) How given. The disclaimer required by this section must be given through 1 or more signs that are: (1) written in English and Spanish; (2) easily readable; and (3) conspicuously posted in the center's waiting room or other area where individuals await service. (Ord. 09-252.)", "num": "3-502", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|5|3-502", "title": "\u00a7 3-502. Disclaimer required.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-502"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-503", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-503. Violation notice. If the Health Commissioner learns that a pregnancy center is in violation of this subtitle, the Commissioner shall issue a written notice ordering the center to correct the violation within 10 days of the notice or within any longer period that the Commissioner specifies in the notice. (Ord. 09-252.)", "num": "3-503", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|5|3-503", "title": "\u00a7 3-503. Violation notice.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-503"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-504to3-505", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 3-504 to 3-505. {Reserved}", "num": "3-504 to 3-505", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|5|3-504to3-505", "title": "3-504 to 3-505. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-504to3-505"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-506", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 3-506. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. The failure to comply with an order issued under \u00a7 3-503 {\"Violation notice\"} of this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 09-252.)", "num": "3-506", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|3|5|3-506", "title": "\u00a7 3-506. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/3-506"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Title 4 Disease Control Subtitle 1 General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 4-101 \u2013 4-103 Subtitle 2 Reporting Diseases \u2014 In General \u00a7\u00a7 4-201 \u2013 4-208 Subtitle 3 Reporting Diseases \u2014 Tuberculosis \u00a7\u00a7 4-301 \u2013 4-306 Subtitle 4 Controlling Communicable Diseases \u00a7\u00a7 4-401 \u2013 4-410 Subtitle 5 Immunizations \u00a7\u00a7 4-501 \u2013 4-511 Subtitle 6 Lead Poisoning \u00a7\u00a7 4-601 \u2013 4-612 Subtitle 7 Biological Terrorism Reporting \u00a7\u00a7 4-701 \u2013 4-708 Subtitle 8 [Reserved] Subtitle 9 Penalties \u00a7\u00a7 4-901 \u2013 4-903", "num": "4", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4", "title": "Title 4 Disease Control", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 1 General Provisions \u00a7 4-101. Nature of reports. \u00a7 4-102. Form and contents of reports. \u00a7 4-103. Inspection of records.", "num": "1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|1", "title": "Subtitle 1 General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-101", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-101. Nature of reports. (a) Reporting does not breach confidentiality. The making of a report as required or permitted by this title is not a breach of a patient's confidentiality. (b) Confidentiality of reports. Except as otherwise required by law, all reports made under this title are confidential and not open to public inspection. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7205(e)(1st cl.), 206(h), 211(e)", "num": "4-101", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|1|4-101", "title": "\u00a7 4-101. Nature of reports.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-101"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-102", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-102. Form and contents of reports. (a) Form. All reports must be made in writing, in the form that the Commissioner requires. (b) Contents. In addition to any other information specified in this title or required by the Commissioner, all reports must contain the following patient information, to the extent known: (1) name; (2) place of dwelling; (3) date of birth or, if not known, approximate age; (4) sex; (5) race/ethnicity; and (6) occupation. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7187(a), 188, 206(c), 209(a)", "num": "4-102", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|1|4-102", "title": "\u00a7 4-102. Form and contents of reports.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-102"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-103", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-103. Inspection of records. The Commissioner may inspect the records of any person or facility subject to this title to determine compliance with this title. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7206(i), 211(f)", "num": "4-103", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|1|4-103", "title": "\u00a7 4-103. Inspection of records.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-103"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/2", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 2 Reporting Diseases \u2014 In General Part I By Health Care Practitioners \u00a7\u00a7 4-201 \u2013 4-204 Part II By Others \u00a7\u00a7 4-207 \u2013 4-208", "num": "2", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2", "title": "Subtitle 2 Reporting Diseases \u2014 In General", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/2"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/2/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I By Health Care Practitioners \u00a7 4-201. Physicians. \u00a7 4-202. Health care facilities. \u00a7 4-203. Laboratories \u2014 in general. \u00a7 4-204. Laboratories \u2014 referral of cultures. 4-205 to 4-206. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2|I", "title": "Part I By Health Care Practitioners", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/2/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-201", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-201. Physicians. (a) Duty to report. Every physician must report to the Commissioner of Health every confirmed or suspected diagnosis of any disease or condition that, the Commissioner specifies by rule or regulation adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article for purposes of this section . (b) When to report. Unless a different time is set by the Commissioner or by State law, the report must be made within 48 hours of the diagnosis. (c) Contents of report. In addition to the information required by \u00a7 4-102 {\"Form and contents of reports\"} of this title , the report must contain the following information: (1) the identity of the disease or condition; and (2) the date of its onset. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7187(a) Editor's Notes Text of subsection (a) conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-201", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2|I|4-201", "title": "\u00a7 4-201. Physicians.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-201"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-202", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-202. Health care facilities. (a) Reporting officer. The chief administrative officer of each hospital, related institution, clinic, pharmacy, or other health care facility must appoint an individual from the facility's full-time staff to serve as the facility's reporting officer. (b) Duty to report. The reporting officer must report to the Commissioner every confirmed or suspected diagnosis of any disease or condition that the Commissioner specifies by rule or regulation adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article for purposes of this section. (c) When to report. Except for specified diseases or conditions that the Commissioner or State law requires to be reported on a more timely basis, the report must be made within 48 hours for all patients who have been: (1) newly admitted to the facility; (2) admitted to an isolation ward; (3) treated on an emergency or out-patient basis; or (4) discharged without having been previously reported. (d) Contents of report. In addition to the information required by \u00a7 4-102 {\"Form and contents of reports\"} of this title , the report must contain the following information: (1) the identity of the disease or condition; and (2) the date of its onset. (e) Nature of report. The reporting of a disease or condition under this section does not absolve the attending physician of the duty to report the same disease or condition under \u00a7 4-201 {\"Physicians\"} of this subtitle . (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7205(a) - (d), (e)(2nd cl.) Editor's Notes Text of subsection (b) conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section..", "num": "4-202", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2|I|4-202", "title": "\u00a7 4-202. Health care facilities.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-202"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-203", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-203. Laboratories \u2014 in general. (a) Duty to report. The individual in charge of any public or private medical laboratory must report to the Commissioner the result of any test of a specimen from a human body that is microscopic cultural, histological, pathological, immunological, serological, or other confirmed or suspected evidence of any disease or condition that the Commissioner specifies by rule or regulation adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article for purposes of this section. (b) When to report. (1) Unless a different time is set by the Commissioner or by State law, the report must be made within 48 hours of obtaining the test results. (2) When more than 1 specimen is taken from the same patient during a single disease episode, the individual in charge of the laboratory need not report every test result that shows evidence of the same disease if: (i) at least 1 positive test report is reported; and (ii) the Commissioner approves in writing the reporting of less than all test results. (c) Contents of report. In addition to the information required by \u00a7 4-102 {\"Form and contents of reports\"} of this title , the report must contain the following information: (1) the date and type of test performed; (2) the results of the test; (3) the name and address of the physician for whom the examination was performed; and (4) if applicable, an enumeration of colonies of acid-fast bacilli, according to Diagnostic Standards of the American Thoracic Society. (d) Test result not final diagnosis. The result of a laboratory test: (1) is not to be considered a final diagnosis; and (2) does not absolve the attending physician of the duty to report her or his diagnosis of the case under \u00a7 4-201 {\"Physicians\"} of this subtitle . (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7206(a) - (d) Editor's Notes Text of subsection (a) conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-203", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2|I|4-203", "title": "\u00a7 4-203. Laboratories \u2014 in general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-203"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-204", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-204. Laboratories \u2014 referral of cultures. (a) When referral required. The Commissioner may require that cultures containing acid-fast bacilli be referred to the Maryland State Laboratory unless they have been identified in accordance with standards set by the Commissioner. (b) Effect of referral. The referral of cultures does not: (1) satisfy the reporting requirements of \u00a7 4-203 {\"Laboratories \u2014 in general\"} of this subtitle ; or (2) excuse delay in transmitting a presumptive culture result to the attending physician. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7206(e)", "num": "4-204", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2|I|4-204", "title": "\u00a7 4-204. Laboratories \u2014 referral of cultures.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-204"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-205to4-206", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 4-205 to 4-206. {Reserved}", "num": "4-205 to 4-206", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2|I|4-205to4-206", "title": "4-205 to 4-206. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-205to4-206"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/2/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II By Others \u00a7 4-207. Lodging facilities. \u00a7 4-208. Vessels in harbor.", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2|II", "title": "Part II By Others", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/2/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-207", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-207. Lodging facilities. (a) \"Lodging facility\" defined. In this section, \"lodging facility\" means any: (1) hotel or motel; (2) rooming house; (3) apartment house; (4) dwelling house; or (5) public or private institution in which individuals abide temporarily or permanently. (b) Duty to report. The individual in charge of a lodging facility must report to the Commissioner anyone in the lodging facility who is suffering from any disease or condition that the Commissioner specifies by rule or regulation adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article for purposes of this section. (c) When to report. Unless a different time is set by the Commissioner or by State law, the report must be made within 24 hours of the individual's being told or otherwise becoming aware of the disease or condition. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7188, 189 Editor's Notes Text of subsection (b) conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-207", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2|II|4-207", "title": "\u00a7 4-207. Lodging facilities.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-207"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-208", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-208. Vessels in harbor. (a) Scope of section. This section does not apply to a vessel that is in medical isolation. (b) Duty to report. The master, chief officer, or consignee of a vessel must report to the Commissioner anyone on board who is suspected of suffering from a communicable disease. (c) When to report. The report must be made immediately on the vessel's coming within \u00bc mile of any dock, wharf, or building in the City. (d) Contents of report. In addition to the information required by \u00a7 4-102 {\"Form and contents of reports\"} of this title , the report must contain the following information: (1) the name and location of the vessel; (2) the name of the suspected communicable disease; and (3) the name and condition of all individuals on board suffering from the disease. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7190", "num": "4-208", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|2|II|4-208", "title": "\u00a7 4-208. Vessels in harbor.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-208"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/3", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 3 Reporting Diseases \u2014 Tuberculosis \u00a7 4-301. Laboratory test results. \u00a7 4-302. Drugs suggesting treatment. \u00a7 4-303. Physician's contact investigation. \u00a7 4-304. Patient leaving hospital or institution. \u00a7 4-305. Discontinued treatment. \u00a7 4-306. Death from tuberculosis.", "num": "3", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|3", "title": "Subtitle 3 Reporting Diseases \u2014 Tuberculosis", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/3"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-301", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-301. Laboratory test results. (a) Duty to report. If a medical laboratory performs mycobacterial drug susceptibility tests of cultures that are identified as M. tuberculosis and that have not been referred to the Maryland Laboratories Administration for this determination, the individual in charge of the laboratory must report the test results to the Commissioner. (b) When to report. Unless a different time is set by the Commissioner or by State law, the report must be made within 48 hours of obtaining the test results. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7206(f)", "num": "4-301", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|3|4-301", "title": "\u00a7 4-301. Laboratory test results.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-301"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-302", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-302. Drugs suggesting treatment. (a) Duty to report. The individual in charge of any public or private pharmacy, other than a facility operated by the Department, must report to the Commissioner of Health whenever it dispenses any anti- tuberculosis medication that the Commissioner specifies by rule or regulation adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article for purposes of this section. (b) When to report. Unless a different time is set by the Commissioner or by State law, the report must be made within 48 hours of having dispensed the medication. (c) Contents of report. In addition to the information required by \u00a7 4-102 {\"Form and contents of reports\"} of this title , the report must contain the following information: (1) the type, and strength of medication issued; (2) the daily dosage; (3) the dosing schedule; (4) the total amount prescribed; (5) the name and address of the physician who issued the prescription; and (6) the date the medication was dispensed. (d) Report not final diagnosis. The prescription of medication: (1) is not to be considered a final diagnosis; and (2) does not absolve the attending physician of the duty to report her or his diagnosis of the case under \u00a7 4-201 {\"Physicians\"} of this subtitle . (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7211(a) - (c) Editor's Notes Text of subsection (a) conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-302", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|3|4-302", "title": "\u00a7 4-302. Drugs suggesting treatment.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-302"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-303", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-303. Physician's contact investigation. (a) Duty to conduct. A physician who treats a patient with tuberculosis must: (1) thoroughly investigate all individuals who might have been exposed to the patient at home, work, or otherwise; or (2) request the Commissioner to undertake this investigation. (b) Duty to report. If the physician undertakes the investigation required by this section, the physician must report the results to the Commissioner. (c) Contents of report. In addition to any other information that the Commissioner requires, the report must contain: (1) evidence that an investigation has been thoroughly conducted; (2) the identity of the source case; and (3) the following information about each individual tested: (i) the date and type of test performed; (ii) the results of the test; and (iii) the personal information listed in \u00a7 4-102 {\"Form and contents of reports\"} of this title . (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7207(a), (b)", "num": "4-303", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|3|4-303", "title": "\u00a7 4-303. Physician's contact investigation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-303"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-304", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-304. Patient leaving hospital or institution. (a) Duty to notify. If a patient with a confirmed or suspected diagnosis of tuberculosis leaves a ward, emergency room, or outpatient department of a hospital or other institution without the consent or against the advice of a physician or other individual in charge, the physician or other individual in charge must notify the Commissioner of the patient's departure. (b) When to notify. Unless a different time is set by the Commissioner or by State law, the notice must be given within 24 hours of the patient's departure. (c) Action by Commissioner. The Commissioner may take whatever action is needed to protect the public from that patient. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7208(a)", "num": "4-304", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|3|4-304", "title": "\u00a7 4-304. Patient leaving hospital or institution.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-304"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-305", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-305. Discontinued treatment. (a) Duty to notify. If any patient being treated for tuberculosis discontinues treatment before the period that the Commissioner sets for that treatment, the attending physician must notify the Commissioner of the discontinuance. (b) When to notify. Unless a different time is set by the Commissioner or by State law, the notice must be given within 24 hours of the discontinuance. (c) Action by Commissioner. The Commissioner may take whatever action is needed to protect the public from that patient. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7210(a)", "num": "4-305", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|3|4-305", "title": "\u00a7 4-305. Discontinued treatment.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-305"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-306", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-306. Death from tuberculosis. (a) Duty to report. Every physician or medical examiner must report to the Commissioner if he or she believes that an individual he or she attended or examined has died of tuberculosis. (b) When to report. Unless a different time is set by the Commissioner or by State law, the report must be made within 48 hours of the death or the discovery of the disease, whichever is later. (c) Contents of report. In addition to the information required by \u00a7 4-102 {\"Form and contents of reports\"} of this title , the report must contain the place of death. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7209(a)", "num": "4-306", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|3|4-306", "title": "\u00a7 4-306. Death from tuberculosis.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-306"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/4", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 4 Controlling Communicable Diseases \u00a7 4-401. \"Communicable disease\" defined. \u00a7 4-402. Investigations generally. \u00a7 4-403. Examinations of individuals. \u00a7 4-404. Action to prevent spread; public notice of disease. \u00a7 4-405. Vacating premises. \u00a7 4-406. Medical isolation \u2014 in general. \u00a7 4-407. Medical isolation \u2014 court papers to be sealed. 4-408 to 4-409. {Reserved} \u00a7 4-410. Prohibited conduct.", "num": "4", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4", "title": "Subtitle 4 Controlling Communicable Diseases", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/4"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-401", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-401. \"Communicable disease\" defined. In this subtitle, \"communicable disease\" means any contagious, infectious, or communicable disease or condition that the Commissioner specifies by rule or regulation adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article for purposes of this subtitle. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-401", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4|4-401", "title": "\u00a7 4-401. \"Communicable disease\" defined.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-401"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-402", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-402. Investigations generally. The Commissioner may investigate any report of a case or suspected case of a communicable disease or communicable disease carrier, to determine the source of infection and the need for restricting movement or isolating affected individuals. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7213(1st sen.)", "num": "4-402", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4|4-402", "title": "\u00a7 4-402. Investigations generally.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-402"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-403", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-403. Examinations of individuals. (a) Scope of section. (1) Except as specified in paragraph (2) of this subsection, this section applies to any individual: (i) who has or whom the Commissioner of Health suspects on reasonable grounds of having a communicable disease; (ii) who is or whom the Commissioner suspects on reasonable grounds of being a communicable disease carrier; or (iii) who is or whom the Commissioner suspects on reasonable grounds of having been in contact with any individual described in item (i) or (ii) of this subsection. (2) This section may not be applied to interfere with any individual who is a bona fide practicing Christian Scientist and who receives treatment from registered Christian Science practitioners. (b) Examination required. On request of the Commissioner, any individual described in subsection (a) of this section must, for the purpose of determining if she or he has a communicable disease or is a carrier: (1) undergo a medical examination; and (2) submit specimens of body fluids, secretion, excretion, or discharge for laboratory examination. (c) By whom made. The medical examination may be made by: (1) the Commissioner or a physician selected by the Commissioner; or (2) at the option of the individual to be examined, any physician qualified to make this sort of examination. (d) Report of physician. The examining physician must promptly report the results of the examination to the Commissioner. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7213(1st sen.), 214(2nd sen.)", "num": "4-403", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4|4-403", "title": "\u00a7 4-403. Examinations of individuals.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-403"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-404", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-404. Action to prevent spread; public notice of disease. Whenever a communicable disease is found to exist, the Commissioner may: (1) take all possible action to prevent the disease from spreading; and (2) give public notice of the disease and of affected places, by all means that the Commissioner believes would be effective, including the posting of affected places. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7197(1st cl.)", "num": "4-404", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4|4-404", "title": "\u00a7 4-404. Action to prevent spread; public notice of disease.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-404"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-405", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-405. Vacating premises. (a) In general. Whenever an infectious agent is discovered in any dwelling or other building that is overcrowded, in a filthy and neglected state, or located in an unhealthy or crowded part of the City, the Commissioner, with the approval of the Mayor, may require the inhabitants of the building to move elsewhere while the City cleans and disinfects the building. (b) City to bear expenses. All expenses incurred under this section for moving, temporary housing, cleaning, and disinfecting are to be borne by the City. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7194", "num": "4-405", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4|4-405", "title": "\u00a7 4-405. Vacating premises.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-405"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-406", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-406. Medical isolation \u2014 in general. In the rules and regulations adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article , the Commissioner may require the medical isolation of individuals having a communicable or potentially communicable disease or condition dangerous to the public health. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7212(1st par.(1st cl.)) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-406", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4|4-406", "title": "\u00a7 4-406. Medical isolation \u2014 in general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-406"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-407", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-407. Medical isolation \u2014 court papers to be sealed. In any court proceeding brought to enforce a medical isolation, all papers filed with the court: (1) must be sealed; and (2) may be inspected only by order of the court, on notice to the individual named in those papers and for good cause shown. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7212(2nd par.)", "num": "4-407", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4|4-407", "title": "\u00a7 4-407. Medical isolation \u2014 court papers to be sealed.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-407"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-408to4-409", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 4-408 to 4-409. {Reserved}", "num": "4-408 to 4-409", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4|4-408to4-409", "title": "4-408 to 4-409. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-408to4-409"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-410", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-410. Prohibited conduct. No person may: (1) refuse, neglect, or otherwise fail to undergo an examination as required by \u00a7 4-403{\"Examinations of individuals\"} of this subtitle; (2) remove or deface any notice posted under \u00a7 4-404 {\"Action to prevent spread; public notice of disease\"} of this subtitle ; (3) refuse, neglect, or otherwise fail to comply fully with an order for medical isolation issued under authority of \u00a7 4-406 {\"Medical isolation\"} of this subtitle ; (4) needlessly expose others to the risk of a communicable disease, whether by exposure of him- or herself, another person, a dead body, or any infected material; or (5) otherwise, by reason of non-cooperation or carelessness, endanger the public health. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7192(2nd cl.), 197(2nd cl.), 212(1st par.(2nd cl.)), 215", "num": "4-410", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|4|4-410", "title": "\u00a7 4-410. Prohibited conduct.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-410"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/5", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 5 Immunizations Part I Purpose and Need \u00a7\u00a7 4-501 \u2013 4-501 Part II Immunization Required \u00a7\u00a7 4-503 \u2013 4-506 Part III Pediatric Registry \u00a7\u00a7 4-509 \u2013 4-511", "num": "5", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5", "title": "Subtitle 5 Immunizations", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/5"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/5/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I Purpose and Need \u00a7 4-501. In general. 4-502. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|I", "title": "Part I Purpose and Need", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/5/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-501", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-501. In general. Immunizations are a proven preventive measure in reducing the morbidity and mortality associated with childhood communicable diseases. Accordingly, to safeguard the public health and welfare, the City needs to attain and sustain an immunization coverage rate of at least 90% for age-appropriate vaccinations among infants and preschool children residing in the City. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7215A(a)", "num": "4-501", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|I|4-501", "title": "\u00a7 4-501. In general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-501"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-502", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 4-502. {Reserved}", "num": "4-502", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|I|4-502", "title": "4-502. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-502"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/5/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II Immunization Required \u00a7 4-503. Parental responsibility. \u00a7 4-504. Scope of enforcement. \u00a7 4-505. Rules and regulations. \u00a7 4-506. Exceptions. 4-507 to 4-508. {Reserved}", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|II", "title": "Part II Immunization Required", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/5/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-503", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-503. Parental responsibility. Except as specified in \u00a7 4-506 {\"Exceptions\"} of this subtitle , every parent and guardian must provide for the timely and appropriate immunization of their minor children against all immunization-preventable childhood diseases, as specified by rule or regulation that the Commissioner adopts under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article . (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7199 Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-503", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|II|4-503", "title": "\u00a7 4-503. Parental responsibility.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-503"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-504", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-504. Scope of enforcement. In enforcing this Part II, each child who is not properly immunized as required is considered a separate offense. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "4-504", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|II|4-504", "title": "\u00a7 4-504. Scope of enforcement.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-504"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-505", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-505. Rules and regulations. In adopting rules and regulations under this Part II, the Commissioner must consider the recommendations of the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "4-505", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|II|4-505", "title": "\u00a7 4-505. Rules and regulations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-505"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-506", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-506. Exceptions. This Part II does not apply: (1) to any child whose parent or guardian, in accordance with the rules and regulations of the State Secretary of Health and Mental Hygiene, objects to immunization on the ground that it conflicts with the parent's or guardian's bona fide religious beliefs and practices; or (2) if, because of a child's medical condition, the administration of an immunizing agent could precipitate a medical emergency. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "4-506", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|II|4-506", "title": "\u00a7 4-506. Exceptions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-506"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-507to4-508", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 4-507 to 4-508. {Reserved}", "num": "4-507 to 4-508", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|II|4-507to4-508", "title": "4-507 to 4-508. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-507to4-508"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/5/III", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part III Pediatric Registry \u00a7 4-509. Commissioner to establish registry. \u00a7 4-510. Reports by health care providers. \u00a7 4-511. Civil penalties: $1,000.", "num": "III", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|III", "title": "Part III Pediatric Registry", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/5/III"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-509", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-509. Commissioner to establish registry. The Commissioner of Health is directed to adopt rules and regulations under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article to implement a registry of immunization history for children residing in the City. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7215A(b) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-509", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|III|4-509", "title": "\u00a7 4-509. Commissioner to establish registry.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-509"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-510", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-510. Reports by health care providers. (a) Duty to report. Each pediatric, family, and general health care provider must report to the Commissioner whenever that provider administers an immunizing agent to any child who is less than 5 years old and resides in the City. (b) When to report. The report must be made within 14 days after administering the immunizing agent. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7215B", "num": "4-510", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|III|4-510", "title": "\u00a7 4-510. Reports by health care providers.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-510"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-511", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-511. Civil penalties: $1,000. (a) Fine. Any health care provider who violates any provision of this Part III or of a rule or regulation adopted under it is subject to a civil penalty of not more than $1,000 for each violation. (b) Withholding assistance; records audit. In addition to imposing a fine, the court may: (1) issue an order for withholding publicly distributed vaccines or pediatric primary care funding or both vaccines and funding; and (2) order the Department to conduct a medical records audit of the health care provider, the cost of the audit to be paid by the health care provider. (c) Subtitle 9 inapplicable. Subtitle 9 {\"Penalties\"} of this title does not apply to violations of this Part III. (Ord. 00-020; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7215C", "num": "4-511", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|5|III|4-511", "title": "\u00a7 4-511. Civil penalties: $1,000.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-511"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/6", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 6 Lead Poisoning Part I Screening Required \u00a7\u00a7 4-601 \u2013 4-604 Part II Registry; Reports \u00a7\u00a7 4-606 \u2013 4-609 Part III Public School Water Fountains \u00a7\u00a7 4-611 \u2013 4-612", "num": "6", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6", "title": "Subtitle 6 Lead Poisoning", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/6"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/6/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I Screening Required \u00a7 4-601. Parental responsibility. \u00a7 4-602. Provider's responsibility. \u00a7 4-603. Exceptions. \u00a7 4-604. Enforcement. 4-605. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|I", "title": "Part I Screening Required", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/6/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-601", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-601. Parental responsibility. Except as specified in \u00a7 4-603 {\"Exceptions\"} of this subtitle , every parent and guardian must provide for the timely and appropriate testing for lead poisoning of his or her minor children, as specified by the rules and regulations that the Commissioner adopts under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article . (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 00-020.) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-601", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|I|4-601", "title": "\u00a7 4-601. Parental responsibility.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-601"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-602", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-602. Provider's responsibility. Except as specified in \u00a7 4-603 {\"Exceptions\"} of this subtitle , every pediatric and primary care health provider must order the timely and appropriate testing for lead poisoning of his or her minor patients, as specified by the rules and regulations that the Commissioner adopts under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article . (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 00-020.) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-602", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|I|4-602", "title": "\u00a7 4-602. Provider's responsibility.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-602"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-603", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-603. Exceptions. This Part I does not apply to any child whose parent or guardian, in accordance with the rules and regulations of the State Secretary of Health and Mental Hygiene, objects to testing on the ground that it conflicts with the parent's or guardian's bona fide religious beliefs and practices. (Ord. 00-020.)", "num": "4-603", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|I|4-603", "title": "\u00a7 4-603. Exceptions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-603"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-604", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-604. Enforcement. (a) Separate offenses. In enforcing this Part I, each child who is not properly tested as required is considered a separate violation. (b) Civil penalties \u2014 Parents and guardians. \u00a7 4-601{\"Parental responsibility\"} of this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of an environmental citation under Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"} of the City Code. (c) Civil penalties \u2014 Providers. Any provider who violates any provision of \u00a7 4-602 {\"Provider's responsibility\"} of this subtitle or of a rule or regulation adopted under it is subject to a civil penalty of not more than $100 for each violation. (d) Subtitle 9 inapplicable. Subtitle 9 {\"Penalties\"} of this title does not apply to violations of this Part I. (Ord. 00-020.)", "num": "4-604", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|I|4-604", "title": "\u00a7 4-604. Enforcement.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-604"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-605", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 4-605. {Reserved}", "num": "4-605", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|I|4-605", "title": "4-605. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-605"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/6/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II Registry; Reports \u00a7 4-606. Commissioner to establish registry. \u00a7 4-607. Reports by medical laboratories. \u00a7 4-608. Reports by child care facilities. \u00a7 4-609. Civil penalties: $100. 4-610. {Reserved}", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|II", "title": "Part II Registry; Reports", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/6/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-606", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-606. Commissioner to establish registry. The Commissioner of Health is directed to adopt rules and regulations under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article to implement a registry of screening history for children residing in the City. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 00-020.) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-606", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|II|4-606", "title": "\u00a7 4-606. Commissioner to establish registry.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-606"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-607", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-607. Reports by medical laboratories. (a) Duty to report. Each medical laboratory must report to the Commissioner whenever that laboratory tests for lead poisoning any child who is less than 7 years old and who resides in the City. (b) When to report. The report must: (1) be made within 14 days after the test is conducted; and (2) contain the information that the Commissioner requires, as specified by the Commissioner's rules and regulations. (Ord. 00-020.)", "num": "4-607", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|II|4-607", "title": "\u00a7 4-607. Reports by medical laboratories.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-607"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-608", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-608. Reports by child care facilities. (a) \"Child care facility\" defined. (1) In this section, \"child care facility\" means any facility that: (i) provides for the care, custody, or control of a minor child; and (ii) is required to be licensed by or registered with the State for that purpose. (2) Subject to the criteria contained in paragraph (1) of this subsection, \"Child care facility\" includes any: (i) child care center; (ii) child care home; (iii) child care institution; (iv) family day care home; (v) non-public nursery school; or (vi) non-public kindergarten. (b) Duty to report. Each child care facility must report to the Commissioner whenever that facility receives a State Health Inventory or other report that contains information on whether a child who resides in the City has been tested for lead poisoning. (c) When to report. The report must: (1) be made within 14 days after the State Health Inventory or other report is received by the facility; and (2) contain the information that the Commissioner requires, as specified by the Commissioner's rules and regulations. (Ord. 00-020.)", "num": "4-608", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|II|4-608", "title": "\u00a7 4-608. Reports by child care facilities.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-608"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-609", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-609. Civil penalties: $100. (a) Fine. Any person who violates any provision of this Part II or of a rule or regulation adopted under it is subject to a civil penalty of not more than $100 for each violation. (b) Subtitle 9 inapplicable. Subtitle 9 {\"Penalties\"} of this title does not apply to violations of this Part II. (Ord. 00-020.)", "num": "4-609", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|II|4-609", "title": "\u00a7 4-609. Civil penalties: $100.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-609"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-610", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 4-610. {Reserved}", "num": "4-610", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|II|4-610", "title": "4-610. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-610"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/6/III", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part III Public School Water Fountains \u00a7 4-611. Inspections required. \u00a7 4-612. Failing fountains.", "num": "III", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|III", "title": "Part III Public School Water Fountains", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/6/III"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-611", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-611. Inspections required. (a) In general. Every 2 years, the Health Commissioner shall test all activated water fountains at each of 10 randomly-selected public schools. (b) Expenses. Testing shall be conducted at the expense of the Baltimore City School System. (Ord. 04-647.)", "num": "4-611", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|III|4-611", "title": "\u00a7 4-611. Inspections required.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-611"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-612", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-612. Failing fountains. Any water fountain that fails to meet EPA standards must be taken out of service. (Ord. 04-647.)", "num": "4-612", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|6|III|4-612", "title": "\u00a7 4-612. Failing fountains.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-612"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/7", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 7 Biological Terrorism Reporting \u00a7 4-701. Definitions. \u00a7 4-702. In general. \u00a7 4-703. EMS calls. \u00a7 4-704. Animal carcasses. \u00a7 4-705. School absences. \u00a7 4-706. Health Center treatments. \u00a7 4-707. Emergency department visits. \u00a7 4-708. Medications.", "num": "7", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|7", "title": "Subtitle 7 Biological Terrorism Reporting", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/7"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-701", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-701. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Suspect symptoms. \"Suspect symptoms\" means those symptoms that, by rule or regulation adopted under \u00a7 2-106 of this article , the Commissioner designates as being indicative of a possible biological terrorist attack. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 01-259.) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "4-701", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|7|4-701", "title": "\u00a7 4-701. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-701"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-702", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-702. In general. (a) When reports to be made. Unless the Commissioner's rules or regulations specify otherwise, each report required by this subtitle must be made daily for all events occurring within the preceding 24 hours. (b) How reports to be made. Each report required by this subtitle must be made electronically, by a method and in the form the Commissioner's rules or regulations specify. (c) Contents of reports. Each report required by this subtitle must contain: (1) all of the information specified in this subtitle for that report; and (2) all other information that the Commissioner requires by rule or regulation. (Ord. 01-259.)", "num": "4-702", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|7|4-702", "title": "\u00a7 4-702. In general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-702"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-703", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-703. EMS calls. (a) Duty to report. The Emergency Medical System of the Baltimore City Fire Department must report to the Commissioner on all calls made to assist individuals who evidence suspect symptoms. (b) Contents of report. In addition to any other information required by rule or regulation, the report must specify for each reporting period: (1) the aggregate number of calls subject to the report; and (2) for each of these calls, the specific suspect symptom or symptoms identified. (Ord. 01-259.)", "num": "4-703", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|7|4-703", "title": "\u00a7 4-703. EMS calls.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-703"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-704", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-704. Animal carcasses. (a) Duty to report. The Department of Public Works, the Department of Transportation, and the Health Department's Bureau of Animal Control must report to the Commissioner on animal carcasses that the Commissioner specifies by rule or regulation. (b) Contents of report. In addition to any other information required by rule or regulation, the report must specify for each reporting period: (1) the aggregate number of carcasses subject to the report; and (2) the number of carcasses for each species. (Ord. 15-435; Ord. 01-259.)", "num": "4-704", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|7|4-704", "title": "\u00a7 4-704. Animal carcasses.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-704"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-705", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-705. School absences. (a) Duty to report. The Baltimore City Public School System must report to the Commissioner on all elementary school absences. (b) Contents of report. In addition to any other information required by rule or regulation, the report must specify for each reporting period: (1) the aggregate percentage of absences Citywide; and (2) the percentage of absences by grade. (Ord. 01-259.)", "num": "4-705", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|7|4-705", "title": "\u00a7 4-705. School absences.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-705"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-706", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-706. Health Center treatments. (a) Duty to report. The Baltimore City Community Health Centers must report to the Commissioner on all patients who evidence suspect symptoms. (b) Contents of report. In addition to any other information required by rule or regulation, the report must specify for each reporting period: (1) the aggregate number of patients subject to the report; and (2) for each of these patients, the specific suspect symptom or symptoms identified. (Ord. 01-259.)", "num": "4-706", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|7|4-706", "title": "\u00a7 4-706. Health Center treatments.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-706"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-707", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-707. Emergency department visits. (a) Duty to report. The reporting officer of each hospital, related institution, clinic, or other health care facility, as appointed under \u00a7 4-202 of this title , must report to the Commissioner on all emergency department visits by individuals who evidence suspect symptoms. (b) Contents of report. In addition to any other information required by rule or regulation, the report must specify for each reporting period: (1) the aggregate number of patients subject to the report; and (2) for each of these patients, the specific suspect symptom or symptoms identified. (Ord. 01-259.)", "num": "4-707", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|7|4-707", "title": "\u00a7 4-707. Emergency department visits.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-707"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-708", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-708. Medications. (a) Duty to report. The individual in charge of any public or private pharmacy must report to the Commissioner on all sales of any over-the-counter medication that the Commissioner designates by rule or regulation as being a medication used for the treatment of suspect symptoms. (b) Contents of report. In addition to any other information required by rule or regulation, the report must specify, for each medication sold during a reporting period: (1) the type and strength of that index medication; and (2) the aggregate number of units sold during the reporting period. (Ord. 01-259.)", "num": "4-708", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|7|4-708", "title": "\u00a7 4-708. Medications.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-708"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/8", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 8 [Reserved]", "num": "8", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|8", "title": "Subtitle 8 [Reserved]", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/8"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/9", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 9 Penalties \u00a7 4-901. Violation a misdemeanor. \u00a7 4-902. Basic penalty: $1,000 and 12 months. \u00a7 4-903. Failure to vaccinate: $500.", "num": "9", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|9", "title": "Subtitle 9 Penalties", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4/9"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-901", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-901. Violation a misdemeanor. Except as otherwise specified in this title, any person who violates any provision of this title is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to the penalties provided in this subtitle. (Ord. 00-020; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "4-901", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|9|4-901", "title": "\u00a7 4-901. Violation a misdemeanor.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-901"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-902", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-902. Basic penalty: $1,000 and 12 months. Except as otherwise specified in this title, the penalty for a violation of this title is a fine of not more than $1,000 or imprisonment for not more than 12 months or both fine and imprisonment for each offense. (Ord. 01-259; Ord. 00-020; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7187(b), inter alia", "num": "4-902", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|9|4-902", "title": "\u00a7 4-902. Basic penalty: $1,000 and 12 months.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-902"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-903", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 4-903. Failure to vaccinate: $500. The penalty for a violation of \u00a7 4-503 {\"Parental responsibility\"} of this title is a fine of not more than $500 for each offense. (Ord. 00-020; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7204(2nd cl.)", "num": "4-903", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|4|9|4-903", "title": "\u00a7 4-903. Failure to vaccinate: $500.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/4-903"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Title 5 Nuisance Control Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 5-101 \u2013 5-102 Subtitle 2 Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Generally \u00a7\u00a7 5-201 \u2013 5-210 Subtitle 3 Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Dwellings \u00a7\u00a7 5-301 \u2013 5-305 Subtitle 4 Lead-Based Paint \u00a7\u00a7 5-401 \u2013 5-408 Subtitle 5 Bodily Wastes \u00a7\u00a7 5-501 \u2013 5-510 Subtitle 6 Mosquitoes \u00a7\u00a7 5-601 \u2013 5-611 Subtitle 7 Weeds \u00a7\u00a7 5-701 \u2013 5-709 Subtitle 8 [Reserved] Subtitle 9 Miscellaneous Requirements \u00a7\u00a7 5-901 \u2013 5-910", "num": "5", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5", "title": "Title 5 Nuisance Control", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7 5-101. Definitions. \u00a7 5-102. Commissioner's duties.", "num": "1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|1", "title": "Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-101", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-101. Definitions. (a) In general. In this title, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Nuisance. \"Nuisance\" includes any of the following, whether or not otherwise regulated by federal, state, or local law: (1) garbage, rubbish, or other waste, as these terms are defined in Title 7 {\"Waste Control\"} of this article ; (2) vermin infestations or other unsanitary conditions; (3) water or other liquid leaks or spills; or (4) any other health or safety hazard. (c) Person in charge. \"Person in charge\", when used with reference to any property, means all persons who own that property or who have possession, charge, care, or control of that property, whether as a personal representative, trustee, guardian, agent, or otherwise, and whether alone or jointly with any other person. (d) Property. (1) \"Property\" means all forms of real and personal property and possessions, (2) \"Property\" includes lots, structures, streets, wharves, docks, and other places. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7112(1st cl.)", "num": "5-101", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|1|5-101", "title": "\u00a7 5-101. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-101"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-102", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-102. Commissioner's duties. The Commissioner of Health is responsible for: (1) inspecting all property in the City, as the Commissioner considers necessary or appropriate to discover nuisances; and (2) requiring the removal of all nuisances so discovered. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7101, 102(1st cl.)", "num": "5-102", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|1|5-102", "title": "\u00a7 5-102. Commissioner's duties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-102"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/2", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 2 Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Generally \u00a7 5-201. Responsibility of persons in charge. \u00a7 5-202. Notice to persons in charge. \u00a7 5-203. Contents of notice. \u00a7 5-204. Service of notice. \u00a7 5-205. Abatement by Commissioner. \u00a7 5-206. Costs and expenses. 5-207 to 5-208. {Reserved} \u00a7 5-209. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 5-210. Penalties: $1,000.", "num": "2", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2", "title": "Subtitle 2 Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Generally", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/2"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-201", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-201. Responsibility of persons in charge. Every person in charge of any property is obligated, jointly and severally with all other persons in charge of that property, to: (1) remove or abate all nuisances on or originating from the property; and (2) comply with all health- or safety-related laws that affect the property. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7112(3rd cl.)", "num": "5-201", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2|5-201", "title": "\u00a7 5-201. Responsibility of persons in charge.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-201"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-202", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-202. Notice to persons in charge. Whenever the Commissioner of Health discovers a condition that the Commissioner considers to be a nuisance or potential nuisance, the Commissioner may issue a written notice to one or more of the following persons or their respective agents: (1) the person in charge of the property on which the condition exists; (2) the person in charge of the property from which the condition originates; and (3) the person in charge of any property that fronts on the street in which the condition exists. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7102(2nd cl.), 108(1st cl.(part))", "num": "5-202", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2|5-202", "title": "\u00a7 5-202. Notice to persons in charge.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-202"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-203", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-203. Contents of notice. A notice issued under this subtitle must: (1) identify the property and the condition; (2) specify the law being violated; (3) specify the corrective action to be taken; (4) state the time within which that action must be taken; and (5) state that, if corrective action is not timely taken: (i) the Commissioner may do the needed work; and (ii) all costs and expenses will be a lien on the property. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7102(2nd cl.)", "num": "5-203", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2|5-203", "title": "\u00a7 5-203. Contents of notice.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-203"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-204", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-204. Service of notice. (a) In general. A notice issued under this subtitle may be served by personal service or by certified mail, return receipt requested. (b) Posting or publication. If the person to be served cannot be found, the Commissioner may: (1) post the notice on the property; or (2) publish the notice in a newspaper of general circulation in the City. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "5-204", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2|5-204", "title": "\u00a7 5-204. Service of notice.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-204"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-205", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-205. Abatement by Commissioner. If the condition is not corrected within the time specified in the notice, the Commissioner may proceed to remove or abate the condition at the expense of the person in charge of the property. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7102(3rd cl.), 110(3rd cl.), 114", "num": "5-205", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2|5-205", "title": "\u00a7 5-205. Abatement by Commissioner.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-205"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-206", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-206. Costs and expenses. (a) Person in charge liable. The person in charge is liable to the City for all costs and expenses incurred in: (1) removing or abating the condition identified in the notice; and (2) posting or publishing notice under \u00a7 5-204 {\"Service of notice\"} of this subtitle . (b) Costs and expenses as lien. Until paid in full, these costs and expenses are a lien on the entire property from which the condition was removed or abated. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7102(4th cl.), 110(3rd cl.), 111", "num": "5-206", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2|5-206", "title": "\u00a7 5-206. Costs and expenses.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-206"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-207to5-208", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 5-207 to 5-208. {Reserved}", "num": "5-207 to 5-208", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2|5-207to5-208", "title": "5-207 to 5-208. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-207to5-208"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-209", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-209. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of an environmental citation as authorized by City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of an environmental citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "5-209", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2|5-209", "title": "\u00a7 5-209. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-209"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-210", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-210. Penalties: $1,000. Any person in charge who refuses, neglects, or otherwise fails to comply with a notice issued under this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7102(5th cl.)", "num": "5-210", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|2|5-210", "title": "\u00a7 5-210. Penalties: $1,000.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-210"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/3", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 3 Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Dwellings \u00a7 5-301. Definitions. \u00a7 5-302. Scope of subtitle. \u00a7 5-303. Enforcement of Maintenance Code. \u00a7 5-304. Right of entry. \u00a7 5-305. Inspector's testimony. 5-306. {Repealed by Ord. 02-475.}", "num": "3", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|3", "title": "Subtitle 3 Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Dwellings", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/3"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-301", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-301. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Dwelling. (1) \"Dwelling\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 202.2 of the Baltimore City Building Code . (2) \"Dwelling\" includes any dwelling unit or rooming unit. (c) Dwelling unit. \"Dwelling unit\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 202.2 of the Baltimore City Building Code . (d) Rooming unit \"Rooming unit\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 202.2 of the Baltimore City Property Maintenance Code . (Ord. 15-427; Ord. 15-426; Ord. 13-093; Ord. 07-552; Ord. 02-475; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7105(f)", "num": "5-301", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|3|5-301", "title": "\u00a7 5-301. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-301"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-302", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-302. Scope of subtitle. This subtitle applies only to dwellings and does not in any way modify or affect the authority of the Commissioner of Health to enter and inspect any building, structure, or premises other than a dwelling. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7105(e)", "num": "5-302", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|3|5-302", "title": "\u00a7 5-302. Scope of subtitle.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-302"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-303", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-303. Enforcement of Maintenance Code. The Commissioner may: (1) enforce all provisions of the Baltimore City Property Maintenance Code ; and (2) issue violation notices and orders under that Code to: (i) abate, remove, or otherwise deal with nuisances and emergencies affecting the public health; and (ii) maintain and augment public health programs, such as rodent control, lead paint poisoning prevention, environmental sanitation, and housing hygiene. (Ord. 13-093; Ord. 02-475; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7104(1st cl.)", "num": "5-303", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|3|5-303", "title": "\u00a7 5-303. Enforcement of Maintenance Code.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-303"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-304", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-304. Right of entry. (a) In general. Whenever the Commissioner has reason to believe that a nuisance exists in any dwelling, the Commissioner has the same authority to enter and inspect that dwelling as does the Commissioner of Housing and Community Development. (b) Procedures. The entry and inspection of a dwelling must comply with the requirements of \u00a7 2-107 {\"Right of entry\"} of this article . (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7105(a)", "num": "5-304", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|3|5-304", "title": "\u00a7 5-304. Right of entry.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-304"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-305", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-305. Inspector's testimony. (a) \"Related law\" defined. In this section, \"related law\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 104.6.2 {\"Authority to seek warrant\"} of the Baltimore City Building Code . (b) Limit on testimony. No inspector who gains entrance to a dwelling for the purpose of investigating a nuisance may obtain or furnish evidence of, or testify to, any offense other than: (1) a violation of this article or of any related law; (2) a felony; or (3) a misdemeanor involving an act of violence that is committed in the inspector's presence. (Ord. 13-093; Ord. 02-475; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7105(d)(1st sen.)", "num": "5-305", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|3|5-305", "title": "\u00a7 5-305. Inspector's testimony.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-305"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-306", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 5-306. {Repealed by Ord. 02-475.}", "num": "5-306", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|3|5-306", "title": "5-306. {Repealed by Ord. 02-475.}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-306"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/4", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 4 Lead-Based Paint \u00a7 5-401. Definitions. \u00a7 5-402. Warning label required. \u00a7 5-403. Placement. \u00a7 5-404. Labels not to indicate interior use. 5-405 to 5-406. {Reserved} \u00a7 5-407. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 5-408. Penalties: $1,000.", "num": "4", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|4", "title": "Subtitle 4 Lead-Based Paint", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/4"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-401", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-401. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Lead-based paint. \"Lead-based paint\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 6-801(j) of the State Environment Article. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a772(part)", "num": "5-401", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|4|5-401", "title": "\u00a7 5-401. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-401"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-402", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-402. Warning label required. No person may possess, offer for sale, sell, or give away in the City any lead-based paint unless it bears the following or substantially equivalent warning: \"Warning \u2014 Contains Lead. Harmful if Eaten. Do not apply on any interior surfaces of a dwelling, or of a place used for the care of children, or on window sills, toys, cribs, or other furniture.\" (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a772", "num": "5-402", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|4|5-402", "title": "\u00a7 5-402. Warning label required.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-402"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-403", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-403. Placement. The warning required by this subtitle must: (1) appear in a conspicuous place on the paint's immediate container; (2) form an integral part of the printed label on that container; and (3) be printed in letters that are legible and that conspicuously contrast with any other printing on the container. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a773, 74(2nd sen.)", "num": "5-403", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|4|5-403", "title": "\u00a7 5-403. Placement.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-403"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-404", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-404. Labels not to indicate interior use. No label on a container of lead-based paint may indicate in any way that the product is suitable for interior surfaces of dwellings or for interior surfaces of any places used for the care of children. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a775", "num": "5-404", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|4|5-404", "title": "\u00a7 5-404. Labels not to indicate interior use.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-404"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-405to5-406", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 5-405 to 5-406. {Reserved}", "num": "5-405 to 5-406", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|4|5-405to5-406", "title": "5-405 to 5-406. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-405to5-406"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-407", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-407. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of an environmental citation as authorized by City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of an environmental citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "5-407", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|4|5-407", "title": "\u00a7 5-407. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-407"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-408", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-408. Penalties: $1,000. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a777", "num": "5-408", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|4|5-408", "title": "\u00a7 5-408. Penalties: $1,000.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-408"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/5", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 5 Bodily Wastes \u00a7 5-501. Spitting in public places. 5-502. {Reserved} \u00a7 5-503. Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 in general. \u00a7 5-504. Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 parents or guardians of minors. 5-505 to 5-506. {Reserved} \u00a7 5-507. Minors urinating or defecating in public. 5-508. {Reserved} \u00a7 5-509. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 5-510. Penalties: Varied.", "num": "5", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5", "title": "Subtitle 5 Bodily Wastes", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/5"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-501", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-501. Spitting in public places. No person may spit on: (1) any footpath or sidewalk of any public street or public square; (2) the floor or anywhere else in any public building under the control of the Mayor and City Council of Baltimore; (3) the floor, platform, or steps of any railroad, bus, or other common carrier or of any depot or station; (4) the floor or steps of any theater, store, factory, or other building that is used in common by the public; (5) the floor of any hall or office used in common by the guests of a hotel or lodging house. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7138", "num": "5-501", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5|5-501", "title": "\u00a7 5-501. Spitting in public places.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-501"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-502", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 5-502. {Reserved}", "num": "5-502", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5|5-502", "title": "5-502. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-502"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-503", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-503. Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 in general. No person may urinate or defecate on or about: (1) any public place, way, or park; or (2) the mall or adjacent parking area of any shopping center. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7171A(a)(1st cl.)", "num": "5-503", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5|5-503", "title": "\u00a7 5-503. Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 in general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-503"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-504", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-504. Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 parents or guardians of minors. A parent or guardian may not knowingly permit a minor for whom the parent or guardian is responsible to violate \u00a7 5-503 {\"Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 in general\"} of this subtitle . (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7171A(b)(3)(i)", "num": "5-504", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5|5-504", "title": "\u00a7 5-504. Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 parents or guardians of minors.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-504"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-505to5-506", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 5-505 to 5-506. {Reserved}", "num": "5-505 to 5-506", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5|5-505to5-506", "title": "5-505 to 5-506. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-505to5-506"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-507", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-507. Minors urinating or defecating in public. (a) Order to stop. A police officer who finds any minor violating \u00a7 5-503 {\"Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 in general\"} of this subtitle must order the minor to stop the violation. (b) Failure to comply. (1) If the minor fails to comply with the order, the police officer may take him or her into custody. (2) After the information needed to carry out the purposes of this subtitle has been recorded, the minor must be: (i) promptly released to his or her parent or guardian, with written notice to the parent or guardian of the violation; and (ii) referred to the Baltimore City Police Department's court sanctioned pre-intake adjustment program. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7171A(b)(1), (2)", "num": "5-507", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5|5-507", "title": "\u00a7 5-507. Minors urinating or defecating in public.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-507"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-508", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 5-508. {Reserved}", "num": "5-508", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5|5-508", "title": "5-508. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-508"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-509", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-509. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 03-595; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "5-509", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5|5-509", "title": "\u00a7 5-509. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-509"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-510", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-510. Penalties: Varied. (a) Spitting: $25/ $50. Any person who violates any provision of \u00a7 5-501 {\"Spitting in public places\"} of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to: (1) for a first offense, a fine of not more than $25; and (2) for a second and any subsequent offense, a fine of not more than $50. (b) Urinating or defecating \u2014 adult offenders: $500 and 30 days. Any adult who violates \u00a7 5-503 {\"Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 in general\"} of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $1,000 or to imprisonment for not more than 30 days or to both fine and imprisonment for each offense. (c) Urinating or defecating \u2014 parent or guardian of minor: $200. Any parent or guardian who violates \u00a7 5-504 {\"Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 parents or guardians of minors\"} of this subtitle within 12 months after receiving written notice of the minor's having violated \u00a7 5-504 {\"Urinating or defecating in public places \u2014 in general\"} of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $200 for each offense. (Ord. 10-340; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7140, art. 19, \u00a7171A(b)(3)(ii), (5)", "num": "5-510", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|5|5-510", "title": "\u00a7 5-510. Penalties: Varied.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-510"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/6", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 6 Mosquitoes \u00a7 5-601. Definitions. \u00a7 5-602. Purpose of subtitle. \u00a7 5-603. Responsibility of persons in charge. \u00a7 5-604. Collections prohibited unless treated. \u00a7 5-605. Methods of treatment. \u00a7 5-606. Prima facie evidence of breeding. \u00a7 5-607. Notice to person in charge. \u00a7 5-608. Abatement by Commissioner. 5-609 to 5-610. {Reserved} \u00a7 5-611. Penalties: $500.", "num": "6", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6", "title": "Subtitle 6 Mosquitoes", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/6"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-601", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-601. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Collection of water. (1) \"Collection of water\" means any collection of standing or running water. (2) \"Collection of water\" includes water contained in any ditch, roof or other gutter, pond, pool, excavation, depression, fountain, tank, well, barrel, tire, urn, can, box, bottle, tub, bucket, or other similar container. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a794", "num": "5-601", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-601", "title": "\u00a7 5-601. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-601"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-602", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-602. Purpose of subtitle. The purpose of this subtitle is to get rid of the discomfort, annoyance, and health hazards that are caused by mosquitoes. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a793(1st cl.)", "num": "5-602", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-602", "title": "\u00a7 5-602. Purpose of subtitle.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-602"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-603", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-603. Responsibility of persons in charge. Every person in charge of any property is obligated, jointly and severally, to comply with the requirements of this subtitle. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a793( 2nd par.( 2nd cl.))", "num": "5-603", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-603", "title": "\u00a7 5-603. Responsibility of persons in charge.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-603"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-604", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-604. Collections prohibited unless treated. No person in charge may keep or permit to be kept any collection of water in which mosquitoes are likely to breed, unless the collection of water is treated to effectively prevent breeding. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a793(3rd par.)", "num": "5-604", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-604", "title": "\u00a7 5-604. Collections prohibited unless treated.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-604"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-605", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-605. Methods of treatment. The method for treating a collection of water to prevent mosquitoes from breeding must be one or more of the following: (1) emptying all water at least once every 5 days; (2) using a larvicide approved by and applied in accordance with the requirements of the United States Environmental Protection Agency; (3) cleaning and keeping the water free of vegetable growth and other obstructions and stocking with mosquito-destroying fish (the absence of half-grown or larger mosquito larvae to be evidence of having complied with this method); (4) filling or draining to the satisfaction of the Commissioner of Health; (5) removing, at least once every 5 days, all tin cans, tin boxes, broken or empty bottles, and similar articles likely to hold water; (6) screening with wire netting of at least 16 meshes to the inch each way; (7) screening with any other material that will prevent the ingress or egress of mosquitoes; or (8) any other method that the Commissioner approves. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a795", "num": "5-605", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-605", "title": "\u00a7 5-605. Methods of treatment.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-605"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-606", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-606. Prima facie evidence of breeding. (a) In general. The natural presence of mosquito larvae in standing or running water is evidence that mosquitoes are breeding there. (b) Required action. Within 5 days of notice from the Commissioner, the person in charge of the premises must act to prevent further breeding. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a796", "num": "5-606", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-606", "title": "\u00a7 5-606. Prima facie evidence of breeding.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-606"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-607", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-607. Notice to person in charge. Whenever the Commissioner discovers a condition that could give rise to the breeding of mosquitoes, the Commissioner must issue a written notice to the person in charge, as provided in Subtitle 2 {\"Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Generally\"} of this title. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a797(part)", "num": "5-607", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-607", "title": "\u00a7 5-607. Notice to person in charge.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-607"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-608", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-608. Abatement by Commissioner. (a) In general. If the condition is not corrected within 10 days of the notice, the Commissioner may proceed to correct the condition at the expense of the person in charge. (b) Costs and expenses. All costs and expenses incurred by the Commissioner are a lien on the property as provided in Subtitle 2 {\"Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Generally\"} of this title. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a797", "num": "5-608", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-608", "title": "\u00a7 5-608. Abatement by Commissioner.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-608"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-609to5-610", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 5-609 to 5-610. {Reserved}", "num": "5-609 to 5-610", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-609to5-610", "title": "5-609 to 5-610. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-609to5-610"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-611", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-611. Penalties: $500. (a) In general. Any person who refuses, neglects, or otherwise fails to comply with a notice issued under this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $500 for each offense. (b) Each day a separate offense. Each day that a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a798(2nd, 3rd cls.), 100", "num": "5-611", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|6|5-611", "title": "\u00a7 5-611. Penalties: $500.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-611"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/7", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 7 Weeds \u00a7 5-701. Responsibility of persons in charge. \u00a7 5-702. Cutting grass, weeds, etc., required. \u00a7 5-703. Destroying noxious weeds, etc., required. \u00a7 5-704. Notice to person in charge. \u00a7 5-705. Abatement by Public Works. 5-706 to 5-707. {Reserved} \u00a7 5-708. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 5-709. Penalties.", "num": "7", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|7", "title": "Subtitle 7 Weeds", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/7"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-701", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-701. Responsibility of persons in charge. Every person in charge of any property is obligated, jointly and severally, to comply with the requirements of this subtitle. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "5-701", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|7|5-701", "title": "\u00a7 5-701. Responsibility of persons in charge.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-701"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-702", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-702. Cutting grass, weeds, etc., required. No person in charge of any land may allow: (1) any grass, weeds, or other rank vegetation on that land to reach a height of 8 inches or more; or (2) any grass, weeds, or other rank vegetation on an abutting sidewalk, gutter, or alley to reach a height of 4 inches or more. (Ord. 15-427; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7161(1st cl.)", "num": "5-702", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|7|5-702", "title": "\u00a7 5-702. Cutting grass, weeds, etc., required.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-702"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-703", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-703. Destroying noxious weeds, etc., required. (a) In general. Every person in charge of any land must keep that land free from ragweeds, wild mustard, wild lettuce, wild parsley, common thistle, milkweed, poison ivy, and all other noxious weeds. (b) Permitted methods. The person in charge must destroy any noxious weeds on the land by: (1) spraying with a chemical compound approved by and applied in accordance with the requirements of the United States Environmental Protection Agency; (2) cutting and removal; (3) digging under; or (4) any other method that the Commissioner approves. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7161(2nd cl.)", "num": "5-703", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|7|5-703", "title": "\u00a7 5-703. Destroying noxious weeds, etc., required.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-703"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-704", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-704. Notice to person in charge. Whenever the Commissioner of Health discovers a violation of this subtitle, the Commissioner may issue a written notice to the person in charge, as provided in Subtitle 2 {\"Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Generally\"} of this title. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7162(1st sen.), 163", "num": "5-704", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|7|5-704", "title": "\u00a7 5-704. Notice to person in charge.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-704"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-705", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-705. Abatement by Public Works. (a) In general. (1) If the condition is not corrected within 10 days of the notice, the Commissioner may notify the Director of Public Works, who must proceed to correct the condition at the expense of the person in charge. (2) Grass, weeds, and other rank vegetation must be cut to a height of not more than 3 inches. (b) Costs and expenses. All costs and expenses incurred by the Director of Public Works are a lien on the property as provided in Subtitle 2 {\"Nuisance Abatement \u2014 Generally\"} of this title. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7162(2nd sen.)", "num": "5-705", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|7|5-705", "title": "\u00a7 5-705. Abatement by Public Works.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-705"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-706to5-707", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 5-706 to 5-707. {Reserved}", "num": "5-706 to 5-707", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|7|5-706to5-707", "title": "5-706 to 5-707. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-706to5-707"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-708", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-708. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of an environmental citation as authorized by City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of an environmental citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "5-708", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|7|5-708", "title": "\u00a7 5-708. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-708"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-709", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-709. Penalties. (a) In general. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to the penalties specified in this section. (b) Basic penalty: $500. Except as specified in subsection (c) of this section, the penalty for a violation is a fine of not more than $500 for each offense. (c) Violation after notice: $1,000. For a person who receives a notice under \u00a75-704 {\"Notice to person in charge\"} of this subtitle and fails to comply with that notice, the penalty is a fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7161(2nd par.)", "num": "5-709", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|7|5-709", "title": "\u00a7 5-709. Penalties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-709"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/8", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 8 [Reserved]", "num": "8", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|8", "title": "Subtitle 8 [Reserved]", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/8"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/9", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 9 Miscellaneous Requirements \u00a7 5-901. Cleaning sidewalks and gutters. \u00a7 5-902. Keeping offensive materials on property. \u00a7 5-903. Livestock. \u00a7 5-904. Deposit on streets, etc. prohibited. \u00a7 5-905. Plastic bags. \u00a7 5-906. Abandoned refrigerators, etc. 5-907 to 5-909. {Reserved} \u00a7 5-910. Penalties.", "num": "9", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|9", "title": "Subtitle 9 Miscellaneous Requirements", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5/9"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-901", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-901. Cleaning sidewalks and gutters. The person in charge of any premises or lot must keep the sidewalk and gutters bounding on the premises or lot open and free from waste or obstructions and clean at all times. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7118, art. 19, \u00a7175", "num": "5-901", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|9|5-901", "title": "\u00a7 5-901. Cleaning sidewalks and gutters.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-901"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-902", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-902. Keeping offensive materials on property. (a) In general. No person may use or keep on that person's property any liquid or solid matter that is or that, after exposure to the atmosphere or otherwise, is likely to become offensive or otherwise a nuisance. (b) Illustrations. This section applies to, among other things, any: (1) blood; (2) refuse coal oil; (3) dead animal or part of an animal; (4) domestic or sanitary sewage; (5) excrement; (6) filth; (7) foul or nauseous liquid; (8) garbage; (9) slaughter house or other trade cleanings; (10) stagnant water; or (11) other offensive matter of any kind. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7119, 120, 123, 125", "num": "5-902", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|9|5-902", "title": "\u00a7 5-902. Keeping offensive materials on property.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-902"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-903", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-903. Livestock. Every person who keeps a cow, horse, or other livestock in the City must keep the animal and its stable or other housing in such a way that no filth or stench becomes offensive to or annoys any neighbor or other person. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7122", "num": "5-903", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|9|5-903", "title": "\u00a7 5-903. Livestock.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-903"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-904", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-904. Deposit on streets, etc. prohibited. No person may place or cause or allow to be placed on any street any noxious or objectionable matter that from its acid content or otherwise might be injurious or harmful to any person or to the clothing, household furnishings, or personal effects of any person. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7121(1st sen.)", "num": "5-904", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|9|5-904", "title": "\u00a7 5-904. Deposit on streets, etc. prohibited.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-904"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-905", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-905. Plastic bags. (a) \"Plastic bag\" defined. \"Plastic bag\" means any bag that is: (1) intended for household use or for packaging garments or other articles intended for household use; (2) larger than 6 inches in diameter at the open end; and (3) made of film less than 1 mil (0.001 inch) thick. (b) Printed warning required. No person may package, deliver, or sell in a plastic bag any article for use in or around the household or sell or distribute any plastic bag for use in or around the household unless the bag bears a warning as required by this section. (c) Text of warning. The warning must warn the consumer against the hazard of suffocation by children in the following or substantially equivalent wording: \"Warning: To avoid danger of suffocation, keep away from babies and children. Do not use in cribs, beds, carriages, or playpens. This bag is not a toy.\" (d) Placement and size. (1) The warning must be printed on, attached to, or accompany each plastic bag or each package of plastic bags delivered to the consumer. (2) The warning must be prominently and conspicuously displayed in bold face type, in accordance with the following table: Combined length and width of bag Minimum Size of Lettering Less than 30 inches 10 points 30 inches or more, but less than 40 inches 14 points 40 inches or more, but less than 60 inches 18 points 60 inches or more 24 points (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7118", "num": "5-905", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|9|5-905", "title": "\u00a7 5-905. Plastic bags.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-905"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-906", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-906. Abandoned refrigerators, etc. No person may place or permit anyone else to place in any location that is outside of a dwelling or other building and accessible to children any abandoned, unattended, or uncrated ice box, refrigerator, or freezer cabinet equipped with a door or lock that cannot be opened from the inside. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a733(1st sen.)", "num": "5-906", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|9|5-906", "title": "\u00a7 5-906. Abandoned refrigerators, etc.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-906"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-907to5-909", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 5-907 to 5-909. {Reserved}", "num": "5-907 to 5-909", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|9|5-907to5-909", "title": "5-907 to 5-909. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-907to5-909"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-910", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 5-910. Penalties. (a) In general. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to the penalties specified in this section. (b) Basic penalty: $500. Except as otherwise specified in this section, the penalty for a violation is a fine of not more than $500 for each offense. (c) Violation after notice: $1,000. For a person who receives a notice under Subtitle 2 of this title to correct a violation of any provision of this subtitle and fails to comply with that notice, the penalty is a fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense. (d) Abandoned refrigerators: $500 and 30 days. (1) For a person who violates any provision of \u00a7 5-906 {\"Abandoned refrigerators, etc.\"} of this subtitle , the penalty is a fine of not more than $500 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days or both fine and imprisonment for each offense. (2) Each day that a violation of \u00a7 5-906 continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a733(2nd, 3rd sens.)", "num": "5-910", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|5|9|5-910", "title": "\u00a7 5-910. Penalties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/5-910"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Title 6 Food Service Facilities Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 6-101 \u2013 6-105 Subtitle 2 License Required \u00a7\u00a7 6-201 \u2013 6-208 Subtitle 3 Food Service Managers \u00a7\u00a7 6-301 \u2013 6-306 Subtitle 4 Fees \u00a7\u00a7 6-402 \u2013 6-408 Subtitle 5 Miscellaneous Regulations \u00a7\u00a7 6-501 \u2013 6-509 Subtitle 6 Suspensions and Revocations \u00a7\u00a7 6-601 \u2013 6-606 Subtitle 7 [Reserved] Subtitle 8 Penalties \u00a7\u00a7 6-801 \u2013 6-802", "num": "6", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6", "title": "Title 6 Food Service Facilities", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7 6-101. Definitions. \u00a7 6-102. Commissioner's duties. \u00a7 6-103. Rules and regulations. \u00a7 6-104. Inspections; samples. \u00a7 6-105. Confiscation of food.", "num": "1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|1", "title": "Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-101", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-101. Definitions. (a) In general. In this title, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Excluded organization. \"Excluded organization\" has the meaning stated in COMAR 10.15.03.02B(28). (c) Food. (1) In general. \"Food\" means any natural or artificial substance or ingredient, whether raw, cooked, or processed, that is used or sold or intended for use or sale, in whole or in part, for human consumption. (2) Inclusions. \"Food\" includes: (i) ice; (ii) beverages; and (iii) chewing gum or any substance used as a component of chewing gum. (3) Exclusions. \"Food\" does not include any: (i) alcoholic beverage, as defined in State Code Article 2 B, \u00a7 1-102(a)(2); or (ii) drug, as defined in \u00a7 21-101(g) of the State Health-General Article. (d) Food service facility. \"Food service facility\" has the meaning stated in COMAR 10.15.03.02B(34). (e) Food service manager. \"Food service manager\" means an individual designated by a food service facility to exercise operational supervision of the facility. (f) License. \"License\" means a license issued under this title to operate a food service facility. (g) Person. (1) In general. \"Person\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 1-107(a) {\"Person: In general\"} of the City Code's General Provisions Article . (2) Qualified inclusion of governmental entities. Notwithstanding \u00a7 1-107(b) {\"Person: Exclusion\"} of the General Provisions Article , in this title \"person\" also includes, except as used in \u00a7 6-802 {\"Penalties\"} of this title , a governmental entity or an instrumentality or unit of a governmental entity. (Ord. 22-125; Ord. 14-308; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a764(1st, 2nd cls.), 165", "num": "6-101", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|1|6-101", "title": "\u00a7 6-101. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-101"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-102", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-102. Commissioner's duties. The Commissioner of Health is responsible for: (1) inspecting, regulating, and licensing food service facilities; (2) obtaining food samples and testing their qualities by chemical or microscopical examination as the Commissioner considers necessary or appropriate; and (3) generally enforcing this title and any applicable State regulations. (Ord. 14-308; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a761(1st sen.)", "num": "6-102", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|1|6-102", "title": "\u00a7 6-102. Commissioner's duties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-102"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-103", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-103. Rules and regulations. In the rules and regulations adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article , the Commissioner, consistent with this title and State law: (1) must designate the classes of licenses to be issued for various types of food service facilities; and (2) may include provisions that govern the construction, design, operation, and maintenance of food service facilities. (Ord. 06-185; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a761(2nd sen.)", "num": "6-103", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|1|6-103", "title": "\u00a7 6-103. Rules and regulations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-103"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-104", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-104. Inspections; samples. (a) Inspections. The Commissioner, all other officers of the Department, and any inspector or police officer authorized by the Commissioner may, at all reasonable hours, enter, have full access to, and inspect: (1) any structure, premises, or vehicle in which food is or is believed to be prepared, manufactured, processed, stored, packaged, handled, or distributed; and (2) any railroad car, truck, wagon, or other vehicle that is or is believed to be used for the conveyance or delivery of food. (b) Samples. During any inspection, the Commissioner, officer, inspector, or police officer may take food samples of up to 1 pound of each item for inspection, testing, or analysis. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a763, 165(j)", "num": "6-104", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|1|6-104", "title": "\u00a7 6-104. Inspections; samples.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-104"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-105", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-105. Confiscation of food. (a) In general. The Commissioner may condemn, confiscate, and either destroy or denature any food that: (1) fails to comply with any requirement of this title; or (2) is possessed by any person who is violating any provision of this title. (b) Prohibited conduct. No person may hide, remove, or assist in hiding or removing any food that has been condemned by the Commissioner. (c) Scope of section. Nothing in this section imposes on the Commissioner the duty or expense of confiscating any food condemned by the Commissioner. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a760, 65(2nd cl.)", "num": "6-105", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|1|6-105", "title": "\u00a7 6-105. Confiscation of food.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-105"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/2", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 2 License Required \u00a7 6-201. In general. \u00a7 6-202. Applications. \u00a7 6-203. Inspection and priority assessment of facility. \u00a7 6-204. Issuance of license. \u00a7 6-205. Scope of license. \u00a7 6-206. Material alterations. \u00a7 6-207. Term and renewal of license. \u00a7 6-208. Provisional licenses.", "num": "2", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|2", "title": "Subtitle 2 License Required", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/2"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-201", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-201. In general. (a) Operation without license prohibited. Except as otherwise provided in this section, no person may operate a food service facility without a license to do so from the Commissioner of Health. (b) Exception for certain excluded organizations. A food service facility may operate without a license issued by the Commissioner of Health if it: (i) is operated by an excluded organization; and (ii) has, in accordance with COMAR 10.15.03.26A, elected to operate without a license and in compliance with the minimum food safety requirements of COMAR 10.15.03.26B-E. (c) License not transferable. A license may not be transferred from place to place or person to person. (Ord. 14-308; Ord. 06-185; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(b)(1)(1st sen.)", "num": "6-201", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|2|6-201", "title": "\u00a7 6-201. In general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-201"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-202", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-202. Applications. (a) In general. An applicant for a license must: (1) submit an application on the form that the Commissioner provides; and (2) pay the applicable processing fees. (b) Contents. In addition to any other information that the Commissioner requires, the application must contain building and installation plans and specifications. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(c)(1st, 2nd, 4th(part) sens.)", "num": "6-202", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|2|6-202", "title": "\u00a7 6-202. Applications.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-202"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-203", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-203. Inspection and priority assessment of facility. (a) Inspection. On receipt of an application, the Commissioner must inspect the food service facility to determine compliance with all applicable laws, rules, and regulations. (b) Priority assessment. To determine the degree of risk that a food service facility poses for a food-borne disease occurrence, the Commissioner must assess each food service facility and classify it in one of the following priority assessment categories, as defined in COMAR 10.15.03.33: (1) high priority facilities, which are at high risk for a food-borne disease occurrence; (2) moderate priority facilities, which are at moderate risk for a food-borne disease occurrence; and (3) low priority facilities, which are at low risk for a food-borne disease occurrence. (Ord. 14-308; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(a)5, (c)(3rd sen.)", "num": "6-203", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|2|6-203", "title": "\u00a7 6-203. Inspection and priority assessment of facility.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-203"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-204", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-204. Issuance of license. The Commissioner must issue a license to the applicant if: (1) the requirements of all applicable laws, rules, and regulations have been met; and (2) the applicant pays the applicable annual fee. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(c)(4th sen.)", "num": "6-204", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|2|6-204", "title": "\u00a7 6-204. Issuance of license.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-204"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-205", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-205. Scope of license. While a license is in effect, it authorizes the operation of the food service facility in accordance with the approved plans and specifications. (Ord. 06-185; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(b)(1)(2nd sen.(last cl.))", "num": "6-205", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|2|6-205", "title": "\u00a7 6-205. Scope of license.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-205"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-206", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-206. Material alterations. (a) Permit required. No person may materially alter a food service facility (e.g., install new or additional equipment, make structural changes, or change the type of operation) without a permit to do so from the Commissioner. (b) Application. The application for a permit under this section must be: (1) submitted on the form that the Commissioner provides; (2) contain the information that the Commissioner requires; and (3) be accompanied by the applicable permit fee. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(c)(parts)", "num": "6-206", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|2|6-206", "title": "\u00a7 6-206. Material alterations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-206"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-207", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-207. Term and renewal of license. (a) Term of license. Unless sooner suspended or revoked, a license expires on the 1 st anniversary of its effective date. (b) Application for renewal. Before a license expires, the licensee periodically may renew it for an additional 1-year term, if the license holder: (1) otherwise is entitled to a license; (2) submits a renewal application to the Commissioner, on the form that the Commissioner provides; and (3) pays the applicable annual fee. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(b)(1)(2nd sen.(1st cl.)), (d)(5)(part)", "num": "6-207", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|2|6-207", "title": "\u00a7 6-207. Term and renewal of license.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-207"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-208", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-208. Provisional licenses. (a) In general. If a licensed food service facility is sold or otherwise transferred to a new owner, the new owner may apply for a short-term, provisional license to permit the continued operation of the facility pending the Commissioner's inspection of the facility. (b) Term and renewal. Unless sooner suspended or revoked: (1) the term of a provisional license is 60 days; and (2) the license may be renewed for one additional 60-day term. (Ord. 06-185.)", "num": "6-208", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|2|6-208", "title": "\u00a7 6-208. Provisional licenses.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-208"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/3", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 3 Food Service Managers \u00a7 6-301. Certain facilities to employ manager. \u00a7 6-302. Managers to be certified. \u00a7 6-303. Certification requirements. \u00a7 6-304. Issuance to individuals only. \u00a7 6-305. Term. \u00a7 6-306. Instructors to be certified.", "num": "3", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|3", "title": "Subtitle 3 Food Service Managers", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/3"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-301", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-301. Certain facilities to employ manager. Each high priority or moderate priority food service facility must employ a food service manager. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(a)2b(part)", "num": "6-301", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|3|6-301", "title": "\u00a7 6-301. Certain facilities to employ manager.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-301"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-302", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-302. Managers to be certified. A food service manager who is employed by a high priority or moderate priority food service facility must, within 90 days of becoming employed by the facility, be certified by the Commissioner of Health in accordance with this subtitle. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(b)(2), (b-1)(1)", "num": "6-302", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|3|6-302", "title": "\u00a7 6-302. Managers to be certified.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-302"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-303", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-303. Certification requirements. (a) Commissioner to adopt. In the rules and regulations adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article , the Commissioner must include rules and regulations for: (1) the certification and recertification of food service managers; and (2) the denial, suspension, and revocation of certifications. (b) Required provisions. These rules and regulations must include: (1) minimum requirements for instruction and examination in food-borne disease prevention, sanitation, hygiene, and food service management; and (2) reciprocity provisions for certifications granted by a political subdivision of this State that has established a food service manager certification program approved by the Commissioner. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(b-1)(2), (3) Editor's Notes Text of subsection (a) conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "6-303", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|3|6-303", "title": "\u00a7 6-303. Certification requirements.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-303"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-304", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-304. Issuance to individuals only. A food service manager certificate may only be issued in the name of an individual. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(b-1)(6)(1st cl.)", "num": "6-304", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|3|6-304", "title": "\u00a7 6-304. Issuance to individuals only.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-304"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-305", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-305. Term. Unless sooner suspended or revoked, a food service manager certificate expires on the 3 rd anniversary of its effective date. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(b-1)(6)(2nd cl.)", "num": "6-305", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|3|6-305", "title": "\u00a7 6-305. Term.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-305"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-306", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-306. Instructors to be certified. Instructors of food service managers must be certified in the same manner as food service managers. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(b-1)(4)", "num": "6-306", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|3|6-306", "title": "\u00a7 6-306. Instructors to be certified.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-306"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/4", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 4 Fees 6-401. {Repealed} \u00a7 6-402. Processing fees. \u00a7 6-403. Annual license fees. \u00a7 6-404. Reduced fee for certain tax exempt entities. \u00a7 6-405. Waived fee for certain day care centers. \u00a7 6-406. Late fees. \u00a7 6-407. Manager certification fees. \u00a7 6-408. Use of fee proceeds.", "num": "4", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|4", "title": "Subtitle 4 Fees", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/4"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-401", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 6-401. {Repealed}", "num": "6-401", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|4|6-401", "title": "6-401. {Repealed}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-401"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-402", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-402. Processing fees. (a) Application fee. When applying for a license to operate a food service facility or applying for a permit to undertake material alterations, the applicant must pay an application fee to help defray the cost of processing the plans and specifications, conducting a risk assessment, reviewing hazard analysis critical control point (HACCP) plans, or undertaking an initial inspection.. (b) Reinspection fee. The applicant must pay an additional fee for each reinspection needed before the license or permit can be approved. (c) Fee amounts. The amount of these fees are as set from time to time by the Commissioner, with the approval of the City Council. (Ord. 14-308; Ord. 06-185; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(d)(4)", "num": "6-402", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|4|6-402", "title": "\u00a7 6-402. Processing fees.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-402"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-403", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-403. Annual license fees. Subject to \u00a7 6-404 {\"Reduced fee for certain tax exempt entities\"} and \u00a7 6-405 {\"Waived fee for certain day care centers\"} of this subtitle , the annual license fee for each class of food service facility is as set from time to time by the Commissioner, with the approval of the City Council. (Ord. 06-185; Ord. 00-033; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(a)12(2nd sen.), (d)(1)", "num": "6-403", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|4|6-403", "title": "\u00a7 6-403. Annual license fees.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-403"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-404", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-404. Reduced fee for certain tax exempt entities. (a) Scope of section. This section applies to an entity described in subsection (b) of this section only as long as that entity maintains tax exempt status under \u00a7 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code. (b) Maximum fee. For the following tax exempt entities, the annual license fee may not exceed $50: (1) any charitable food facility that provide meals to low-income individuals for no charge, such as soup kitchens, soup pantries, or shelter homes; (2) any youth organization whose membership is composed of minors and that operates temporary facilities for the purpose of raising funds to support the organization; and (3) any child group day care center that is licensed to provide care and supervision to school age and preschool age children. (Ord. 06-185; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(a)8 - 11, (d)(2)", "num": "6-404", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|4|6-404", "title": "\u00a7 6-404. Reduced fee for certain tax exempt entities.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-404"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-405", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-405. Waived fee for certain day care centers. As long as the Maryland Department of Human Resources continues to contract with the City Department of Health for 2 or more environmental sanitarians to inspect and monitor child group day care centers in the City, the annual license fee for those centers is waived. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(d)(3)", "num": "6-405", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|4|6-405", "title": "\u00a7 6-405. Waived fee for certain day care centers.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-405"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-406", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-406. Late fees. (a) In general. Any licensee who is required to pay a license fee and who fails to renew the license within 5 days of its expiration must pay an additional late fee for each day after the expiration date that the license remains unrenewed. (b) Amount. The amount of the late fee is as set from time to time by the Commissioner, with the approval of the City Council. (06-247; Ord. 06-185; Ord. 00-033; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(d)(5)", "num": "6-406", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|4|6-406", "title": "\u00a7 6-406. Late fees.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-406"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-407", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-407. Manager certification fees. Fees imposed for certification of food service managers are as set by the Commissioner. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(b-1)(5)", "num": "6-407", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|4|6-407", "title": "\u00a7 6-407. Manager certification fees.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-407"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-408", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-408. Use of fee proceeds. (a) In general. (1) The Commissioner must promptly account for and pay to the Director of Finance all fees received under this subtitle. (2) The Director of Finance must credit these fees to a special fund entitled \"Food Service Facilities\", to be used: (i) to cover the cost of licensing, inspecting, and regulating food service facilities; and (ii) to help defray the cost of environmental health inspection programs that affect food service facilities. (b) Accounting to Council. The Director of Finance must submit a quarterly accounting of this fund to the Chair of the Budget and Appropriations Committee of the City Council. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(d)(6), (7)", "num": "6-408", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|4|6-408", "title": "\u00a7 6-408. Use of fee proceeds.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-408"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/5", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 5 Miscellaneous Regulations \u00a7 6-501. Definitions. \u00a7 6-502. Compliance with health laws required. \u00a7 6-503. Disguising adulterated, etc., food prohibited. \u00a7 6-504. Possession, etc., of adulterated, etc., food prohibited. \u00a7 6-505. Frozen food regulations. \u00a7 6-505.1. Dated food products. \u00a7 6-506. Litter and rubbish prohibited. \u00a7 6-507. Trans fats prohibited. \u00a7 6-508. Polystyrene foam products. \u00a7 6-509. Children's meals.", "num": "5", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5", "title": "Subtitle 5 Miscellaneous Regulations", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/5"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-501", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-501. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Adulterated food. \"Adulterated food\" means any food that: (1) has been prepared, manufactured, processed, stored, packaged, handled, distributed, or sold under insanitary conditions that reasonably could be expected to have contaminated it; (2) is considered to be adulterated within the meaning of the Maryland Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act; or (3) otherwise violates the requirements of the Maryland Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act. (c) Unwholesome food. \"Unwholesome food\" means any food that: (1) has absorbed or mixed with deleterious gases, liquids, or solids; (2) is decomposed, rotten, tainted, or otherwise impure; or (3) is otherwise in any way: (i) deleterious to health; (ii) liable to introduce, cause, or increase sickness; or (iii) liable to impair or upset functions of the human body. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a764(part)", "num": "6-501", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-501", "title": "\u00a7 6-501. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-501"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-502", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-502. Compliance with health laws required. Each food service facility must comply with all applicable health laws, rules, and regulations of the federal government, the State of Maryland, and the City of Baltimore. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(e)", "num": "6-502", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-502", "title": "\u00a7 6-502. Compliance with health laws required.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-502"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-503", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-503. Disguising adulterated, etc., food prohibited. No food service facility may use any acid, drug, chemical, coloring, or other substance to disguise any adulterated or unwholesome food. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a758(1st sen.), 59(last cl.)", "num": "6-503", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-503", "title": "\u00a7 6-503. Disguising adulterated, etc., food prohibited.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-503"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-504", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-504. Possession, etc., of adulterated, etc., food prohibited. No food service facility may sell, offer for sale, transport, or have on its premises, in any vehicle, or on the premises of any other person any adulterated or unwholesome food. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a759(last cl.)", "num": "6-504", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-504", "title": "\u00a7 6-504. Possession, etc., of adulterated, etc., food prohibited.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-504"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-505", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-505. Frozen food regulations. (a) Definitions. (1) In general. In this section, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (2) Defrost. \"Defrost\" means to allow any frozen food to thaw so that it is not readily recognizable as frozen food. (3) Frozen food. \"Frozen food\" means any food that has been kept at or below 0\u00b0 F. (b) Representing defrosted food as fresh prohibited. No food service facility may represent or advertise as fresh any frozen food that has been allowed to defrost. (c) Selling certain defrosted food prohibited. No food service facility may sell or offer for sale any food designed to be sold frozen (such as \"TV dinners\", packaged fruits, and frozen desserts) if it has been allowed to defrost. (d) Certain defrosted food to be labeled. No food service facility may sell or offer for sale any meat, fish, seafood, or poultry that, in whole or in part, has been frozen and then allowed to defrost unless a notice stating \"this product has been frozen and thawed\" is: (1) conspicuously displayed on a sign; and (2) attached to each package or item of meat, fish, seafood, or poultry. (Ord. 04-672; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a767", "num": "6-505", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-505", "title": "\u00a7 6-505. Frozen food regulations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-505"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-505.1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-505.1. Dated food products. (a) Definitions. (1) In general. In this section, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (2) Dated food. \"Dated food\" means any food that contains an expiration date on its label or packaging. (3) Expiration date. \"Expiration date\" means: (1) any date designated as an \"expires on\" date, \"sell by\" date, \"pull by\" date, \"use by\" date, or \"best if used by\" date; or (2) any similar time guide for the sale or use of a food product by food service facilities or consumers. (b) Notice after expiration date. (1) No food service facility may sell or offer for sale any dated food after its expiration date unless that food is: (i) segregated from its non-expired food counterpart; and (ii) accompanied by a conspicuous notice that states: \"This Food is Being Sold Past Its Expiration Date\". (2) The notice required by this subsection must be: (i) on a sign at least 11\" by 14\"; and (ii) printed in letters at least 1\" high. (c) Perishable, etc., foods. Nothing in this section authorizes: (1) the sale of any perishable food, such as milk, cheese, meat, eggs, and baby food, past its expiration date; or (2) the sale or possession of any adulterated or unwholesome food. (d) Altering, etc., expiration date. No food service facility may alter, remove, cover, disguise, or otherwise obscure the expiration date of any dated food. (Ord. 02-438.)", "num": "6-505.1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-505.1", "title": "\u00a7 6-505.1. Dated food products.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-505.1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-506", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-506. Litter and rubbish prohibited. (a) In general. Each food service facility must keep its entire premises, including all abutting sidewalks, alleys, footpaths, gutters, and other public rights-of-way, free of litter and rubbish. (b) Complaints; hearing. (1) If, during any license year, the Commissioner receives 10 or more complaints of the facility's having violated this section, the Commissioner must conduct a hearing under Subtitle 6 {\"Suspensions and Revocations\"} of this title to determine if the license should be suspended, its renewal denied, or other penalties imposed. (2) For purposes of this subsection, the Commissioner may accept a complaint only if it: (i) is in writing; (ii) is made by an adult; (iii) includes the complainant's name and address; and (iv) is submitted independently of any other person's complaint about the facility. (c) Litterer remains subject to prosecution. Nothing in this section precludes the imposition of penalties under any other law that governs the disposition of litter, trash, garbage, or other waste. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165A", "num": "6-506", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-506", "title": "\u00a7 6-506. Litter and rubbish prohibited.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-506"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-507", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-507. Trans fats prohibited. (a) \"Food containing trans fat\" defined. (1) \"Food containing trans fat\" means, except as provided in paragraph (2) of this subsection, any food that: (i) is labeled as containing vegetable shortening, margarine, or any kind of partially hydrogenated vegetable oil; (ii) lists vegetable shortening, margarine, or any kind of partially hydrogenated vegetable oil as an ingredient; or (iii) contains vegetable shortening, margarine, or any kind of partially hydrogenated vegetable oil. (2) \"Food containing trans fat\" does not include food with a nutrition facts label or other documentation from the manufacturer that lists the food's trans fat content as less than 0.5 grams per serving. (b) Prohibited use, etc. (1) Except as provided in paragraph (2) of this subsection, food containing trans fat may not be stored, distributed, held for service, used in preparation of any menu item, or served in any food service facility. (2) This subsection does not apply to food that is served directly to patrons in the original sealed package of the manufacturer. (c) Labels; documentation. (1) (i) Except as provided in subparagraph (ii) of this paragraph, a food service facility must maintain on site the original label for any food: (A) that contains fats, oils, or shortenings; (B) that is required by federal or state law to have a label when purchased by a food service facility; and (C) that is stored, distributed, held for service, used in preparation of any menu items, or served by the food service facility. (ii) The Health Commissioner may permit a food service facility to submit acceptable documentation from the manufacturer that indicates whether a food contains vegetable shortening, margarine, or any kind of partially hydrogenated vegetable oil, or that indicates a food's trans fat content. (3) If a food contains fats, oils, or shortenings and is not required to be labeled when purchased, the food service facility must obtain and maintain documentation from the manufacturer that indicate whether the food contains vegetable shortening, margarine, or any kind of partially hydrogenated vegetable oil, or that indicates the food's trans fat content. (4) In the rules and regulations adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article , the Commissioner may include rules or regulations that govern the documentation acceptable or required under paragraphs (i)(ii) and (2) of this subsection. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 08-001.) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "6-507", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-507", "title": "\u00a7 6-507. Trans fats prohibited.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-507"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-508", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-508. Polystyrene foam products. (a) Definitions. (1) In general. In this section, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (2) Disposable food service ware. (i) In general. \"Disposable food service ware\" means cups, plates, dishes, bowls, trays, clamshell containers, take-away containers, or similar items used for serving food or for transporting small amounts of prepared food. (ii) Exclusion. \"Disposable food service ware\" does not include coolers. (3) Polystyrene foam. \"Polystyrene foam\" means blown polystyrene and expanded and extruded foams (sometimes called Styrofoam\u2122) that are thermoplastic petrochemical materials utilizing a styrene monomer and processed by any number of techniques, including fusion of polymer spheres (expandable bead polystyrene), injection molding, foam molding, and extrusion-blown molding (extruded foam polystyrene). (b) Prohibited use. (1) In general. Except as provided in paragraph (2) of this subsection, no food service facility may use any disposable food service ware that is made from polystyrene foam. (2) Exceptions. Paragraph (1) of this subsection does not apply to: (i) food or beverages that have been filled and sealed in polystyrene-foam containers outside of the City before the food or beverages were received by a food service facility in the City; or (ii) materials used to package raw, uncooked, or butchered meat, fish, poultry, or seafood for off-premises consumption. (Ord. 18-125.) Editor's Notes This section was enacted by Ordinance 18-125 (enacted April 19, 2018). Section 3 of that Ordinance provides that the new law takes effect 18 months after the date of enactment (i.e., on October 19, 2019).", "num": "6-508", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-508", "title": "\u00a7 6-508. Polystyrene foam products.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-508"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-509", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-509. Children's meals. (a) \"Children's meal\" defined. (1) In general. In this section, \"children's meal\" means a combination of food items that is: (i) prepared by and offered for purchase at a food service facility as a unit at a single price; and (ii) represented to be or otherwise primarily intended for consumption by children. (2) Exception for prepackaged food items. \"Children's meal\" does not include a combination of food items that has been prepackaged by or at a facility other than the food service facility offering the prepackaged combination for purchase. (b) Beverage limitation. No food service facility may offer a children's meal that includes a beverage unless that beverage is: (1) water, sparkling water, or flavored water, with no added natural or artificial sweeteners; (2) milk or a non-dairy milk alternative; or (3) 100% fruit juice or fruit juice combined with water or sparkling water, with no added natural or artificial sweeteners, in a serving size of no more than 8 ounces. (c) Qualification. Nothing in this section precludes a food service facility from providing, if specifically requested by the purchaser of the children's meal, any lawful beverage as a substitute or alternative for the beverages being offered in accordance with subsection (b) of this section. (Ord. 18-126.)", "num": "6-509", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|5|6-509", "title": "\u00a7 6-509. Children's meals.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-509"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/6", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 6 Suspensions and Revocations \u00a7 6-601. Notice of violation or hazard. \u00a7 6-602. Suspension on failure to correct. \u00a7 6-603. Suspension without notice. \u00a7 6-603.1. Suspension or nonrenewal for multiple or unpaid citations. \u00a7 6-604. Reinstatement of suspended licenses. \u00a7 6-605. Revocation of licenses. \u00a7 6-606. Public notices.", "num": "6", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|6", "title": "Subtitle 6 Suspensions and Revocations", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/6"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-601", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-601. Notice of violation or hazard. (a) In general. Whenever the Commissioner finds that any food service facility is in violation of any provision of this title or is in an unsanitary or other condition that, in the Commissioner's judgment, constitutes a hazard to the public health, the Commissioner may issue a violation notice to the facility. (b) Contents. The violation notice must: (1) cite the violation or condition; (2) specify the corrective action to be taken and the time within which that action must be taken; and (3) state that a hearing will be provided to the facility, if one is requested under Title 2, Subtitle 3 {\"Administrative Hearings\"} of this article . (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(f)(1), (f)(2)(part)", "num": "6-601", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|6|6-601", "title": "\u00a7 6-601. Notice of violation or hazard.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-601"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-602", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-602. Suspension on failure to correct. (a) Order of suspension. If a food service facility fails to comply with the violation notice within the time prescribed, the Commissioner may then issue an order of suspension to the facility. (b) Contents. An order issued under this section may: (1) immediately suspend the facility's license; and (2) order all food service facility operations to be discontinued immediately. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(f)(2)", "num": "6-602", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|6|6-602", "title": "\u00a7 6-602. Suspension on failure to correct.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-602"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-603", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-603. Suspension without notice. (a) Order of violation and suspension. If the Commissioner considers it necessary in the interest of public health, the Commissioner may issue an order of immediate suspension to the facility without having first issued a violation notice under \u00a7 6-601 {\"Notice of violation or hazard\"} of this subtitle . (b) Contents. An order issued under this section: (1) must cite the violation or condition; (2) must specify the corrective action to be taken and the time within which that action must be taken; and (3) may order all food service facility operations to be discontinued immediately. (c) Opportunity for hearing. The notice must also state that a hearing will be provided to the facility under Title 2, Subtitle 3 {\"Administrative Hearings\"} of this article , if one is requested. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(f)(3)", "num": "6-603", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|6|6-603", "title": "\u00a7 6-603. Suspension without notice.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-603"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-603.1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-603.1. Suspension or nonrenewal for multiple or unpaid citations. (a) In general. The Commissioner may suspend or refuse to renew the license of a food service facility if: (1) at any time within the preceding 6 months, the facility has been served with 3 or more citations issued for reasons related to public health or to the prevention of disease, epidemics, or nuisances affecting public health, under either or both of: (i) City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (ii) City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}; or (2) at any time within the preceding 12 months, the facility has been declared to be in default of 1 or more citations issued for reasons related to public health or to the prevention of disease, epidemics, or nuisances affecting public health, under either of: (i) City Code Article 1, \u00a7 40-8 {\"Environmental Citations: Default by person cited\"} ; or (ii) City Code Article 1, \u00a7 41-10 {\"Civil Citations: Default\"} . (b) Covered offenses. (1) For purposes of this section, citations issued for violations of the following provisions of the City Code are presumed to be issued for reasons related to public health or to the prevention of disease, epidemics, or nuisances affecting public health: \uf0b7 Article 13. Housing and Urban Renewal o Subtitle 5 {\"Licensing of Rental Dwellings\"}. \uf0b7 Article 15. Licensing and Regulation o Subtitle 17. Street Vendors o Subtitle 18. Itinerant Wholesale Produce Dealers \uf0b7 Article 19. Police Ordinances o \u00a7 1-2. Placement {of advertising circulars} without permission o \u00a7 7-2. Vehicle alarms: Prohibited devices o \u00a7 8-18. Burglar alarms: Penalties o \u00a7 13-1 or \u00a7 13-2. Disorderly drinking o \u00a7 14-2 or \u00a7 14-3. Drinking in public places \uf0b7 Subtitle 25. Loitering \u2013 General o \u00a7 26-6. Loitering \u2013 Drug-Free Zone o \u00a7 27-3. Loitering \u2013 For assignation, etc. o \u00a7 27-15. Loitering \u2013 Prostitution-Free Zone \uf0b7 Subtitle 32. Minors \u2013 Sales in Proximity to Liquor Store o \u00a7 34-5. Curfews: Prohibited conduct of parents, guardians, etc. o \u00a7 34-6. Curfews: Prohibited conduct of establishments o \u00a7 40-9. Dirt Bikes, etc. \u2013 Motor fuel sales o \u00a7 41-2. Outdoor telephones: Prohibited placement o \u00a7 45-2. Signs on public property: Posting prohibited o \u00a7 47-5. Nighttime soliciting o \u00a7 50-2. Obstructing street, etc., or gutter o \u00a7 50-3. Merchandise projecting from building o \u00a7 50-26. Fires in street, lane, or alley o \u00a7 50-41. Playing, etc., in street, etc. o \u00a7 50-56. Soliciting customers o \u00a7 59-18. Reporting {theft or loss of firearm} required \uf0b7 Article 23. Sanitation o \u00a7 2-1. Mixed refuse: Receptacles o \u00a7 2-2. Mixed refuse: Handling o \u00a7 4-1 or \u00a7 4-2. Receptacles on collection days \uf0b7 Article 24. Water o \u00a7 21-1. Rules and regulations {to protect supply and facilities} o \u00a7 21-2. Suspension, etc., of certain uses o \u00a7 21-3. Injuring fire hydrants o \u00a7 21-4. Wrongful use after cut-off o \u00a7 21-5. Refusal of entry o \u00a7 21-6. Interference with equipment; illegal use of water \uf0b7 Article 26. Surveys, Streets, and Highways o Subtitle 6. Building Address Numbers \uf0b7 Article 31. Transit and Traffic o \u00a7 16-12. Vehicles on sidewalks o \u00a7 18-8. Riding {bicycles} on sidewalk \uf0b7 Article 32. Zoning o \u00a7 5-704. {Use permit \u2013}When required o \u00a7 16-803. Storage, etc., of abandoned, etc., vehicles \uf0b7 Building, Fire, and Related Codes Article \u2013 Building Code o \u00a7 114.14. Work without permit o \u00a7 115.3. Stop-work order: Unlawful continuance o \u00a7 116. Unsafe structures o \u00a7 120. Condemnation proceedings o \u00a7 123.8. Failure to comply with notice or citation o \u00a7 3302.2 Construction Safeguards: Waste and debris \uf0b7 Building, Fire, and Related Codes Article \u2013 Fire Code o \u00a7 109.9. Maintenance: Overcrowding o \u00a7 111.1. Unsafe buildings: General o \u00a7 1001.2. Means of Egress: Minimum requirements \uf0b7 Building, Fire, and Related Codes Article \u2013 Property Maintenance Code o Chapter 3. General Requirements \uf0b7 Health Code o Title 5. Nuisance Control o Title 6: Food Service Facilities o Title 7: Waste Control o Title 8: Air Pollution o Title 9: Noise Regulation o Title 10: Animal Control and Protection o Title 11: Swimming Pools o Title 12: Tobacco Products and Smoking Devices o Title 15: Ephedrine Products (2) Nothing in this subsection precludes the Health Commissioner from determining, in his or her discretion, that a citation for an offense was issued for reasons related to public health or to the prevention of disease, epidemics, or nuisances affecting public health. (c) Notice and opportunity for hearing. Before suspending or refusing to renew a license under this section, the Commissioner must provide the facility with notice and an opportunity for hearing, as provided in Title 2, Subtitle 3 {\"Administrative Hearings\"} of this article . ( Ord. 24-341 ; Ord. 22-124; Ord. 20-361; Ord. 18-130; Ord. 16-581; Ord. 15-427; Ord. 14-310; Ord. 13-093; Ord. 12-046; Ord. 09-243.)", "num": "6-603.1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|6|6-603.1", "title": "\u00a7 6-603.1. Suspension or nonrenewal for multiple or unpaid citations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-603.1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-604", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-604. Reinstatement of suspended licenses. (a) Application for reinstatement. (1) Any food service facility may apply to the Commissioner for reinstatement of a suspended license. (2) In addition to any other information that the Commissioner requires, the application must include a signed statement by the applicant that, in the applicant's opinion, the violation or condition that caused the suspension has been corrected. (b) Reinspection. Within 10 days of receiving the application and the reinspection fee specified in subsection (d) of this section, the Commissioner must reinspect the facility. (c) Required reinstatement. If, on reinspection, the facility is found to comply with all applicable requirements, the license must be reinstated. (d) Reinspection fees. (1) The fee for a reinspection performed during regular working hours is $100. (2) The fee for a reinspection performed during weekend or evening hours is $300. (Ord. 09-243; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(g)(3rd par.)", "num": "6-604", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|6|6-604", "title": "\u00a7 6-604. Reinstatement of suspended licenses.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-604"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-605", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-605. Revocation of licenses. (a) Grounds. The Commissioner may revoke the license of a food service facility if that facility: (1) commits more than one violation of this title; or (2) interferes with the performance of the Commissioner's duties. (b) Notice and opportunity for hearing. Before revoking a license, the Commissioner must provide the facility with notice and opportunity for hearing, as provided in Title 2, Subtitle 3 {\"Administrative Hearings\"} of this article . (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7165(h)", "num": "6-605", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|6|6-605", "title": "\u00a7 6-605. Revocation of licenses.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-605"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-606", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-606. Public notices. (a) Suspensions \u2013 Posting of premises. (1) A food service facility that has had its license suspended must post a public notice of the suspension throughout the suspension period. (2) The notice must: (i) state the term of and reasons for the suspension; (ii) be in the form and tenor that the Commissioner specifies; and (iii) be conspicuously placed, as directed by the Commissioner, so that it is visible to and can be read by passing pedestrians. (b) Suspensions, revocations, and nonrenewals \u2013 Timely notice through social media. (1) Promptly after a food service facility has had its license suspended, revoked, or denied renewal, the Commissioner must provide, through 2 or more social media outlets, public notice of the suspension, revocation, or nonrenewal. (2) The notice must include the same information as that required by subsection (c)(2) of this section for a website listing. (c) Suspensions, revocations, and nonrenewals \u2013 Website listings. (1) The Commissioner must maintain, update at least weekly, and publish on the Department's website a list of all food service facilities that, at any time during the preceding calendar month or during the current calendar month to the latest update, have had their licenses suspended, revoked, or denied renewal. (2) The listing must include: (i) the name (including trade name) and address of the facility; (ii) the effective date and term of the suspension, revocation, or nonrenewal; and (iii) the reasons for the suspension, revocation, or nonrenewal. (Ord. 15-399; Ord. 09-217; Ord. 02-439; Ord. 99-391.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a761(3)", "num": "6-606", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|6|6-606", "title": "\u00a7 6-606. Public notices.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-606"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/7", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 7 [Reserved]", "num": "7", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|7", "title": "Subtitle 7 [Reserved]", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/7"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/8", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 8 Penalties \u00a7 6-801. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 6-802. Penalties.", "num": "8", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|8", "title": "Subtitle 8 Penalties", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6/8"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-801", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-801. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this title may be enforced by issuance of an environmental citation as authorized by City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of an environmental citation to enforce this title does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "6-801", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|8|6-801", "title": "\u00a7 6-801. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-801"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-802", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 6-802. Penalties. (a) In general. Any person who violates any provision of this title or of a rule or regulation adopted under this title is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to the penalties specified in this section. (b) Basic penalty: $1,000. Except as specified in subsection (c) of this section, the penalty for a violation is a fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense. (c) Operating without or in violation of license: $1,000 and 12 months. (1) For operating a food service facility without a license or in violation of the terms of a license, the penalty is a fine of not more than $1,000 or imprisonment for not more than 12 months or both fine and imprisonment for each offense. (2) Each day that a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "6-802", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|6|8|6-802", "title": "\u00a7 6-802. Penalties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/6-802"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Title 7 Waste Control Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 7-101 \u2013 7-103 Subtitle 2 Solid Waste Collection \u00a7\u00a7 7-201 \u2013 7-230 Subtitle 3 Transporting Waste \u00a7\u00a7 7-301 \u2013 7-307 Subtitle 4 Landfills \u00a7\u00a7 7-401 \u2013 7-419 Subtitle 5 Hydraulic Fracturing \u00a7\u00a7 7-501 \u2013 7-504 Subtitle 6 Prohibited Disposal \u00a7\u00a7 7-601 \u2013 7-632 Subtitle 7 Littering \u00a7\u00a7 7-701 \u2013 7-706", "num": "7", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7", "title": "Title 7 Waste Control", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7 7-101. Definitions. \u00a7 7-102. Findings and declaration of public policy. \u00a7 7-103. Rules and regulations.", "num": "1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|1", "title": "Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-101", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-101. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Dispose. \"Dispose\" includes abandon, deposit, discard, discharge, dump, junk, leave, place, scrap, or throw. (c) Garbage. \"Garbage\" means waste that results from the distribution, preparation, or serving of food. (d) Hauler. (1) In general. \"Hauler\" means any person who: (i) contracts with others for the collection, transportation, or disposal of solid waste; or (ii) except as specified in paragraph (3) of this subsection, engages in the collection, transportation, or disposal of solid waste generated by that person him-, her-, or itself. (2) Inclusions. \"Hauler\" includes a person described in paragraph (1) of this subsection even if that person is operating under a demolition permit issued by the City. (3) Exclusions. \"Hauler\" does not include any person who occupies residential property and collects, transports, or disposes of solid waste generated by the residential use of that property. (e) Land clearance debris. \"Land clearance debris\" means any trees, other vegetation, or their roots that result from land clearance for streets, parks, playgrounds, construction projects, or other similar projects. (f) Landfill. \"Landfill\" means any private or public property where waste is disposed of by placement on or burial in the ground. (g) Rock. \"Rock\" means rock, stones, or boulders that result from land clearance, grading, and the preparation of construction sites and similar projects. (h) Rubbish. \"Rubbish\" includes paper, rags, ashes, leaves, tree branches, yard trimmings, furniture, appliances, cans, glass, crockery, junk vehicles, tires, automotive parts, paints, and oils. (i) Rubble. (1) In general. \"Rubble\" means any waste that results from the demolition of buildings, structures, or streets. (2) Inclusions. \"Rubble\" includes masonry, concrete, asphalt, wood, plaster, paper, glass, metal, roof materials, or other materials used in the construction of buildings, structures, or streets. (j) Solid waste. \"Solid waste\" means all waste that is neither gaseous nor liquid. (k) Trade waste. (1) In general. \"Trade waste\" means waste that results from construction or from any other business, commercial, or industrial operation. (2) Inclusions. \"Trade waste\" includes plastics, cartons, chemicals, paints, greases, oils, and other petroleum products, sawdust, and dead animals. (l) Waste. (1) Defined inclusions. \"Waste\" includes any of the following material, as defined in this section, whether putrescible or nonputrescible: (i) garbage; (ii) land clearance debris; (iii) rock; (iv) rubbish; (v) rubble; and (vi) trade waste. (2) Additional inclusions. \"Waste\" also includes any of the following material, whether putrescible or nonputrescible: (i) asbestos; (ii) ashes; (iii) dead animals; (iv) hazardous waste; (v) incinerator residue; (vi) medical waste; (vii) refuse; (viii) street cleanings; (ix) trash; and (x) wastewater treatment residue. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7 221(parts), 266(parts), 264(b)(2), 266(c)(parts), 267(a) , art. 23, \u00a722(a)(7)", "num": "7-101", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|1|7-101", "title": "\u00a7 7-101. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-101"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-102", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-102. Findings and declaration of public policy. (a) Findings. The Mayor and City Council finds that: (1) the dumping of waste and other material is a sickening blight on the streets, sidewalks, parks, open spaces, and waters of the City; (2) as the available amount of landfill space decreases, incidents of dumping have increased, most notoriously (though not exclusively) by those involved in the construction business; and (3) this dumping of waste, as well as its collection and transportation, is a serious threat to the environment and the health, welfare, safety, peace, and comfort of the City's inhabitants. (b) Policy. The Mayor and City Council declares as a matter of policy that, to eliminate these hazards, it is necessary to: (1) strictly regulate the collection, transportation, and disposal of all waste in the City; (2) expand the prohibitions against illegal dumping; and (3) increase the penalties for violations. (c) Commissioner to regulate. Because of the serious health and environmental consequences, the Commissioner of Health is granted jurisdiction over the collection, transportation, and disposition of waste by commercial haulers and other persons in the City. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7135(a), 220(parts), 264(a)", "num": "7-102", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|1|7-102", "title": "\u00a7 7-102. Findings and declaration of public policy.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-102"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-103", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-103. Rules and regulations. In the rules and regulations adopted under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article , the Commissioner may include provisions, consistent with this title and State law, that govern: (1) the storage facilities, vehicles, equipment, and operations of all persons transporting waste; and (2) the construction, design, operation, maintenance, and completion of landfills in the City. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7220(b)(last cl.), 223(b), 267(d)(part), 270(a)(part)", "num": "7-103", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|1|7-103", "title": "\u00a7 7-103. Rules and regulations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-103"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 2 Solid Waste Collection Part I Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 7-201 \u2013 7-202 Part II License Required \u00a7\u00a7 7-205 \u2013 7-211 Part III Suspensions and Revocations \u00a7\u00a7 7-214 \u2013 7-215 Part IV Haulers' Duties \u00a7\u00a7 7-218 \u2013 7-222 Part V Using Unlicensed Haulers \u00a7\u00a7 7-225 \u2013 7-226 Part VI Penalties \u00a7\u00a7 7-229 \u2013 7-230", "num": "2", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2", "title": "Subtitle 2 Solid Waste Collection", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7 7-201. Definitions. \u00a7 7-202. Scope of subtitle. 7-203 to 7-204. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|I", "title": "Part I Definitions; General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-201", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-201. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) License. \"License\" means a license issued under this subtitle to operate as a hauler. (c) Small hauler. \"Small hauler\" means a hauler that uses only 1 truck for which the manufacturer's rated capacity is \u00be ton or less and the gross weight of which is 7,000 pounds or less. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7266(c)", "num": "7-201", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|I|7-201", "title": "\u00a7 7-201. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-201"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-202", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-202. Scope of subtitle. This subtitle does not apply to: (1) any person while employed by or under contract with the City for public work; (2) a 1- or 2-day community clean-up where neighbors join in to pay the cost of hiring a truck; or (3) a scrap metal dealer who: (i) is licensed under City Code Article 2 , Subtitle 8; and (ii) is transporting materials for purchase, sale, recycling, or storage (but not for disposal). (Ord. 10-389; Ord. 04-692; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7266(c)", "num": "7-202", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|I|7-202", "title": "\u00a7 7-202. Scope of subtitle.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-202"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-203to7-204", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-203 to 7-204. {Reserved}", "num": "7-203 to 7-204", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|I|7-203to7-204", "title": "7-203 to 7-204. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-203to7-204"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II License Required \u00a7 7-205. In general. \u00a7 7-206. Applications. \u00a7 7-207. Inspection of applicant. \u00a7 7-208. Issuance of license. \u00a7 7-209. Term and renewal of license. \u00a7 7-210. License fees. \u00a7 7-211. Inspection of licensee. 7-212 to 7-213. {Reserved}", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|II", "title": "Part II License Required", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-205", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-205. In general. No person may operate as a hauler in the City without a license to do so from the Commissioner of Health. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7267(a), (a-1)", "num": "7-205", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|II|7-205", "title": "\u00a7 7-205. In general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-205"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-206", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-206. Applications. The application for a license: (1) must be on the form that the Commissioner provides; and (2) in addition to any other information that the Commissioner requires, must contain the type, make, year, capacity, and vehicle tag number of every vehicle that the applicant proposes to use. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7267(a), (a-1)", "num": "7-206", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|II|7-206", "title": "\u00a7 7-206. Applications.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-206"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-207", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-207. Inspection of applicant. On receipt of the application, the Commissioner may inspect the applicant's storage facilities, vehicles, and other equipment before issuing a license. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7267(d)", "num": "7-207", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|II|7-207", "title": "\u00a7 7-207. Inspection of applicant.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-207"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-208", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-208. Issuance of license. The Commissioner must issue a license to the applicant if the applicant: (1) has complied with all applicable laws, rules, and regulations; and (2) pays the applicable license fee. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7267(d)", "num": "7-208", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|II|7-208", "title": "\u00a7 7-208. Issuance of license.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-208"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-209", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-209. Term and renewal of license. (a) Term of license. (1) An applicant may, at the applicant's option, apply for either an annual license or a 90-day license. (2) Unless sooner suspended or revoked: (i) an annual license expires on the 1 st annual anniversary of its issuance; and (ii) a 90-day license expires on the 90 th day after its issuance. (b) Application for renewal. Before a license expires, the licensee periodically may renew the license for an additional 1-year or 90-day term, as the case may be, if the licensee: (1) otherwise is entitled to a license; (2) submits a renewal application to the Commissioner, on the form that the Commissioner provides; and (3) pays the applicable fee. (Ord. 12-159; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7267(c)(2nd sen.)", "num": "7-209", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|II|7-209", "title": "\u00a7 7-209. Term and renewal of license.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-209"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-210", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-210. License fees. (a) In general. The fees to be charged under this subtitle are as set by the Commissioner, with the approval of the Board of Estimates. (b) Differentials. The fees so set: (1) must include a pro rated differential for 90-day licenses; and (2) may include an appropriate differential for small haulers. (Ord. 12-159; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7267(c)(2nd sen.)", "num": "7-210", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|II|7-210", "title": "\u00a7 7-210. License fees.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-210"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-211", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-211. Inspection of licensee. (a) When authorized. From time to time as the Commissioner sees fit, the Commissioner may inspect any storage facilities, vehicles, and other equipment of a licensee. (b) When required. The Commissioner must undertake an inspection of a licensee if the Commissioner receives written complaints from 2 or more unrelated persons that the licensee is in violation of the subtitle. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7270(a)", "num": "7-211", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|II|7-211", "title": "\u00a7 7-211. Inspection of licensee.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-211"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-212to7-213", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-212 to 7-213. {Reserved}", "num": "7-212 to 7-213", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|II|7-212to7-213", "title": "7-212 to 7-213. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-212to7-213"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/III", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part III Suspensions and Revocations \u00a7 7-214. Suspension or revocation after notice. \u00a7 7-215. Suspension or revocation after conviction. 7-216 to 7-217. {Reserved}", "num": "III", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|III", "title": "Part III Suspensions and Revocations", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/III"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-214", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-214. Suspension or revocation after notice. (a) Notice \u2014 in general. The Commissioner may issue a violation notice to a licensee who the Commissioner believes is in violation of any provision of: (1) this subtitle; (2) any rule or regulation adopted under this subtitle; or (3) the terms of the license issued to the licensee. (b) Notice \u2014 contents. The violation notice must: (1) cite the violation; (2) specify the corrective action to be taken; and (3) state the time within which that action must be taken. (c) Action on failure to correct. If the licensee fails to make the corrections within the time prescribed, the Commissioner may suspend or revoke the license, subject to the hearing provisions of Title 2, Subtitle 3 {\"Administrative Hearings\"} of this article . (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7270(a)", "num": "7-214", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|III|7-214", "title": "\u00a7 7-214. Suspension or revocation after notice.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-214"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-215", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-215. Suspension or revocation after conviction. Subject to the hearing provisions of Title 2, Subtitle 3 {\"Administrative Hearings\"} of this article , the Commissioner may suspend or revoke the license of any licensee who is convicted of a violation of: (1) this subtitle; (2) any rule or regulation adopted under this subtitle; or (3) any other law involving the handling or disposition of waste. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7270(d)", "num": "7-215", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|III|7-215", "title": "\u00a7 7-215. Suspension or revocation after conviction.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-215"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-216to7-217", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-216 to 7-217. {Reserved}", "num": "7-216 to 7-217", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|III|7-216to7-217", "title": "7-216 to 7-217. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-216to7-217"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/IV", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part IV Haulers' Duties \u00a7 7-218. Identification of vehicles. \u00a7 7-219. Identification of containers. \u00a7 7-220. Sanitation requirements. \u00a7 7-221. Hours of collection. \u00a7 7-222. Daily log. 7-223 to 7-224. {Reserved}", "num": "IV", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|IV", "title": "Part IV Haulers' Duties", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/IV"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-218", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-218. Identification of vehicles. (a) In general. (1) Except as specified in subsection (b) of this section, every vehicle used by a hauler to collect, transport, or dispose of waste must be identified as specified in this subsection. (2) The name and business telephone number of the hauler must be displayed: (i) on both sides of the vehicle; and (ii) in lettering that: (A) conspicuously contrasts with its background; and (B) is of a size, shape, and color to be readily legible, during daylight hours, from a distance of 50 feet while the vehicle is stationary. (3) The required display may be on removable devices. (b) Small haulers. Every vehicle used by a small hauler must have its license displayed inside the vehicle in a way that is visible at all times from outside the vehicle. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7269(d), (h)", "num": "7-218", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|IV|7-218", "title": "\u00a7 7-218. Identification of vehicles.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-218"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-219", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-219. Identification of containers. (a) In general. Each waste container that has a capacity of 2 cubic yards or more must be labeled with: (1) the name of the licensee; and (2) an identification number assigned by the Commissioner. (b) Form. The label must be: (1) on the outside of the container; and (2) in permanent lettering that is: (i) plainly distinguishable; and (ii) at least 3 inches high. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7269(d), (h)", "num": "7-219", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|IV|7-219", "title": "\u00a7 7-219. Identification of containers.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-219"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-220", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-220. Sanitation requirements. (a) Preventing spills. Each licensee must: (1) comply with the requirements of Subtitle 3 {\"Transporting Waste\"} of this title; (2) cover or tie down all solid waste being transported on an open-type vehicle so as to prevent spillage; and (3) take all other action necessary to prevent leakage or loss of any waste from either containers or vehicles. (b) Cleaning vehicles. Each vehicle used to collect, transport, or dispose of waste must be maintained in a clean condition that minimizes odors and prevents insect and rodent breeding. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7269(a) - (c)", "num": "7-220", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|IV|7-220", "title": "\u00a7 7-220. Sanitation requirements.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-220"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-221", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-221. Hours of collection. (a) In general. Except as specified in subsection (b) of this section, no hauler or any other person may collect solid waste in the City at any time before 7 a.m. or after 11 p.m. on any day, weekends and legal holidays included. (b) Nonresidential areas. A licensed hauler may collect solid waste at any time from a business, commercial, industrial, institutional, or other nonresidential use structure as long as no residential structure is within 300 feet of any collection point. (c) Emergency waivers. The Commissioner may waive the requirements of this section in the event of a civil emergency. (Ord. 08-079; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a726(f)", "num": "7-221", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|IV|7-221", "title": "\u00a7 7-221. Hours of collection.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-221"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-222", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-222. Daily log. (a) Driver to keep. The driver of any vehicle being used under a hauler's license must keep a daily log that includes, for each collection of solid waste: (1) the location from which the waste is collected; (2) the general nature of the solid waste; (3) the name of the customer from whom the waste is collected; and (4) when, where, and how the waste was disposed of. (b) Inspection. The licensee must permit the Commissioner to inspect this log at any time during regular business hours. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7269(g)", "num": "7-222", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|IV|7-222", "title": "\u00a7 7-222. Daily log.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-222"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-223to7-224", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-223 to 7-224. {Reserved}", "num": "7-223 to 7-224", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|IV|7-223to7-224", "title": "7-223 to 7-224. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-223to7-224"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/V", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part V Using Unlicensed Haulers \u00a7 7-225. List of licensees. \u00a7 7-226. Prohibited conduct. 7-227 to 7-228. {Reserved}", "num": "V", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|V", "title": "Part V Using Unlicensed Haulers", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/V"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-225", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-225. List of licensees. (a) Commissioner to publish. The Commissioner must publish and distribute to all community associations listed with the Department of Planning an annual list of all small haulers licensed under this subtitle who indicate their availability to provide services to residential properties. (b) Warnings. This list must contain a notice of the prohibition in \u00a7 7-226 {\"Prohibited conduct\"} of this subtitle against using an unlicensed hauler. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7268(d)", "num": "7-225", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|V|7-225", "title": "\u00a7 7-225. List of licensees.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-225"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-226", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-226. Prohibited conduct. No person may contract with or hire any hauler who is not licensed under this subtitle. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7267(b)", "num": "7-226", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|V|7-226", "title": "\u00a7 7-226. Prohibited conduct.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-226"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-227to7-228", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-227 to 7-228. {Reserved}", "num": "7-227 to 7-228", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|V|7-227to7-228", "title": "7-227 to 7-228. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-227to7-228"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/VI", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part VI Penalties \u00a7 7-229. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 7-230. Penalties: $500/day.", "num": "VI", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|VI", "title": "Part VI Penalties", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/2/VI"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-229", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-229. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 03-595; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "7-229", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|VI|7-229", "title": "\u00a7 7-229. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-229"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-230", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-230. Penalties: $500/day. (a) In general. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle or of any rule or regulation adopted under this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $500 for each offense. (b) Each day a separate offense. Each day that a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7272", "num": "7-230", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|2|VI|7-230", "title": "\u00a7 7-230. Penalties: $500/day.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-230"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/3", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 3 Transporting Waste \u00a7 7-301. Coverings and containers required. \u00a7 7-302. Loading and care. \u00a7 7-303. Noncommercial vehicles. \u00a7 7-304. Replacement of spills. 7-305 to 7-306. {Reserved} \u00a7 7-307. Penalties: $500.", "num": "3", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|3", "title": "Subtitle 3 Transporting Waste", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/3"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-301", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-301. Coverings and containers required. (a) In general. Every vehicle that transports waste on the streets of this City, including vehicles used by the City, must be equipped with adequate coverings and containers to prevent the spilling or dropping of any waste. (b) Construction. The vehicle and all waste containers must: (1) be strong and tight; (2) have sides high enough above the load that no part of the load can fall, leak, or spill; and (3) be covered with heavy canvas or other substantial material. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7121(2nd sen.), art. 23, \u00a7\u00a715, 16", "num": "7-301", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|3|7-301", "title": "\u00a7 7-301. Coverings and containers required.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-301"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-302", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-302. Loading and care. (a) In general. No person may allow any vehicle or container to be so fully loaded, in such bad repair, of such faulty construction, or so improperly operated that any waste can fall, leak, or spill. (b) Care by drivers, etc. No person driving, loading, unloading, or cleaning any vehicle or container used to carry waste may do so or permit any other person to do so in any way that is needlessly offensive or filthy in respect to any person or property. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 23, \u00a7\u00a717, 18(1st cl.)", "num": "7-302", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|3|7-302", "title": "\u00a7 7-302. Loading and care.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-302"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-303", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-303. Noncommercial vehicles. The owner of every noncommercial vehicle used to carry any waste or any sand, dirt, gravel, loam, earth, fertilizer, filth, stone, brick, or coal must keep the load in a tight and secure condition so that no part of the load can fall, leak, or spill. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 23, \u00a720(1st cl.)", "num": "7-303", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|3|7-303", "title": "\u00a7 7-303. Noncommercial vehicles.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-303"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-304", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-304. Replacement of spills. If any material falls, leaks, or spills from a vehicle or container, the individual operating the vehicle or in charge of the container must immediately stop and remove and secure the fallen, leaked, or spilled material. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7121(3rd sen.(last cl.)), art. 23, \u00a718(2nd cl.)", "num": "7-304", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|3|7-304", "title": "\u00a7 7-304. Replacement of spills.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-304"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-305to7-306", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-305 to 7-306. {Reserved}", "num": "7-305 to 7-306", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|3|7-305to7-306", "title": "7-305 to 7-306. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-305to7-306"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-307", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-307. Penalties: $500. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $500 for each offense. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "7-307", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|3|7-307", "title": "\u00a7 7-307. Penalties: $500.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-307"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 4 Landfills Part I Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 7-401 \u2013 7-402 Part II License Required \u00a7\u00a7 7-406 \u2013 7-411 Part III Suspensions, Revocations, and Denials \u00a7\u00a7 7-414 \u2013 7-415 Part IV Penalties \u00a7\u00a7 7-418 \u2013 7-419", "num": "4", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4", "title": "Subtitle 4 Landfills", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7 7-401. Definitions. \u00a7 7-402. Exceptions from subtitle. 7-403 to 7-405. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|I", "title": "Part I Definitions; General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-401", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-401. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) License. \"License\" means a license issued under this subtitle to operate a landfill. (c) Person. (1) In general. \"Person\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 1-107(a) {\"Person: In general\"} of the City Code's General Provisions Article . (2) Qualified inclusion of governmental entities. Notwithstanding \u00a7 1-107(b) {\"Person: Exclusion\"} of the General Provisions Article , in this subtitle \"person\" also includes, except as used in \u00a7 7-419 {\"Penalties\"} of this subtitle , a governmental entity or an instrumentality or unit of a governmental entity. (Ord. 22-125; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "7-401", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|I|7-401", "title": "\u00a7 7-401. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-401"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-402", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-402. Exceptions from subtitle. This subtitle does not apply to: (1) the random placement of broken stones or rubble as a foundation, sustaining wall, or similar structure to control erosion; or (2) to the otherwise lawful filling of land exclusively with earth fill material that contains: (i) no more than 10% organic matter or rubble by volume; and (ii) no object larger than 12 inches in any dimension. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7221(a)(2nd cl.), 221(1), 224", "num": "7-402", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|I|7-402", "title": "\u00a7 7-402. Exceptions from subtitle.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-402"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-403to7-405", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-403 to 7-405. {Reserved}", "num": "7-403 to 7-405", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|I|7-403to7-405", "title": "7-403 to 7-405. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-403to7-405"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II License Required \u00a7 7-406. In general. \u00a7 7-407. Applications. \u00a7 7-408. Issuance of license. \u00a7 7-409. Term and renewal of license. \u00a7 7-410. License fees. \u00a7 7-411. Notice of changed information. 7-412 to 7-413. {Reserved}", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|II", "title": "Part II License Required", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-406", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-406. In general. No person may operate a landfill in the City without a license to do so from the Commissioner of Health. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7222(b)", "num": "7-406", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|II|7-406", "title": "\u00a7 7-406. In general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-406"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-407", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-407. Applications. The application for a license: (1) must be on the form that the Commissioner provides; and (2) in addition to any other information that the Commissioner requires, must contain: (i) the name of the applicant; (ii) the applicant's home address; (iii) the address of the applicant's principal place of business; (iv) the location of the proposed landfill; (v) the name under which the business is carried on; (vi) a detailed engineering plan of the proposed operation of the landfill; and (vii) evidence satisfactory to the Commissioner that the applicant has obtained a bond or other security, running to the benefit of the City, in the form, amount, and tenor: (A) as required by State law for landfills licensed by the State; or (B) if no bond or security is required by State law, as the Commissioner requires. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7223(a)(1)(1st cl.), (2), (3)(1st cl.)", "num": "7-407", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|II|7-407", "title": "\u00a7 7-407. Applications.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-407"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-408", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-408. Issuance of license. The Commissioner must issue a license to the applicant if: (1) the Commissioner approves the engineering plan; (2) the applicant has complied with all applicable laws, rules, and regulations; and (3) the applicant pays the applicable fee. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7223(a)(3)(2nd cl.)", "num": "7-408", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|II|7-408", "title": "\u00a7 7-408. Issuance of license.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-408"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-409", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-409. Term and renewal of license. (a) Term of license. Unless sooner suspended or revoked, a license expires annually on the anniversary of its effective date. (b) Application for renewal. Before a license expires, the licensee periodically may renew the license for an additional 1-year term, if the licensee: (1) otherwise is entitled to a license; (2) submits a renewal application to the Commissioner, in the form that the Commissioner requires; and (3) pays the applicable renewal fee. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7223(a)(intro), (a)(4)(1st sen.)", "num": "7-409", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|II|7-409", "title": "\u00a7 7-409. Term and renewal of license.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-409"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-410", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-410. License fees. The annual fee for a license is as set by the Commissioner, with the approval of the Board of Estimates. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7223(a)(4)(2nd sen.)", "num": "7-410", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|II|7-410", "title": "\u00a7 7-410. License fees.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-410"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-411", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-411. Notice of changed information. A licensee must immediately notify the Commissioner of any change in any of the information contained in or accompanying the licensee's application for a license or a renewal. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7223(a)(1)(2nd cl.)", "num": "7-411", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|II|7-411", "title": "\u00a7 7-411. Notice of changed information.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-411"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-412to7-413", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-412 to 7-413. {Reserved}", "num": "7-412 to 7-413", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|II|7-412to7-413", "title": "7-412 to 7-413. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-412to7-413"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4/III", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part III Suspensions, Revocations, and Denials \u00a7 7-414. Suspensions. \u00a7 7-415. Revocations and denials of renewal. 7-416 to 7-417. {Reserved}", "num": "III", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|III", "title": "Part III Suspensions, Revocations, and Denials", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4/III"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-414", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-414. Suspensions. (a) Grounds. Subject to the hearing provisions of Title 2, Subtitle 3 {\"Administrative Hearings\"} of this article , the Commissioner of Health may suspend a license: (1) on receipt of notice of intent to cancel the bond or other security required by State law; (2) if the licensee fails to comply with the engineering plan approved by the Commissioner; or (3) if the Commissioner finds the licensee has caused a condition that is hazardous to the public health, safety, or welfare. (b) Duration. A suspension continues until the licensee: (1) satisfies the Commissioner that the cause for the suspension has been adequately corrected; and (2) has reimbursed the City for any expenses that the City incurs as a result of the condition. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7223(a)(5)", "num": "7-414", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|III|7-414", "title": "\u00a7 7-414. Suspensions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-414"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-415", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-415. Revocations and denials of renewal. Subject to the hearing provisions of Title 2, Subtitle 3 {\"Administrative Hearings\"} of this article , the Commissioner of Health may revoke or refuse to renew the license of any licensee who repeatedly violates any of the conditions of the license or any of the provisions of this subtitle or of the rules and regulations adopted under this subtitle. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7223(a)(6)", "num": "7-415", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|III|7-415", "title": "\u00a7 7-415. Revocations and denials of renewal.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-415"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-416to7-417", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-416 to 7-417. {Reserved}", "num": "7-416 to 7-417", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|III|7-416to7-417", "title": "7-416 to 7-417. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-416to7-417"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4/IV", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part IV Penalties \u00a7 7-418. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 7-419. Penalties: $1,000 and $100/day.", "num": "IV", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|IV", "title": "Part IV Penalties", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/4/IV"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-418", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-418. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 03-595; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "7-418", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|IV|7-418", "title": "\u00a7 7-418. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-418"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-419", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-419. Penalties: $1,000 and $100/day. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle or of a rule or regulation adopted under this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense, plus $100 for each day that the offense continues. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7226(a)", "num": "7-419", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|4|IV|7-419", "title": "\u00a7 7-419. Penalties: $1,000 and $100/day.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-419"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/5", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 5 Hydraulic Fracturing \u00a7 7-501. Definitions. \u00a7 7-502. Hydraulic fracturing prohibited \u00a7 7-503. Storing, treating, disposing, etc., wastewater prohibited. \u00a7 7-504. Penalties: $1,000 and 90 days.", "num": "5", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|5", "title": "Subtitle 5 Hydraulic Fracturing", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/5"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-501", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-501. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Flow back. \"Flow back\" means the fracturing fluids that return to the surface after a hydraulic fracture is completed. (c) Hydraulic fracturing. \"Hydraulic fracturing\" means the drilling technique (also known colloquially as \"fracking\", \"hydrofracking\", or \"hydrofracturing\") of expanding or creating fractures in rock by injecting fluids, often a mixture of water and chemicals, sand, or other substances, and often under pressure, into or underneath the surface of the rock for various purposes, including well drilling and natural gas exploration and production. (Ord. 13-113.)", "num": "7-501", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|5|7-501", "title": "\u00a7 7-501. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-501"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-502", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-502. Hydraulic fracturing prohibited No person may make use of hydraulic fracturing to extract oil, gas, or other hydrocarbons within the City of Baltimore. (Ord. 16-591.)", "num": "7-502", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|5|7-502", "title": "\u00a7 7-502. Hydraulic fracturing prohibited", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-502"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-503", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-503. Storing, treating, disposing, etc., wastewater prohibited. No person may store, treat, discharge, or dispose of in the City or in or on any City-owned facility or property, wherever situated, any flow back or other wastewater resulting from hydraulic fracturing. (Ord. 16-591; Ord. 13-113.)", "num": "7-503", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|5|7-503", "title": "\u00a7 7-503. Storing, treating, disposing, etc., wastewater prohibited.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-503"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-504", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-504. Penalties: $1,000 and 90 days. (a) In general. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $1,000, imprisonment for not more than 90 days, or both fine and imprisonment for each offense. (b) Each day a separate offense. Each day that a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 16-591; Ord. 13-113.)", "num": "7-504", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|5|7-504", "title": "\u00a7 7-504. Penalties: $1,000 and 90 days.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-504"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 6 Prohibited Disposal Part I Scope of Subtitle \u00a7\u00a7 7-601 \u2013 7-601 Part II Prohibited Activities \u00a7\u00a7 7-606 \u2013 7-610 Part III Seizure and Forfeiture of Vehicles \u00a7\u00a7 7-616 \u2013 7-624 Part IV Enforcement; Penalties \u00a7\u00a7 7-627 \u2013 7-632", "num": "6", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6", "title": "Subtitle 6 Prohibited Disposal", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I Scope of Subtitle \u00a7 7-601. \"Litter\" excluded. 7-602 to 7-605. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|I", "title": "Part I Scope of Subtitle", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-601", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-601. \"Litter\" excluded. This subtitle does not apply to the disposal of \"litter\", as defined in Subtitle 7 of this title. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-601", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|I|7-601", "title": "\u00a7 7-601. \"Litter\" excluded.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-601"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-602to7-605", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-602 to 7-605. {Reserved}", "num": "7-602 to 7-605", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|I|7-602to7-605", "title": "7-602 to 7-605. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-602to7-605"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II Prohibited Activities \u00a7 7-606. In general. \u00a7 7-607. Disposing of offensive materials. \u00a7 7-608. Dumping on public property. \u00a7 7-609. Dumping on private property. \u00a7 7-610. Burning waste. 7-611 to 7-615. {Reserved}", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|II", "title": "Part II Prohibited Activities", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-606", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-606. In general. No person may dispose of any waste or other material except: (1) in a receptacle and at a location approved by law for waste disposal; (2) at a licensed landfill; or (3) at any other disposal site authorized by law to receive waste. (Ord. 08-044; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7135(c), 220, 222(a), 264(c), art. 23, \u00a713", "num": "7-606", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|II|7-606", "title": "\u00a7 7-606. In general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-606"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-607", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-607. Disposing of offensive materials. (a) In general. No person may dispose of or permit to discharge or flow onto any public or private property, with or without the owner's permission, any liquid or solid matter that is or that, after exposure to the atmosphere or otherwise, is likely to become offensive or otherwise a nuisance. (b) Illustrations. This section applies to, among other things, any: (1) blood; (2) refuse coal oil; (3) dead animal or part of an animal; (4) domestic or sanitary sewage; (5) excrement; (6) filth; (7) foul or nauseous liquid; (8) garbage; (9) slaughter house or other trade cleanings; (10) stagnant water; or (11) offensive matter of any kind. (Ord. 08-044; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7119, inter alia", "num": "7-607", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|II|7-607", "title": "\u00a7 7-607. Disposing of offensive materials.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-607"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-608", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-608. Dumping on public property. No person may dump or dispose of any garbage, waste, wire, glass, nails, or any other matter: (1) in or on any gutter, sidewalk, street, open space, wharf, or other public place; or (2) except for litter, as defined in Subtitle 7 of this title, into any public trash receptacle located on or along any sidewalk, street, open space, wharf, or other public place. (Ord. 12-065; Ord. 08-044; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7\u00a7167, 172", "num": "7-608", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|II|7-608", "title": "\u00a7 7-608. Dumping on public property.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-608"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-609", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-609. Dumping on private property. No person may dump or otherwise dispose of any earth, dirt, sand, ashes, gravel, rocks, garbage, waste, or any other matter on any private property, including in or near any waste receptacle on the property, without the permission of the property owner or the owner's agent. (Ord. 11-478; Ord. 08-044; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7135, 160(1st cl.)", "num": "7-609", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|II|7-609", "title": "\u00a7 7-609. Dumping on private property.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-609"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-610", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-610. Burning waste. No person may burn or cause to be burned any garbage or other waste except as specifically authorized by law. (Ord. 08-044; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7135, 160(1st cl.)", "num": "7-610", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|II|7-610", "title": "\u00a7 7-610. Burning waste.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-610"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-611to7-615", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-611 to 7-615. {Reserved}", "num": "7-611 to 7-615", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|II|7-611to7-615", "title": "7-611 to 7-615. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-611to7-615"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6/III", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part III Seizure and Forfeiture of Vehicles \u00a7 7-616. Responsibility of vehicle owner. \u00a7 7-617. Grounds for seizure. \u00a7 7-618. Seizure by police. \u00a7 7-619. Referral to Solicitor. \u00a7 7-620. Forfeiture petition; notice. \u00a7 7-621. Answer to petition. \u00a7 7-622. Early retrieval by owner. \u00a7 7-623. Order of forfeiture. \u00a7 7-624. Order of release. 7-625 to 7-626. {Reserved}", "num": "III", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III", "title": "Part III Seizure and Forfeiture of Vehicles", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6/III"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-616", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-616. Responsibility of vehicle owner. The registered owner of a vehicle or, for a leased or rented vehicle, the lessee or renter, as identified on the lease or rental agreement, is prima facie responsible for any waste or other material transported in or disposed of from that vehicle. (Ord. 11-478; Ord. 08-044; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7136(3rd sen.)", "num": "7-616", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-616", "title": "\u00a7 7-616. Responsibility of vehicle owner.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-616"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-617", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-617. Grounds for seizure. (a) In general. Except as provided in subsection (b) of this section, any vehicle used for or in connection with disposing waste or other material in violation of this subtitle is subject to seizure and forfeiture. (b) Unauthorized possession or use of vehicle. A vehicle may not be forfeited under this subtitle solely because of an act or omission by a person other than the vehicle's owner committed or omitted: (1) while the vehicle was unlawfully in that other person's possession; or (2) while the vehicle was lawfully in that other person's possession, but the owner can establish by a preponderance of the evidence that the violation of this subtitle was committed without the owner's actual knowledge. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-617", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-617", "title": "\u00a7 7-617. Grounds for seizure.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-617"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-618", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-618. Seizure by police. (a) When warrant not needed. A police officer need not have a warrant to seize a vehicle if: (1) the police officer has probable cause to believe the vehicle has been used in violation of this subtitle; and (2) a warrant is not constitutionally required under the circumstances. (b) Removal of vehicle. Whenever a police officer seizes a vehicle under this subtitle, the police officer may cause it to be moved to a place designated by the Police Commissioner. (c) Vehicle not repleviable. A vehicle seized under this subtitle is not repleviable and remains in the custody of the Police Department, subject only to the orders and decrees of the court or official with jurisdiction over it. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-618", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-618", "title": "\u00a7 7-618. Seizure by police.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-618"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-619", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-619. Referral to Solicitor. (a) Police to refer case. Promptly after seizure, the Police Department must notify the City Solicitor in writing of the facts and circumstances supporting the seizure. (b) Solicitor's review. (1) On receiving the report, the Solicitor must conduct an independent review of the facts and circumstances surrounding the seizure. (2) If the Solicitor finds sufficient evidence that the vehicle was used in violation of this subtitle, the Solicitor must notify the vehicle's owner, by registered or certified mail, of the seizure and of the City's intent to institute forfeiture proceedings. (3) If, on the other hand, the Solicitor finds that there is insufficient evidence to prove violation, the Solicitor must surrender the vehicle to the owner on the owner's request. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-619", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-619", "title": "\u00a7 7-619. Referral to Solicitor.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-619"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-620", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-620. Forfeiture petition; notice. (a) Filing; copies to parties in interest. If the Solicitor determines that the vehicle should be forfeited, the Solicitor must, within 90 days after the seizure of the vehicle: (1) petition the Circuit Court for Baltimore City in the name of the City against the vehicle, as designated by make, model, year, and motor or serial number; and (2) at the same time, send by registered or certified mail and by first class mail to the owner and any known secured party: (i) copies of the petition; and (ii) a notice of the time and place for a response to be filed. (b) Contents. The petition for forfeiture must contain: (1) the name of the registered owners of the vehicle; (2) the name of any secured party; (3) a statement of the facts and circumstances surrounding the seizure of the vehicle; (4) a statement of the specific grounds for forfeiture; and (5) a request that the vehicle be forfeited to the City. (c) Publication of notice. (1) Within 7 days of the petition's filing, the Solicitor must publish notice of the seizure and forfeiture proceeding in 1 or more newspapers published in the City. (2) The notice must: (i) state the substance and object of the forfeiture petition; and (ii) state that any person claiming an interest in the seized vehicle must file a defense to the petition within 15 days of the date of the notice. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-620", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-620", "title": "\u00a7 7-620. Forfeiture petition; notice.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-620"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-621", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-621. Answer to petition. (a) When to be made. Any defense to the petition must be filed within 15 days after publication of the notice. (b) How to be made. (1) All defenses to a petition for forfeiture must be made by answer. (2) The answer must: (i) comply with the Maryland Rules of Procedure as to form and contents; (ii) be divided into numbered paragraphs, each containing a separate and distinctive averment; and (iii) respond to each material allegation contained in the petition, specifically admitting, denying, or explaining the facts alleged, unless the respondent is without knowledge or an admission or explanation would tend to incriminate the respondent, in either of which events the respondent must so state and that statement will operate as a denial. (c) Effect. (1) Every allegation in the petition that is not denied in the answer is considered admitted, except as to persons unknown. (2) New or affirmative matter alleged in the answer is considered denied or avoided by the petitioner without the need of any replication, unless the court orders otherwise. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-621", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-621", "title": "\u00a7 7-621. Answer to petition.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-621"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-622", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-622. Early retrieval by owner. (a) Appraisal of vehicle. (1) If the owner of the seized vehicle wants to repossess it before the petition is decided, the Clerk of the Circuit Court must have an appraisal made. (2) The appraisal must be in writing, under oath, to the Clerk of the Circuit Court. (b) Owner to submit bond. (1) When the appraisal is filed, the owner may post a bond, payable to the City, in an amount equal to: (i) all court costs that may accrue; plus (ii) the greater of: (A) the appraised value of the vehicle; (B) the aggregate amount of the liens on the vehicle, as shown in the records required by law for the notice or perfection of liens; or (C) $500. (2) The bond must have security approved by the Clerk and be conditioned for performance on the final judgment of the Circuit Court. If a petition for forfeiture has been filed, the bond must be filed in the Circuit Court for Baltimore City. (c) Judgment on bond. If the court directs that the vehicle be forfeited, judgment may be entered against the obligors on the bond, without further or other proceeding, to be discharged by the payment of the appraised value of the vehicle and court costs, on which judgment execution may be issued. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-622", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-622", "title": "\u00a7 7-622. Early retrieval by owner.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-622"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-623", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-623. Order of forfeiture. (a) In general. (1) If the court determines that the vehicle should be forfeited, the court must order the vehicle forfeited to the City. (2) If, however, the court determines that the vehicle is subject to a bona fide recorded security interest created without the knowledge that the vehicle was being or was to be used in violation of this subtitle, the court must order that the vehicle be released within 5 days to the secured party of record. (b) Disposition of vehicle subject to security interest. (1) The secured party must sell the vehicle in a commercially reasonable manner. (2) Any sale ordered under this section must be made for cash and must vest in the purchaser a clear and absolute title to the vehicle. (3) The proceeds of the sale must be applied as follows: (i) to the court costs of the forfeiture proceedings; (ii) to the balance due to the secured party, including all reasonable costs incident to the sale; (iii) to payment of all other expenses of the proceedings for forfeiture, including expenses of seizure, maintenance, or custody; and (iv) to the general funds of the City. (c) Disposition of forfeited vehicle. Any vehicle that has been ordered forfeited to the City may be: (1) sold as provided in City Code Article 31 , Subtitle 31, Part 5; or (2) destroyed. (d) Proceeds of bond. The proceeds of any bond posted to regain possession of the vehicle must be applied as provided for proceeds of sale under subsection (b)(3) of this section. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-623", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-623", "title": "\u00a7 7-623. Order of forfeiture.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-623"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-624", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-624. Order of release. If, after a full hearing, the court determines that the vehicle should not be forfeited, the court must order the vehicle released. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-624", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-624", "title": "\u00a7 7-624. Order of release.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-624"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-625to7-626", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-625 to 7-626. {Reserved}", "num": "7-625 to 7-626", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|III|7-625to7-626", "title": "7-625 to 7-626. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-625to7-626"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6/IV", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part IV Enforcement; Penalties \u00a7 7-627. Grants for reports of violations. \u00a7 7-628. Liability for costs and expenses. 7-629 to 7-630. {Reserved} \u00a7 7-631. Enforcement by Environmental or Civil Citation. \u00a7 7-632. Criminal penalties.", "num": "IV", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|IV", "title": "Part IV Enforcement; Penalties", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/6/IV"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-627", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-627. Grants for reports of violations. (a) In general. The City Housing Commissioner must establish a grants program for rewarding community and neighborhood associations that have registered for this purpose with the Housing Commissioner and have been designated by citizens reporting illegal disposal or other violations of this subtitle. (b) Grant awards. (1) Funds for these grants are as provided in the Ordinance of Estimates. (2) The amount of the grants, the criteria for allocating and awarding them, and the purposes for which they may be used are as set forth in the rules and regulations adopted by the Commissioner under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article . (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 09-202.) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "7-627", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|IV|7-627", "title": "\u00a7 7-627. Grants for reports of violations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-627"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-628", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-628. Liability for costs and expenses. Any person who, in violation of \u00a7 7-608 {\"Dumping on public property\"} of this subtitle or in violation of any other provision of law, dumps or otherwise disposes of matter in or on property owned, leased, or controlled by the City is liable to the City for the costs of: (1) removing the matter dumped or disposed of; and (2) repairing any damage caused by the dumping or disposal. (Ord. 12-065; Ord. 11-478.)", "num": "7-628", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|IV|7-628", "title": "\u00a7 7-628. Liability for costs and expenses.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-628"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-629to7-630", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-629 to 7-630. {Reserved}", "num": "7-629 to 7-630", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|IV|7-629to7-630", "title": "7-629 to 7-630. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-629to7-630"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-631", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-631. Enforcement by Environmental or Civil Citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 09-202; Ord. 08-044; Ord. 03-595; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "7-631", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|IV|7-631", "title": "\u00a7 7-631. Enforcement by Environmental or Civil Citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-631"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-632", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-632. Criminal penalties. (a) Basic penalty: $1,000 and 90 days. Except as specified in subsection (b) of this section, any person who violates any provision of this subtitle or who authorizes any employee or agent to violate any provision of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to any one or more of the following for each offense: (1) a fine of not more than $1,000; and (2) imprisonment for not more than 90 days. (b) Enhanced penalty: $1,000 and 12 months. If the violation entails the disposal, in any 24-hour period, of material that weighs 25 or more pounds or material that comprises 10 or more cubic feet, the penalty for a violation of this subtitle is any one or more of the following for each offense: (1) a fine of not more than $1,000; (2) imprisonment for not more than 12 months; and (3) revocation of the privilege of seeking a building permit in the City. (Ord. 11-478; Ord. 09-202; Ord. 08-044; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7136(1st sen.), art. 19, \u00a7176", "num": "7-632", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|6|IV|7-632", "title": "\u00a7 7-632. Criminal penalties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-632"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/7", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 7 Littering \u00a7 7-701. \"Litter\" defined. \u00a7 7-702. Littering prohibited. \u00a7 7-703. Material from vehicle. 7-704. {Reserved} \u00a7 7-705. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 7-706. Penalties: $500", "num": "7", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|7", "title": "Subtitle 7 Littering", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7/7"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-701", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-701. \"Litter\" defined. \"Litter\" means to discard or otherwise dispose of, in any way other than as authorized by \u00a7 7-601 of this title , of small amounts of paper, beverage containers, glass, garbage, or other waste that: (1) weigh less than 1 pound; (2) comprise less than 1 cubic foot; and (3) are not toxic, noxious, or otherwise a threat to the public health or safety. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7254(b), (c)(part)", "num": "7-701", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|7|7-701", "title": "\u00a7 7-701. \"Litter\" defined.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-701"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-702", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-702. Littering prohibited. No person may: (1) litter on any public or private property; or (2) permit the accumulation of litter on any property under that person's control. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7264(c)", "num": "7-702", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|7|7-702", "title": "\u00a7 7-702. Littering prohibited.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-702"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-703", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-703. Material from vehicle. The registered owner of a vehicle is prima facie responsible for any litter disposed of from that vehicle. (Ord. 08-044.)", "num": "7-703", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|7|7-703", "title": "\u00a7 7-703. Material from vehicle.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-703"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-704", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 7-704. {Reserved}", "num": "7-704", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|7|7-704", "title": "7-704. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-704"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-705", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-705. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 03-595; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "7-705", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|7|7-705", "title": "\u00a7 7-705. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-705"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-706", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 7-706. Penalties: $500 (a) In general. Any person who violates this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of $500 for each offense. (b) Each day a separate offense. Each day a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 08-040; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "7-706", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|7|7|7-706", "title": "\u00a7 7-706. Penalties: $500", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/7-706"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Title 8 Air Pollution Subtitle 1 Prohibited Emissions \u00a7\u00a7 8-101 \u2013 8-125 Subtitle 2 Open Burning \u00a7\u00a7 8-201 \u2013 8-204 Subtitle 3 Penalties. \u00a7\u00a7 8-301 \u2013 8-302", "num": "8", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8", "title": "Title 8 Air Pollution", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 1 Prohibited Emissions Part I In General \u00a7\u00a7 8-101 \u2013 8-106 Part II Commercial Solid Waste Incinerators \u00a7\u00a7 8-110 \u2013 8-125", "num": "1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1", "title": "Subtitle 1 Prohibited Emissions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/1/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I In General \u00a7 8-101. Definitions. \u00a7 8-102. Commissioner's powers. 8-103 to 8-104. {Reserved} \u00a7 8-105. Prohibited conduct. \u00a7 8-106. Order to correct conditions. 8-107 to 8-109. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|I", "title": "Part I In General", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/1/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-101", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-101. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Air pollution. \"Air pollution\" means the presence in the outdoor atmosphere of any odor, solid, vapor, liquid, gas, or other substance in such quantities and of such duration that it: (1) can be predicted with reasonable certainty to be injurious to property or to human, plant, or animal life; or (2) unreasonably interferes with the proper enjoyment of the property of others or with the comfort of the public. (c) Emission standard. (1) \"Emission standard\" means a requirement that limits the quantity, quality, rate, or concentration of emissions from a source. (2) \"Emission standard\" includes any requirement that relates to the operation or maintenance of a source to assure continuous emission reduction. (d) Source. \"Source\" means any equipment, process, structure, space, material, or activity that contributes to air pollution. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "8-101", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|I|8-101", "title": "\u00a7 8-101. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-101"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-102", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-102. Commissioner's powers. (a) Surveys, studies, etc. The Commissioner of Health may undertake surveys, investigations, studies, and like activities to determine the condition of the air in the City and how best to improve that condition. (b) Setting emission standards. In the rules and regulations that the Commissioner adopts under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article , the Commissioner may set emission standards and adopt procedures for implementing and enforcing those standards. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a78, 11", "num": "8-102", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|I|8-102", "title": "\u00a7 8-102. Commissioner's powers.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-102"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-103to8-104", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 8-103 to 8-104. {Reserved}", "num": "8-103 to 8-104", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|I|8-103to8-104", "title": "8-103 to 8-104. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-103to8-104"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-105", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-105. Prohibited conduct. No person may operate or use any source, whether indoors or outdoors, static or mobile, that contributes to air pollution in any way or amount that exceeds emission standards: (1) set by federal or state law or regulation; or (2) set by the Commissioner. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a77", "num": "8-105", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|I|8-105", "title": "\u00a7 8-105. Prohibited conduct.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-105"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-106", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-106. Order to correct conditions. Whenever as the result of an investigation or study, the result of a complaint, or otherwise, the Commissioner of Health finds that any source is contributing to air pollution in excess of that permitted by law, the Commissioner may issue a written notice to the owner or operator of that source and order the owner or operator to remove or control the cause of the emission in the manner and within the time specified in the notice. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a710", "num": "8-106", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|I|8-106", "title": "\u00a7 8-106. Order to correct conditions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-106"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-107to8-109", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 8-107 to 8-109. {Reserved}", "num": "8-107 to 8-109", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|I|8-107to8-109", "title": "8-107 to 8-109. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-107to8-109"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/1/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II Commercial Solid Waste Incinerators \u00a7 8-110. Short title; Purpose. \u00a7 8-111. Definitions. \u00a7 8-112. Scope. \u00a7 8-113. Rules and regulations. \u00a7 8-114. Pollutants to be continuously monitored. \u00a7 8-115. Monitoring system to be continuously active. \u00a7 8-116. Emission limits. \u00a7 8-117. City adoption and enforcement of more stringent Federal and State requirements. 8-118 to 8-119. {Reserved} \u00a7 8-120. Required CEMS reports. \u00a7 8-121. Data disclosure. \u00a7 8-122. Inspections. 8-123. {Reserved} \u00a7 8-124. Certification of Air Monitoring Contractor. \u00a7 8-125. Criminal penalties. 8-126. {Repealed by Ord. 22-125} Editor's Notes This Part II was added by Ordinance 19-232 (enacted March 7, 2019). Section 2 of that Ordinance provides that, except as otherwise provided in Section 3, the new law takes effect 18 months after the date of enactment (i.e., on September 7, 2020). Section 3, in turn, provides that \u201cthe Health Commissioner may begin to certify Air Monitoring Contractors in accordance with \u00a7 8-124 ... of this Ordinance on or after the date this Ordinance is enacted.\u201d", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II", "title": "Part II Commercial Solid Waste Incinerators", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/1/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-110", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-110. Short title; Purpose. (a) Short title. This Part II shall be known and may be cited as the \"Baltimore Clean Air Act\" (b) Purpose. The purpose and intent of this Part II is to ensure that accurate and complete information is available to the City and general public about pollutants released from commercial solid waste incinerators within the City and to exercise the authority granted to the City under the Maryland Environment Code. (Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-110", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-110", "title": "\u00a7 8-110. Short title; Purpose.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-110"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-111", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-111. Definitions. (a) In general. The following words and phrases when used within this Part II, unless the context clearly indicates otherwise, shall have the meaning ascribed to them in this section. (b) Air Monitoring Contractor. \" Air Monitoring Contractor\" means an environmental engineer certified by the City to design, install, operate, and maintain the Continuous Emissions Monitoring Systems required by this Part II. (c) Commercial solid waste incinerator; Facility. \"Commercial solid waste incinerator\" or \"facility\" means any facility in Baltimore City that produces energy or disposes of waste by combusting a solid fuel or waste, or gases produced on-site from the gasification or pyrolysis of a solid fuel or waste, and that is capable of processing at least 25 tons of solid fuel or waste per day. (d) Continuous Emissions Monitoring System; CEMS. (1) In general. \"Continuous Emissions Monitoring System\" or \"CEMS\" means a pollution monitoring system capable of sampling, conditioning, analyzing, and providing a record of emissions at frequent intervals that meets U.S. Environmental Protection Agency or Maryland Department of the Environment data acquisition and availability requirements. (2) Sampling frequency. Except as specified in paragraph (3) of this subsection, the sampling frequency capability sufficient to qualify a system as a CEMS for the purposes of this Part II must at a minimum deliver a monitoring sample: (i) once per minute; or (ii) any lesser frequency of interval, up to no less than once per hour, that provides sufficient data for a direct determination of compliance with all applicable emission limitations imposed by this Part II. (3) Dioxin and furan sampling. In the case of dioxins and furans, long-term sampling equipment may be used if real-time monitors are not commercially available, so long as year-round monitoring is still achieved through back-to-back use of long-term monthly samples. (e) {Repealed} (f) Solid fuel; Waste. \" Solid fuel\" or \"waste\" means any solid waste, discarded material, recyclable materials, sludges, by-products, commercial chemical products, municipal waste, hazardous waste, biomass, processed debris, special medical waste, sterilized special medical waste, sewage sludge, scrap tires, auto shredder residue, refuse-derived fuel, processed engineered fuel, or solid fuel produced from municipal waste. (g) TEQDF-WHO98. \"TEQ DF -WHO 98 \" means a unit of measurement for dioxins and furans, standardized to toxic equivalents, calculated in accordance with the World Health Organization's 1998 method. (Ord. 22-125; Ord. 22-124; Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-111", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-111", "title": "\u00a7 8-111. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-111"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-112", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-112. Scope. All commercial solid waste incinerators located within Baltimore City are subject to the requirements of this Part II.", "num": "8-112", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-112", "title": "\u00a7 8-112. Scope.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-112"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-113", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-113. Rules and regulations. In the rules and regulations that the Commissioner adopts under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article , the Health Commissioner may adopt rules and regulations to implement this Part II. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 19-232.) Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "8-113", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-113", "title": "\u00a7 8-113. Rules and regulations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-113"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-114", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-114. Pollutants to be continuously monitored. Each facility must, at its own expense, contract with an Air Monitoring Contractor certified by the Health Commissioner in accordance with \u00a7 8-124 {\"Certification of Air Monitoring Contractor\"} to install, operate, and maintain Continuous Emissions Monitoring Systems (\"CEMS\") equipment to monitor, measure, and disclose the smokestack emission of the following pollutants: (1) Dioxins and Furans, as measured at a point, after all air pollution control devices, where the exhaust gases have cooled to below 200 degrees Centigrade; (2) Carbon Dioxide (CO 2 ) and Carbon Monoxide (CO); (3) Hydrochloric Acid (HCl) and Hydrofluoric Acid (HF); (4) Nitrogen Oxides (NOx); (5) Sulfur Dioxides (SO 2 ); (6) Particulate Matter (PM); (7) Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs); (8) Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons (PAHs); and (9) Arsenic, Cadmium, Chromium (VI), Lead, Manganese, Mercury, Nickel, Selenium, and Zinc. (Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-114", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-114", "title": "\u00a7 8-114. Pollutants to be continuously monitored.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-114"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-115", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-115. Monitoring system to be continuously active. (a) In general. A facility's CEMS must be operational at all times that the facility is operating. (b) Gaps of more than 30 minutes a violation. CEMS downtime that exceeds 30 consecutive minutes while a facility is operating are a violation of this section. (Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-115", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-115", "title": "\u00a7 8-115. Monitoring system to be continuously active.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-115"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-116", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-116. Emission limits. (a) For Mercury and Sulfur Dioxide. Each facility must meet the following pollution limits: (1) Mercury: 15 micrograms per dry standard cubic meter (\u03bcg/dscm) corrected at 7% O2 (2) Sulfur Dioxide (SO2): 18 parts per million dry volume (ppmvd) corrected at 7% O2 (24 hour geometric mean) (b) Limits for Dioxins/Furans and Nitrogen Oxides. Starting January 1, 2022, in addition to the limits imposed by subsection (b) of this section, each facility must meet the following pollution limits: Dioxins/Furans (PCDD/F): 2.6 nanograms TEQDF-WHO98 per dry standard cubic meter (ng/dcsm) corrected at 7% O2 Nitrogen Oxides (NOx): 45 parts per million dry volume (ppmvd) corrected at 7% O2 (24 hour block arithmetic mean) 40 parts per million dry volume (ppmvd) corrected at 7% O2 (12 month rolling average) (Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-116", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-116", "title": "\u00a7 8-116. Emission limits.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-116"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-117", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-117. City adoption and enforcement of more stringent Federal and State requirements. (a) Adoption. If the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency or the State of Maryland adopts a more stringent standard, limit, or requirement for the emission of air contaminants, a more stringent standard of performance for any facility regulated by this Part II, or a more stringent standard of performance for stationary sources that would apply to a facility than is imposed by this Part II, the facility must meet the more stringent requirement. (b) Enforcement. It is expressly the intent of the City in adopting the standards, limits, requirements, and standards of performance referenced in subsection (a) of this section to make those more stringent requirements independently enforceable by the City of Baltimore. (Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-117", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-117", "title": "\u00a7 8-117. City adoption and enforcement of more stringent Federal and State requirements.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-117"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-118to8-119", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 8-118 to 8-119. {Reserved}", "num": "8-118 to 8-119", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-118to8-119", "title": "8-118 to 8-119. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-118to8-119"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-120", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-120. Required CEMS reports. (a) Reports required. (1) Each facility must provide a daily report to its Air Monitoring Contractor that details: (i) the daily emissions from the facility of the pollutants listed in \u00a7 8-114 {\"Pollutants to be continuously monitored\"} of this Part II ; and (ii) the reasons for any CEMS downtime. (2) All data supplied as part of the reports required by this section are property of the City of Baltimore. (b) Form of report. The daily report required by this section must be in the form specified by the Health Commissioner and include all relevant machine readable raw data. (c) Reasonable access required. A facility must provide reasonable access to its property and operations to the Air Monitoring Contractor responsible for preparing the reports required by this section to enable the reports to be prepared and verified. (d) Historical reports. Each facility must provide its Air Monitoring Contractor with all emissions reports for the facility previously provided to the Maryland Department of the Environment, and any prior Air Monitoring Contractor for the facility, at the time that the Air Monitoring Contractor begins monitoring the facility. (Ord. 22-124; Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-120", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-120", "title": "\u00a7 8-120. Required CEMS reports.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-120"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-121", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-121. Data disclosure. (a) In general. (1) The Air Monitoring Contractor must disclose the information it receives in the daily reports required by \u00a7 8-120(a) {\"Required CEMS reports: Reports required\"} of this Part II to the public on a publicly accessible webpage capable of providing an easy to read graphical portrayal of the information. (2) The Air Monitoring Contractor must archive all of the daily reports received from a facility under \u00a7 8-120 {\"Required CEMS reports\"} of this Part II and make this archived historical data, together with all data provided by the facility under \u00a7 8-120(d) {\"Required CEMS reports: Historical reports\"}, available on a publicly accessible webpage capable of providing an easy to read graphical portrayal of the information. (b) Reports to Health Department. The Air Monitoring Contractor for a facility must provide reports to the Health Commissioner, in the form specified by the Commissioner and including all relevant machine readable raw data, about emissions from the facility: (1) whenever the facility exceeds an emission limit set under \u00a7 8-116 {\"Emission limits\"} or \u00a7 8-117 {\"City adoption and enforcement of more stringent Federal and State requirements\"}; (2) at regular intervals set by the Commissioner; and (3) whenever requested by the Commissioner, or the Commissioner's designee. (Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-121", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-121", "title": "\u00a7 8-121. Data disclosure.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-121"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-122", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-122. Inspections. (a) In general. The Air Monitoring Contractor for a facility must periodically inspect the Continuous Emissions Monitoring Systems installed at the facility and verify that they are operating correctly. (b) Times and intervals. Inspections required by this section must take place at times and intervals chosen by the Health Commissioner and will not be announced in advance to the facility. (c) Frequency. No fewer than 4 inspections must be conducted each calendar year. (Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-122", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-122", "title": "\u00a7 8-122. Inspections.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-122"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-123", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 8-123. {Reserved}", "num": "8-123", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-123", "title": "8-123. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-123"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-124", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-124. Certification of Air Monitoring Contractor. (a) Required capabilities. In order to be certified as an Air Monitoring Contractor, an applicant must demonstrate to the Health Commissioner's satisfaction that it, using its own resources or in partnership with 1 or more co-applicants, is capable of: (1) procuring or developing, and thereafter installing, CEMS equipment at a subject facility; (2) performing regular inspections as required by \u00a7 8-122 {\"Inspections\"} of this Part II ; and (3) developing software utilities capable of capturing and publically displaying CEMS data needed for the daily reports required by \u00a7 8-120 {\"Required CEMS reports\"} of this Part II . (b) Conflicts of interests. In order to be certified as an Air Monitoring Contractor, an applicant must not have had a contract, other than a contract to perform the duties of an Air Monitoring Contractor under this Part II, with a facility, or the owner or operator of a facility: (1) within the past 10 years; or (2) for the duration of their role as an Air Monitoring Contractor. (c) Certification. No later than 6 months after the effective date of this Ordinance, the Baltimore City Health Department shall certify an applicant meeting the requirements of subsections (a) and (b) of this section as an Air Monitoring Contractor within 90 days of receiving: (1) information, in the form required by the Health Commissioner, sufficient to demonstrate that the applicant meets the requirements of subsections (a) and (b) of this section; and (2) payment of the application fee set by the Board of Estimates. (Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-124", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-124", "title": "\u00a7 8-124. Certification of Air Monitoring Contractor.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-124"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-125", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-125. Criminal penalties. (a) In general. Any person who violates any provision of this Part II, or of a rule or regulation adopted under this Part II, is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $1,000 or imprisonment for not more than 90 days or both fine and imprisonment for each offense. (b) Multiple simultaneous violations. If a person is responsible for simultaneous violations of more than 1 section of this Part II, simultaneously failing to monitor, measure, and disclose the emission of more than 1 pollutant as required by \u00a7 8-114 {\"Pollutants to be continuously monitored\"} of this Part II , or simultaneously violating more than 1 standard required by \u00a7 8-116 {\"Emission limits\"} of this Part II , each separate violation constitutes a separate offense. (c) Continuing violations. Each day that a violation continues constitutes a separate offense. (Ord. 19-232.)", "num": "8-125", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-125", "title": "\u00a7 8-125. Criminal penalties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-125"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-126", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 8-126. {Repealed by Ord. 22-125} Editor's Notes For the Code-wide standard for severability of provisions, see General Provisions Article , \u00a7 1-214 .", "num": "8-126", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|1|II|8-126", "title": "8-126. {Repealed by Ord. 22-125}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-126"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/2", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 2 Open Burning \u00a7 8-201. Definitions. \u00a7 8-202. Purpose of subtitle. \u00a7 8-203. Open burning prohibited. \u00a7 8-204. Exceptions.", "num": "2", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|2", "title": "Subtitle 2 Open Burning", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/2"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-201", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-201. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Open burning. \"Open burning\" means any fire or smoke-producing process that emits particulates or gases directly into the atmosphere without passing through any air pollution control equipment. (c) Waste. \"Waste\" has the meaning stated in \u00a77-101 of this article . (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a718 (parts)", "num": "8-201", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|2|8-201", "title": "\u00a7 8-201. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-201"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-202", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-202. Purpose of subtitle. (a) Findings and policy. Because open burning can be a major contributor to air pollution in Baltimore City, the City's policy is to eliminate or control pollution from open burning to protect the health, welfare, and property of the people of the City. (b) Commissioner to regulate. For this reason, the Commissioner of Health may regulate open burning. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a717", "num": "8-202", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|2|8-202", "title": "\u00a7 8-202. Purpose of subtitle.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-202"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-203", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-203. Open burning prohibited. Except as specified in \u00a7 8-204 {\"Exceptions\"} of this subtitle , no person may permit or carry on any open burning of waste. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a719", "num": "8-203", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|2|8-203", "title": "\u00a7 8-203. Open burning prohibited.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-203"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-204", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-204. Exceptions. (a) Outdoor cooking. The use of outdoor grills and fireplaces to prepare food is permitted unless prohibited by the Fire Department. (b) Disposal of explosives. The open burning of highly explosive or other dangerous materials for which there is no other known method of disposal or under other unusual circumstances is permitted if, on written request, the burning is: (1) approved by the Commissioner and the Chief of the Fire Department; and (2) done under the supervision of the Fire Department. (c) Training. The open burning of solid, liquid, or gaseous fuels, materials, or buildings is permitted if done for training purposes under the direct control and supervision of Fire Department instructors. (d) Salamanders, etc. Unless prohibited by the Fire Department, the use of salamanders or similar devices by construction or other workers for heat is permitted if: (1) no smoke violation or other nuisance is created; and (2) the salamander or other device is of a type approved by the Chief of the Fire Department. (e) Emergencies. In an emergency declared by the Commissioner, the open burning of household and normal business waste is permitted if it is done in compliance with the conditions and requirements that the Commissioner specifies for the emergency. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a720", "num": "8-204", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|2|8-204", "title": "\u00a7 8-204. Exceptions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-204"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/3", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 3 Penalties. \u00a7 8-301. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 8-302. Criminal penalties: $1,000.", "num": "3", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|3", "title": "Subtitle 3 Penalties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8/3"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-301", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-301. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this title may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this title does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 03-595; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "8-301", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|3|8-301", "title": "\u00a7 8-301. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-301"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-302", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 8-302. Criminal penalties: $1,000. (a) In general. Except as otherwise specified, any person who violates any provision of this title is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense. (b) Each day a separate offense. Each day that a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 19-232; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a710(2nd sen.), 16, 21", "num": "8-302", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|8|3|8-302", "title": "\u00a7 8-302. Criminal penalties: $1,000.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/8-302"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Title 9 Noise Regulation Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 9-101 \u2013 9-104 Subtitle 2 Basic Sound Level Standards \u00a7\u00a7 9-201 \u2013 9-218 Subtitle 3 Entertainment and Commercial Noise \u00a7\u00a7 9-301 \u2013 9-317 Subtitle 4 Amplified Sounds in Market Center \u00a7\u00a7 9-401 \u2013 9-418", "num": "9", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9", "title": "Title 9 Noise Regulation", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/1", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7 9-101. Definitions. \u00a7 9-102. Construction of title. \u00a7 9-103. Exemptions. \u00a7 9-104. Rules and regulations.", "num": "1", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|1", "title": "Subtitle 1 Definitions; General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/1"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-101", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-101. Definitions. (a) In general. In this title, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Impulse sound. \"Impulse sound\" means a short burst of acoustical energy such as that produced by weapons fire, a punch press, or a drop hammer. A pressure time history of a single impulse includes a rapid rise to a maximum peak pressure followed by a somewhat slower decay, both occurring within 1 second. (c) Motor vehicle. \"Motor vehicle\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 11-135 of the State Transportation Article {\"Maryland Vehicle Law\"}. (d) Noise. \"Noise\" means any steady-state or impulse sound that occurs on either a continuous or intermittent basis. (e) Peak pressure. \"Peak pressure\" is the sound level in decibels of an impulse sound measured with sound instrumentation that uses the flat response or linear scale. (f) Steady-state sound. \"Steady-state sound\" means a periodic or random variation, with a duration of more than 1 second, in atmospheric pressure at audible frequencies. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7230 (parts)", "num": "9-101", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|1|9-101", "title": "\u00a7 9-101. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-101"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-102", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-102. Construction of title. (a) Liberal construction. This title is to be liberally construed to effectuate its purposes. (b) Conflict with federal or state law. This title is not to be construed to permit anything that is prohibited by any other federal, state, or local law or regulation. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7230(g)(2)", "num": "9-102", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|1|9-102", "title": "\u00a7 9-102. Construction of title.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-102"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-103", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-103. Exemptions. (a) In general. This title does not apply to any of the following: (1) motor vehicles, aircraft, or other equipment used in an emergency by any government agency or by any public service company, as defined in State Code Article 78; or (2) warning devices necessary for public safety, such as train horns and police, fire, and ambulance sirens. (b) Qualified exemptions. This title does not apply to the following, except as specifically stated in this title or where standards are set by the rules or regulations adopted by the Commissioner under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article : (1) motor vehicles; or (2) the construction, repair, or demolition of a structure or street. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7231 Editor's Notes Text conformed to include express cross-reference to \u00a7 2-106. Cf. Editor\u2019s Note to that section.", "num": "9-103", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|1|9-103", "title": "\u00a7 9-103. Exemptions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-103"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-104", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-104. Rules and regulations. (a) Commissioner may adopt. The rules and regulations that the Commissioner adopts under \u00a7 2-106 {\"Rules and regulations\"} of this article may include provisions that set: (1) standards and procedures for measuring noise; (2) noise standards for motor vehicle operation; (3) noise standards for the construction, repair, or demolition of structures or streets; (4) standards, limitations, and procedures for obtaining a temporary exemption authorized by Subtitle 2 {\"Basic Sound Level Standards\"} or Subtitle 3 {\"Entertainment and Commercial Noise\"} of this title; and (5) other noise standards that the Commissioner considers necessary and appropriate for the protection of the public. (b) Public hearing. (1) Before adopting any rules or regulations under this section, the Commissioner must hold a public hearing at which all interested persons are given an opportunity to testify on any proposed standard and to submit alternative proposals for the Commissioner's consideration. (2) Notice of the hearing must be published in a newspaper of general circulation in Baltimore City, at least once a week for the 2 successive weeks immediately before the hearing. (Text Conformed 03/10/21; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7232(d)", "num": "9-104", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|1|9-104", "title": "\u00a7 9-104. Rules and regulations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-104"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 2 Basic Sound Level Standards Part I Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 9-201 \u2013 9-202 Part II Maximum Sound Levels \u00a7\u00a7 9-205 \u2013 9-209 Part III Enforcement \u00a7\u00a7 9-212 \u2013 9-214 Part IV Penalties \u00a7\u00a7 9-217 \u2013 9-218", "num": "2", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2", "title": "Subtitle 2 Basic Sound Level Standards", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7 9-201. Definitions. \u00a7 9-202. Declaration of findings and policy. 9-203 to 9-204. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|I", "title": "Part I Definitions; General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-201", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-201. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Decibel or dB. (1) \"Decibel\" or \"dB\" means the unit of measurement of relative sound intensity equal to 20 times the logarithm to the base 10 of the ratio of the effective sound pressure to a reference pressure of 20 micronewtons per square meter. (2) In formula, dB = 20 Log 10 P/P 0 where P is the average pressure of the measured sound, and P 0 indicates the reference pressure considered to be the weakest audible pressure a young ear can detect under ideal listening conditions. (c) Person. (1) In general. \"Person\" has the meaning stated in \u00a7 1-107(a) {\"Person: In general\"} of the City Code's General Provisions Article . (2) Qualified inclusion of governmental entities. Notwithstanding \u00a7 1-107(b) {\"Person: Exclusion\"} of the General Provisions Article , in this subtitle \"person\" also includes, except as used in \u00a7 9-218 {\"Penalties\"} of this subtitle , a governmental entity or an instrumentality or unit of a governmental entity. (d) Sound Level A or db(A). \"Sound level A\" or \"dB(A)\" is the sound level in decibels, measured with a sound level meter that uses the A-weighting network or scale, as specified in ANSI S1.4 (\"Specification for Sound Level Meters\"), as amended from time to time. (e) Use. \"Use\" means any activity, occupation, business, or operation that is conducted on land or in or on a street, building, pier, wharf, or other structure. (f) Zone, commercial. \"Zone, commercial\" means any of the following zoning districts established under the Baltimore City Zoning Code: (1) all Commercial Zoning Districts; (2) all OIC Office-Industrial Campus Zoning Districts; (3) all BSC Bio-Science Campus Zoning Districts; (4) all IMU-1 and IMU-2 Industrial Mixed-Use Zoning Districts; and (5) all TOD Transit-Oriented Development Zoning Districts. (g) Zone, manufacturing. \"Zone, manufacturing\" means any of the following zoning districts established under the Baltimore City Zoning Code: (1) all I-1 Light Industrial Zoning Districts; (2) all I-2 General Industrial Zoning Districts; and (3) all MI Maritime Industrial Zoning Districts. (h) Zone, residential. \"Zone, residential\" means any of the following zoning districts established under the Baltimore City Zoning Code: (1) all Residential Zoning Districts; and (2) all Office-Residential Zoning Districts. (Ord. 22-125; Ord. 19-244; Ord. 16-581; Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7230 (parts)", "num": "9-201", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|I|9-201", "title": "\u00a7 9-201. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-201"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-202", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-202. Declaration of findings and policy. (a) Findings. The Mayor and City Council finds that excessive or unnecessary noise in the City is a menace to the welfare and prosperity of the people of the City. (b) Policy. It is the public policy of the City that: (1) everyone is entitled to an ambient noise level that is not detrimental to life, health, or enjoyment of property; (2) the ambient noise level in the City should be controlled and reduced: (i) to promote the public health, safety, and welfare and the peace and quiet of the City's inhabitants; and (ii) to facilitate the enjoyment of the City's natural attractions; and (3) to those ends, standards for noise must be set and enforced, as provided in this subtitle. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7229(1st, 2nd pars.)", "num": "9-202", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|I|9-202", "title": "\u00a7 9-202. Declaration of findings and policy.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-202"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-203to9-204", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 9-203 to 9-204. {Reserved}", "num": "9-203 to 9-204", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|I|9-203to9-204", "title": "9-203 to 9-204. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-203to9-204"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II Maximum Sound Levels \u00a7 9-205. In general. \u00a7 9-206. Limits. \u00a7 9-207. Nighttime sound reductions. \u00a7 9-208. Permitted deviations. \u00a7 9-209. Temporary exemptions. 9-210 to 9-211. {Reserved}", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|II", "title": "Part II Maximum Sound Levels", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-205", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-205. In general. (a) Prohibited conduct. (1) No person may cause or permit a sound level that exceeds the applicable level specified in this subtitle. (2) No person may cause or permit a sound level from the construction, repair, or demolition of a structure or street that exceeds any applicable level set by a rule or regulation of Commissioner, except in accordance with a temporary exemption permit or as necessary to do emergency work. (3) No person may operate a motor vehicle so as to exceed any applicable sound level set by a rule or regulation of the Commissioner. (4) No person may use a vehicle horn except: (i) as reasonably necessary to assure safe operation; or (ii) as an emergency warning signal. (b) Measurements. For sound levels set in this subtitle, measurements must be made with instruments calibrated by means of accepted acoustical techniques to an accuracy of plus or minus 1 dB(A). (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7235(a), (e), 238(a). (b), (e)", "num": "9-205", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|II|9-205", "title": "\u00a7 9-205. In general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-205"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-206", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-206. Limits. (a) In general. (1) Except as otherwise specified in this subtitle, the maximum permissible sound levels are as set in this section for the applicable zone. (2) Where the property line of a use coincides with a zone boundary, the level specified in this section for the zone boundary controls. (3) Where the use is on a public street, the \"property line\" referred to in this section is the boundary of the public right-of-way. (b) Manufacturing zones. If the sound is from a use in a manufacturing zone, the maximum permissible sound level is: (1) 75 dB(A) at any point on the property line of the use; (2) 70 dB(A) at any point on a boundary that separates the manufacturing zone from a commercial zone; and (3) 70 dB(A) at any point on a boundary that separates the manufacturing zone from a residential zone. (c) Commercial zones. If the sound is from a use in a commercial zone, the maximum permissible sound level is: (1) 61 dB(A) at any point on the property line of the use; (2) 64 dB(A) at any point on a boundary that separates the commercial zone from a manufacturing zone; and (3) 58 dB(A) at any point on a boundary that separates the commercial zone from a residential zone. (d) Residential zone. If the sound is from a use in a residential zone, the maximum permissible sound level is: (1) 55 dB(A) at any point on the property line of the use; (2) 61 dB(A) at any point on a boundary that separates the residential zone from a manufacturing zone; and (3) 58 dB(A) at any point on a boundary that separates the residential zone from a commercial zone. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7235(b), (c)", "num": "9-206", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|II|9-206", "title": "\u00a7 9-206. Limits.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-206"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-207", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-207. Nighttime sound reductions. Between the hours of 9 p.m. and 7 a.m., the maximum permissible sound specified in \u00a7 9-206 {\"Limits\"} of this subtitle must be reduced by 5 dB(A) for any use that: (1) borders on a residential zone; or (2) except for the level permitted by \u00a7 9-206(d)(2) of this subtitle , is in a residential zone. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7236(a)", "num": "9-207", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|II|9-207", "title": "\u00a7 9-207. Nighttime sound reductions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-207"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-208", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-208. Permitted deviations. (a) Short, durational deviations. The maximum permissible sound levels specified in \u00a7 9-206 {\"Limits\"} of this subtitle may be exceeded by no more than: (1) 5 dB(A) for a total of not more than 12 minutes in any 1-hour period; (2) 10 dB(A) for a total of not more than 3 minutes in any 1-hour period; or (3) 15 dB(A) for a total of not more than 30 seconds in any 1-hour period. (b) Home activities \u2014 vehicle repairs; power tools. Noncommercial vehicular repairs and the use of home workshops, power tools, and power garden equipment are allowed: (1) between the hours of 7 a.m. and 9 p.m. on weekdays; and (2) between the hours of 10 a.m. and 10 p.m. on weekends and legal holidays. (c) Home activities \u2014 air conditioners; heat pumps. Air conditioning and heat pump equipment used to cool or heat housing on residential property may exceed the maximum sound levels specified in \u00a7 9-206 {\"Limits\"} of this subtitle as long as the sound level does not exceed: (1) for air conditioning equipment, 70 dB(A) at any point on the property line of any other residential property; and (2) for heat pump equipment, 75 dB(A) at any point on the property line of any other residential property. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7236(b), (d)", "num": "9-208", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|II|9-208", "title": "\u00a7 9-208. Permitted deviations.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-208"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-209", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-209. Temporary exemptions. (a) In general. When in the public interest, the Commissioner of Health may grant a temporary exemption from the maximum permissible sound levels specified in this subtitle. (b) Application and fee. (1) An application for a temporary exemption must be made in the form that the Commissioner requires. (2) In addition to any other information that the Commissioner requires, the application must contain: (i) the name of the applicant; (ii) the location of the property for which the exemption is requested; (iii) the dates and hours for which the exemption is requested; (iv) the equipment, instruments, or devices that will be producing the sound; (v) the name of the person in charge of operating the equipment, instruments, or devices; and (vi) a statement of the reason the exemption is needed or appropriate. (c) Form and contents of exemption. A temporary exemption must: (1) be in writing; (2) be signed by the Commissioner; and (3) set forth: (i) the name of the person granted the exemption; (ii) the location of the property for which the exemption is granted; (iii) the dates and hours for which the exemption is effective; (iv) the equipment, instruments, or devices to which the exemption is limited; and (v) any conditions or requirements that the Commissioner specifies. (d) Exemption limitations. A temporary exemption: (1) may be granted only for a period that is reasonable in view of all the facts, but in no case for more than 30 days; (2) may not be granted more than 3 times in any 1 calendar year for the same location; and (3) may not permit its holder to exceed the maximum permissible sound levels specified in this subtitle by more than 25 dB(A). (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7239", "num": "9-209", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|II|9-209", "title": "\u00a7 9-209. Temporary exemptions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-209"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-210to9-211", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 9-210 to 9-211. {Reserved}", "num": "9-210 to 9-211", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|II|9-210to9-211", "title": "9-210 to 9-211. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-210to9-211"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2/III", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part III Enforcement \u00a7 9-212. Notice of violation. \u00a7 9-213. Enforcement by Commissioner. \u00a7 9-214. Private actions; civil damages. 9-215 to 9-216. {Reserved}", "num": "III", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|III", "title": "Part III Enforcement", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2/III"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-212", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-212. Notice of violation. (a) In general. If the Commissioner of Health believes that any provision of this subtitle has been violated, the Commissioner may issue a written notice to the alleged violator. (b) Contents of notice and order. The notice must: (i) specify the nature and facts of the violation, including the dB(A) readings noted and the time and place of their detection; and (ii) order corrective action to be taken within the time the Commissioner specifies. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7240(a)(parts)", "num": "9-212", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|III|9-212", "title": "\u00a7 9-212. Notice of violation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-212"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-213", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-213. Enforcement by Commissioner. If corrective action is not taken within the time specified in the notice, the Commissioner may institute injunctive or other legal proceedings in a court of competent jurisdiction (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7240(c), 242(b)", "num": "9-213", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|III|9-213", "title": "\u00a7 9-213. Enforcement by Commissioner.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-213"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-214", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-214. Private actions; civil damages. (a) Private action to enforce. (1) Subject to paragraph (2) of this subsection, any person may bring a civil action against any person who is in violation of a noise control requirement of this subtitle. (2) An action under this subsection may not be brought: (i) before 60 days after the complainant has given notice of the violation to the Commissioner and to the alleged violator; or (ii) while the Commissioner is diligently prosecuting a civil action to require the alleged violator to comply with this subtitle. (b) Action for civil damages. Nothing in this subtitle limits the right of any person to damages or other relief for injury to person or property. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7\u00a7240(d), 242(c)", "num": "9-214", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|III|9-214", "title": "\u00a7 9-214. Private actions; civil damages.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-214"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-215to9-216", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 9-215 to 9-216. {Reserved}", "num": "9-215 to 9-216", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|III|9-215to9-216", "title": "9-215 to 9-216. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-215to9-216"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2/IV", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part IV Penalties \u00a7 9-217. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 9-218. Penalties: $1,000.", "num": "IV", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|IV", "title": "Part IV Penalties", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/2/IV"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-217", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-217. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 03-595; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "9-217", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|IV|9-217", "title": "\u00a7 9-217. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-217"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-218", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-218. Penalties: $1,000. (a) In general. Any person who neglects, refuses, or otherwise fails to comply with a notice issued under this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense. (b) Each day a separate offense. Each day a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7242(a)", "num": "9-218", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|2|IV|9-218", "title": "\u00a7 9-218. Penalties: $1,000.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-218"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 3 Entertainment and Commercial Noise Part I General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 9-301 \u2013 9-303 Part II Entertainment Noise \u00a7\u00a7 9-306 \u2013 9-309 Part III Commercial Noise \u00a7\u00a7 9-312 \u2013 9-313 Part IV Penalties \u00a7\u00a7 9-316 \u2013 9-317", "num": "3", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3", "title": "Subtitle 3 Entertainment and Commercial Noise", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I General Provisions \u00a7 9-301. Declaration of findings and intent. \u00a7 9-302. Scope of subtitle. \u00a7 9-303. Temporary exemptions. 9-304 to 9-305. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|I", "title": "Part I General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-301", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-301. Declaration of findings and intent. (a) Findings. Having received an increasing number of complaints from citizens about noise and having made certain observations of the subject, the Mayor and City Council of Baltimore finds that: (1) excessive noise, when unnecessary for religious, political, civic, commercial, or other constitutionally protected activities or when not generated in the course of other activity needed to carry on daily life, creates a significant threat to the public health, safety, and welfare in an area as densely populated as Baltimore City; (2) the noises most obnoxious to the public health, safety, and welfare and least necessary for any constitutionally protected or otherwise necessary activity are those that are loud, boisterous, raucous, or unseemly or that are generated solely for the purpose of entertainment, without regard to the health, welfare, and safety of others who, because of population density, are unable to avoid being subjected to those noises; and (3) an alarming increase is occurring in the frequency and volume of this sort of noise, particularly from loud parties and from the plethora of portable sound-producing and reproducing devices available today. (b) Intent. The Mayor and City Council intend this subtitle, therefore, to serve as a vehicle for the control and regulation of noises that pose a threat to the health, safety, or welfare of the citizens of Baltimore. One part of this subtitle regulates noises generated solely for entertainment purposes. Another balances the public interest in regulating certain types of noisy commercial advertising to protect the public health, safety, and welfare, on the one hand, against the interests of the commercial establishments employing that advertising, on the other. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7220(parts)", "num": "9-301", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|I|9-301", "title": "\u00a7 9-301. Declaration of findings and intent.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-301"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-302", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-302. Scope of subtitle. (a) Commercial, etc., speech. This subtitle applies to commercial, religious, political, civic, or free speech activities only: (1) where the application is clearly indicated; or (2) where religious, fraternal, civic, political, charitable, or civic organizations provide entertainment, as in the case of \"fund raisers\" or similar activities. (b) Exemptions from subtitle. This subtitle does not apply to: (1) City-sponsored events in public parks; (2) sound equipment used at any professional sports stadium; or (3) any public service company, as defined in State Code Article 78. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7\u00a7220(parts), 221(d)", "num": "9-302", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|I|9-302", "title": "\u00a7 9-302. Scope of subtitle.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-302"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-303", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-303. Temporary exemptions. (a) In general. The Commissioner of Health may issue a permit for a temporary exemption from this subtitle for commercial, political, civic, charitable, or other organizations to conduct activities such as fund raisers, carnivals, bazaars, meetings, and other special events. (b) Application. (1) An application for a temporary exemption must be made in the form that the Commissioner requires. (2) In addition to any other information that the Commissioner requires, the application for must contain: (i) the name of the applicant; (ii) the location of the property for which the exemption is requested; (iii) the dates and the hours for which the exemption is requested; (iv) the equipment, instruments, or devices that will be producing the sound; (v) the name of the person in charge of operating the equipment, instruments, or devices; and (vi) a statement of the reason the exemption is needed or appropriate. (c) Considerations. Before issuing a temporary exemption, the Commissioner must consider the impact of the proposed activity on the surrounding area. (d) Form and contents of exemption. A temporary exemption must: (1) be in writing; (2) be signed by the Commissioner; and (3) set forth: (i) the name of the person granted the exemption; (ii) the location of the property for which the exemption is granted; (iii) the dates and hours for which the exemption is effective; (iv) the equipment, instruments, or devices to which the exemption is limited; and (v) any conditions or requirements that the Commissioner specifies. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7223", "num": "9-303", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|I|9-303", "title": "\u00a7 9-303. Temporary exemptions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-303"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-304to9-305", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 9-304 to 9-305. {Reserved}", "num": "9-304 to 9-305", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|I|9-304to9-305", "title": "9-304 to 9-305. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-304to9-305"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II Entertainment Noise \u00a7 9-306. Prohibited conduct \u2014 in general. \u00a7 9-307. Prohibited conduct \u2014parents or guardians of minors. \u00a7 9-308. Presumptions. \u00a7 9-309. Violations by minors. 9-310 to 9-311. {Reserved}", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|II", "title": "Part II Entertainment Noise", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-306", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-306. Prohibited conduct \u2014 in general. Except as authorized under \u00a7 9-303 {\"Temporary exemptions\"} of this subtitle , no person may play, operate, or permit to be played or operated any radio, musical instrument, phonograph, tape- or compact disc-player, or other device for the production or reproduction of sound if: (1) that sound is used or intended for entertainment; and (2) either: (i) the device is in a building or other structure or in a vehicle, and the sound can be heard more than 50 feet away from the building, structure, or vehicle or, if further, 50 feet from the boundaries of the property surrounding the building or structure; (ii) the device is in or on a public street, building, park, or other public area, in or on a public access area, such as a shopping mall, parking lot, etc., or on any private property and the sound can be heard more than 50 feet from its source; or (iii) the device is being played between 10 p.m. and 8 a.m. in or on a public street, unless the person is participating in a school band or a licensed parade or has otherwise been authorized to play the device. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7238(d), art. 19, \u00a7221(a)", "num": "9-306", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|II|9-306", "title": "\u00a7 9-306. Prohibited conduct \u2014 in general.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-306"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-307", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-307. Prohibited conduct \u2014parents or guardians of minors. A parent or guardian may not knowingly permit a minor for whom the parent or guardian is responsible to violate \u00a7 9-306 {\"Prohibited conduct \u2014 in general\"} of this subtitle . (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7221(c)(3)(i)", "num": "9-307", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|II|9-307", "title": "\u00a7 9-307. Prohibited conduct \u2014parents or guardians of minors.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-307"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-308", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-308. Presumptions. (a) Devices indoors. If the device is located in a building or other structure or in a vehicle, the owner, occupant, resident, manager, operator, or other person in charge of the premises or vehicle, if present, is presumed to be responsible in the absence of evidence to the contrary. (b) Devices outdoors. If the device is outdoors, the person possessing it is presumed to be responsible in the absence of evidence to the contrary. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7221(b)", "num": "9-308", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|II|9-308", "title": "\u00a7 9-308. Presumptions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-308"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-309", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-309. Violations by minors. (a) Order to stop. A police officer who finds any minor violating \u00a7 9-306 {\"Prohibited conduct \u2014 in general\"} of this subtitle must order the minor to stop the violation. (b) Failure to comply. (1) If the minor fails to comply with the order, the police officer may take him or her into custody. (2) After the information necessary to carry out the purposes of this subtitle has been recorded, the minor must be: (i) promptly released to his or her parent or guardian, with written notice to the parent or guardian of the violation; and (ii) referred to the Baltimore City Police Department's court sanctioned pre-intake adjustment program. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7221(c)(1), (2)", "num": "9-309", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|II|9-309", "title": "\u00a7 9-309. Violations by minors.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-309"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-310to9-311", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 9-310 to 9-311. {Reserved}", "num": "9-310 to 9-311", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|II|9-310to9-311", "title": "9-310 to 9-311. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-310to9-311"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3/III", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part III Commercial Noise \u00a7 9-312. Loudspeakers, etc. \u00a7 9-313. Outcrying. 9-314 to 9-315. {Reserved}", "num": "III", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|III", "title": "Part III Commercial Noise", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3/III"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-312", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-312. Loudspeakers, etc. (a) In general. No commercial enterprise may broadcast over a loudspeaker or other device so that the sound can be heard more than 100 feet from the boundaries of the building or property where the device is located or, if the device is in a vehicle, more than 100 feet from that vehicle. (b) Presumption. The owner, manager, operator, or other person in charge of the building, premises, or vehicle from which the sound emanates is presumed to be responsible in the absence of evidence to the contrary. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7222", "num": "9-312", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|III|9-312", "title": "\u00a7 9-312. Loudspeakers, etc.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-312"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-313", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-313. Outcrying. (a) Restricted hours. Except as specified in subsection (b) of this section, no person may sell anything by outcry between 10 p.m. and 8 a.m. (b) Sporting events, etc. This section does not apply to the sale of merchandise, food, or beverages at licensed sporting events, parades, fairs, circuses, and similar, licensed entertainment events. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 11, \u00a7238(c)", "num": "9-313", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|III|9-313", "title": "\u00a7 9-313. Outcrying.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-313"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-314to9-315", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 9-314 to 9-315. {Reserved}", "num": "9-314 to 9-315", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|III|9-314to9-315", "title": "9-314 to 9-315. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-314to9-315"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3/IV", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part IV Penalties \u00a7 9-316. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 9-317. Penalties.", "num": "IV", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|IV", "title": "Part IV Penalties", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/3/IV"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-316", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-316. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 03-595; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "9-316", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|IV|9-316", "title": "\u00a7 9-316. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-316"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-317", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-317. Penalties. (a) In general. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle or of a rule or regulation adopted under this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, subject to the penalties specified in this section. (b) Entertainment noise \u2014 adult offenders: $500 and 30 days. For an adult who violates \u00a7 9-306 {\"Prohibited conduct \u2014 in general\"} of this subtitle , the penalty is a fine of not more $500 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days or both fine and imprisonment for each offense. (c) Entertainment noise \u2014 parent or guardian of minor: $100. For a parent or guardian who violates \u00a7 9-307 {\"Prohibited conduct \u2014parents or guardians of minors\" } of this subtitle within 12 months after receiving written notice of the minor's having violated \u00a7 9-306 {\"Prohibited conduct \u2014 in general\"} of this subtitle , the parent or guardian is subject to a fine of not more than $100 for each offense. (d) Commercial noise \u2014 Outcrying: $1,000. For any violation of \u00a7 9-313 {\"Outcrying\"} of this subtitle , the penalty is a fine of not more than $1,000 for each offense. (e) Commercial noise \u2014 Loudspeakers: $1,000 and 60 days. (1) For any violation of \u00a7 9-312 {\"Loudspeakers, etc.\"} of this subtitle , the penalty is a fine of not more than $1,000 or imprisonment for not more than 60 days or both fine and imprisonment for each offense. (2) Every day that a violation continues is a separate offense. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "9-317", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|3|IV|9-317", "title": "\u00a7 9-317. Penalties.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-317"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Subtitle 4 Amplified Sounds in Market Center Part I Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 9-401 \u2013 9-403 Part II General Regulations \u00a7\u00a7 9-406 \u2013 9-408 Part III Permits \u00a7\u00a7 9-412 \u2013 9-414 Part IV Penalties \u00a7\u00a7 9-417 \u2013 9-418", "num": "4", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4", "title": "Subtitle 4 Amplified Sounds in Market Center", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4/I", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part I Definitions; General Provisions \u00a7 9-401. Definitions. \u00a7 9-402. Declaration of findings and intent. \u00a7 9-403. Scope of subtitle. 9-404 to 9-405. {Reserved}", "num": "I", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|I", "title": "Part I Definitions; General Provisions", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4/I"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-401", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-401. Definitions. (a) In general. In this subtitle, the following terms have the meanings indicated. (b) Commercial speech. \"Commercial speech\" means speech or sound intended to promote any business or other activity carried on for profit. (c) Market Center Urban Renewal Area. \"Market Center Urban Renewal Area\" means the urban renewal area established by the Market Center Urban Renewal Plan. (d) Publicly amplified sound. \"Publicly amplified sound\" means any radio, phonograph, tape- or compact disc-player, loudspeaker, or other device that: (1) electronically produces, reproduces, or amplifies the human voice or other sound; and (2) is located: (i) on public property; or (ii) on private property, but either: (A) in an unenclosed area; or (B) if in an enclosed area, within 9 feet of the nearest outside edge of the enclosed area. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7225 (parts)", "num": "9-401", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|I|9-401", "title": "\u00a7 9-401. Definitions.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-401"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-402", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-402. Declaration of findings and intent. (a) Importance of Market Center. (1) The Mayor and City Council finds that the Market Center Urban Renewal Area serves the City in many important and irreplaceable ways. Most obviously, the area is a heavily used and essential retail and office district, providing job and shopping opportunities for countless thousands of City residents. (2) Equally important, however, is the traditional role that the area serves as a public forum for many diverse kinds of City residents. On any given day, dozens of people come to the district to promote their ideas on a wide variety of topics. (b) Amplified sounds as hindrance. (1) The Mayor and City Council further finds that publicly amplified sounds have hindered the use of the Market Center Urban Renewal Area, both as a shopping, office, and employment center and as a marketplace for the exchange of ideas. (2) While recognizing that, in some instances, the reasonable use of amplification equipment facilitates the exchange of ideas, the Mayor and City Council finds that the indiscriminate use of this equipment in the Market Center has created a condition demonstrably injurious to the health, welfare, and safety of the citizens of Baltimore. (3) Specifically, the Mayor and City Council finds that publicly amplified sound: (i) can be injurious to the health of those exposed to it; (ii) when coming from or near a first floor business, impairs the use of offices above that business; (iii) deters people from coming to the Market Center to shop, work, and exchange ideas; (iv) hinders efforts to attract new businesses to the Market Center; (v) can be hazardous to pedestrians and motorists in the Market Center; and (vi) aggravates an already-existing din that is caused by the architecture, building arrangement, proximity of businesses, and topography of the area and that is at its worst between 11 a.m. to 3 p.m. (c) Intent. Through the enactment of this subtitle, the Mayor and City Council intends: (1) to promote the use of the retail district both as a shopping, office, and employment center and as a marketplace for the exchange of ideas; and (2) to control the negative effects of publicly amplified sounds while allowing reasonable use of electronic amplification equipment. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7224", "num": "9-402", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|I|9-402", "title": "\u00a7 9-402. Declaration of findings and intent.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-402"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-403", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-403. Scope of subtitle. (a) In general. With respect to publicly amplified sounds in the Market Center Urban Renewal Area, this subtitle supersedes all other noise regulations of the City Code. (b) Public service companies excepted. This subtitle does not apply work performed by a public service company, as defined in State Code Article 78. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7\u00a7226, 227", "num": "9-403", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|I|9-403", "title": "\u00a7 9-403. Scope of subtitle.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-403"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-404to9-405", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 9-404 to 9-405. {Reserved}", "num": "9-404 to 9-405", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|I|9-404to9-405", "title": "9-404 to 9-405. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-404to9-405"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4/II", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part II General Regulations \u00a7 9-406. Commercial speech. \u00a7 9-407. Noncommercial speech. \u00a7 9-408. Loud and raucous sounds prohibited. 9-409 to 9-411. {Reserved}", "num": "II", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|II", "title": "Part II General Regulations", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4/II"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-406", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-406. Commercial speech. (a) Prohibited during certain hours. In the Market Center Urban Renewal Area, between 11 a.m. and 3 p.m., Monday through Saturday, no person may use any publicly amplified sound that contains commercial speech. (b) Permit required for other times. At all other times in the Market Center Urban Renewal Area, no person may use any publicly amplified sound that contains commercial speech without a permit to do so from the Commissioner. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7\u00a7228, 229(b)(1st sen.)", "num": "9-406", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|II|9-406", "title": "\u00a7 9-406. Commercial speech.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-406"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-407", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-407. Noncommercial speech. At all times in the Market Center Urban Renewal Area, no person may use any publicly amplified sound not containing commercial speech without a permit to do so from the Commissioner. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7229(a)(1st sen.)", "num": "9-407", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|II|9-407", "title": "\u00a7 9-407. Noncommercial speech.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-407"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-408", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-408. Loud and raucous sounds prohibited. All loud and raucous sound is prohibited in the Market Center Urban Renewal Area. The receipt of a permit is not a defense to a charge of producing or causing any loud and raucous sound. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7229(e)", "num": "9-408", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|II|9-408", "title": "\u00a7 9-408. Loud and raucous sounds prohibited.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-408"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-409to9-411", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 9-409 to 9-411. {Reserved}", "num": "9-409 to 9-411", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|II|9-409to9-411", "title": "9-409 to 9-411. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-409to9-411"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4/III", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part III Permits \u00a7 9-412. Applications. \u00a7 9-413. Review and issuance. \u00a7 9-414. Judicial review of denied application. 9-415 to 9-416. {Reserved}", "num": "III", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|III", "title": "Part III Permits", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4/III"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-412", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-412. Applications. (a) Form. An application for a permit must be made in the form that the Commissioner requires. (b) Contents. In addition to any other information that the Commissioner requires, the application must contain: (1) the name of the applicant; (2) the location of the property for which the permit is requested; (3) the dates and hours for which the exemption is requested; (4) the equipment that will be used to amplify sound; and (5) a statement as to whether the sound will contain commercial speech. (Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "9-412", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|III|9-412", "title": "\u00a7 9-412. Applications.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-412"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-413", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-413. Review and issuance. (a) In general. The Commissioner must: (1) review each application for a permit within 3 days of its submission; and (2) on a reasonable showing that the amplifying equipment will not be used to produce loud and raucous sounds, promptly issue the permit to the applicant. (b) Speech content not to be considered. Except as needed to verify the noncommercial nature of any proposed speech, the Commissioner may not inquire into the content of the speech sought to be amplified. (c) Fees. The Commissioner may not charge a fee for the permit. (d) Term. Each permit: (1) is valid for 60 days; and (2) may be renewed an indefinite number of times. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7\u00a7 229(a)(2nd sen.), (b)(2nd sen.), (c), (d), 230(a)(1st sen.)", "num": "9-413", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|III|9-413", "title": "\u00a7 9-413. Review and issuance.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-413"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-414", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-414. Judicial review of denied application. (a) Commissioner to seek review. Within 3 days after the Commissioner of Health denies any application for a permit, the Commissioner must: (1) apply to the Circuit Court for Baltimore City for a review of the denial; and (2) notify the applicant by first class mail of this action. (b) Absence of Court decision. If the Court does not render a decision within 10 days of the permit denial, the applicant may proceed as if a permit had been granted, subject to: (1) the same restrictions and obligations that apply to permits issued under this subtitle; and (2) immediate termination of all electronic amplification if the Court later affirms the Commissioner's denial. (c) Appeal of decision. If the Court affirms the Commissioner's denial of the permit, the Commissioner must cooperate with any motion for expedited appeal made by the applicant. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7230(a)(2nd, 3rd sens.), (b), (c), (d)", "num": "9-414", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|III|9-414", "title": "\u00a7 9-414. Judicial review of denied application.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-414"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-415to9-416", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code 9-415 to 9-416. {Reserved}", "num": "9-415 to 9-416", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|III|9-415to9-416", "title": "9-415 to 9-416. {Reserved}", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-415to9-416"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4/IV", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Part IV Penalties \u00a7 9-417. Enforcement by citation. \u00a7 9-418. Penalties: $500 and 10 days.", "num": "IV", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|IV", "title": "Part IV Penalties", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9/4/IV"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-417", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-417. Enforcement by citation. (a) In general. In addition to any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement procedure, this subtitle may be enforced by issuance of: (1) an environmental citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 40 {\"Environmental Control Board\"}; or (2) a civil citation under City Code Article 1 , Subtitle 41 {\"Civil Citations\"}. (b) Process not exclusive. The issuance of a citation to enforce this subtitle does not preclude pursuing any other civil or criminal remedy or enforcement action authorized by law. (Ord. 03-595; Ord. 99-548.)", "num": "9-417", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|IV|9-417", "title": "\u00a7 9-417. Enforcement by citation.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-417"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-418", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code \u00a7 9-418. Penalties: $500 and 10 days. Any person who violates any provision of this subtitle is guilty of a misdemeanor and, on conviction, is subject to a fine of not more than $500 or to imprisonment for not more than 10 days or to both fine and imprisonment for each offense. (Ord. 99-548.) Prior Codifications City Code, 1976/83, art. 19, \u00a7231", "num": "9-418", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|9|4|IV|9-418", "title": "\u00a7 9-418. Penalties: $500 and 10 days.", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/9-418"}
{"index": {"_id": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/10", "_type": "page"}}
{"body": "Baltimore City Code Title 10 Animal Control and Protection Subtitle 1 General Provisions \u00a7\u00a7 10-101 \u2013 10-116 Subtitle 2 Licensing \u00a7\u00a7 10-201 \u2013 10-224 Subtitle 3 General Care and Control \u00a7\u00a7 10-301 \u2013 10-314 Subtitle 4 Animal Protection \u00a7\u00a7 10-401 \u2013 10-411 Subtitle 5 Public Nuisance Animals \u00a7\u00a7 10-501 \u2013 10-504 Subtitle 6 Wild Animals, etc.; Prohibited Animals \u00a7\u00a7 10-601 \u2013 10-603 Subtitle 7 Attack Dogs; Dangerous and Vicious Animals \u00a7\u00a7 10-701 \u2013 10-706 Subtitle 8 Impoundment \u00a7\u00a7 10-801 \u2013 10-808 Subtitle 9 Horse Riding and Driving \u00a7\u00a7 10-901 \u2013 10-924 Subtitle 10 Hearings and Judicial Review \u00a7\u00a7 10-1001 \u2013 10-1010 Subtitle 11 Penalties \u00a7\u00a7 10-1101 \u2013 10-1102", "num": "10", "path": "library|City of Baltimore, Md., Code|health|10", "title": "Title 10 Animal Control and Protection", "url": "/us/md/cities/baltimore/code/health/10"}
